What is she supposed to do, the ČSSD? The Czech Social Democrats in Prague know neither Voldemort nor the "confectionery".
The new councillors of the capital from the ranks of the ČSSD will obviously have to overcome certain language barriers in order to be able to understand their old-new colleagues from the ODS in the Prague Council and in the city representation.
The newspaper Aktuálně.cz has "knocked off" members of the new council from the ČSSD a bit, as they dominate the notorious slang, which has naturalized itself in recent years in which today's coalition partners in Prague were at the helm.
The encoded vocabulary, introduced by Prague's political elite in the past era of Lord Mayor Pavel Bém, is borrowed from some of the best-known actors, situations and causae.
Surprisingly, it became apparent that the new councils were somewhat in the dark with regard to these new terms.
At least that's what they say.
"Who's Voldemort?"
"I don't know."
"I'm more of a newcomer in Prague politics," Lukáš Kaucký, the new councillor for culture, responded to the test of "godfather vocabulary."
But councillor Karel Březina - a political veteran - also reacted in a similar way.
For example, on the term "golf hole."
“That’s the only expression of those you’re presenting to me here that I use every now and then.”
“Otherwise I don’t think much about slang.”
From the vocabulary published by the Tageblatt Aktuálně.cz in this year's June before the ODS Electoral Congress, the editors picked out six terms, especially those that are notoriously associated with the Prague magistrate.
The Social Democrats should define the terms "Voldemort," "largest Prague golf hole," "go into pastry," "bullar naking," "betrayal of the capital," and "flailing."
Three Prague councillors agreed to the test, the fourth - Antonín Weinert, the future head of Prague's school system - first promised his participation in the conversation, but then did not even accept the listener.
The fifth councillor, Petr Dolínek, declined the test from the outset.
"I don't care about these things, and I don't judge to each other how the middle-class Democrats deal with each other."
"It's important to me how we understand each other as part of our programs," he countered.
As already indicated, Lukáš Kaucký allegedly did not realize in the above-mentioned headline that the influential entrepreneur and friend of the ex-mayor Bém, Roman Janoušek.
Kaucky says nothing at all and the only term he correctly guessed during the test was "the greatest Prague golf hole".
"That's the Blanka tunnel," says Kaucký, but added: "But I'd rather know that from the newspaper."
Jokingly: "I'll probably have to take a language course."
The "golf hole" is then correctly deciphered not only by Matador Karel Březina, but also by the councillor for environmental issues Lukáš Plachý as a tunnel Blanka.
However, both Social Democrats also know who the term Voldemort stands for and Lukáš Plachý even suspects where this figure can be found and thus what it means to "go into pastry".
"I believe, that means to go to some Prague catering business," Plachý is quite close to the puzzle solution when you know that it is the seat of Janoušek's companies.
In agreement with Březina, however, he asserts that he had drawn this knowledge out of the newspaper rather than this vocabulary.
Judging by the test participants, there actually seems to be no similarly naturalized slang among the Social Democrats.
"I've also asked my colleagues, but obviously doesn't really there's anything like that with us.
"We call ourselves by name, every now and then we also use abbreviations or nicknames," says the chairman of Prague's ČSSD Petr Hulínský - called "Hulda" by his party comrades.
"At school I was called Bříza (birch), but I don't know if my colleagues also call me that," Karel Březina reveals his nickname.
Deník Aktu�álně.cz has tried the name and behold - it works.
Councillor Weinert is for the party comrades the "grandpa" or less friendly the "old", the representative Miroslav Poche, who drew attention to himself before the elections by machinations in party sponsorship, is called by some colleagues "Igráček" (well-known Czech Lego figure).
Nicknames are thus the only one that the Social Democrats allow in intra-partisan language.
Hulínský supposedly doesn't like it to be called "Hulda," but Deník Aktuálně.cz still registered another nickname of the Prague chairman.
His nickname "Doctor of rubber stick rights" allegedly has to do with Petr Hulínský earning his title Doctor juris at the Bratislaver Police Academy.
The government wants to stop the torture of "witches" and issued a brochure
Women who are stampeded as witches in Burkina Faso usually have some common societal characteristics.
They are more likely to belong to the older generation, they can't find a job - for health reasons, they're single and don't have children.
When one of these women "becomes" a witch, she becomes strictly isolated so that she can't become dangerous to the rest of the village.
“Men are rarely affected by this fate.”
"Even young and healthy women are spared this, as they help at work and give birth to children," a religious sister describes the situation in the capital of the West African state of Burkina Faso in a special home for more than one hundred women who have been ejected from their communities.
The local government now decided to take action against this abnormal "social measure."
And for this reason issued a brochure intended to prevent the "witch persecution".
But the matter has a catch.
"More than 70 percent of all adults in Burkina Faso can't read," the "derStandard" newspaper pointed to the dilemma of written enlightenment.
However, being accused of witchcraft is also common in other African countries.
In Ghana, Burkina Faso's neighbouring country, 27-year-old Ammy Hemmahová was tortured to death by three women and two men - they doused her with petroleum and simply set her on fire.
Among other things, this incident was reported by the BBC.
The perpetrators claimed, however, that the mathing had only been a coincidence of unfortunate circumstances.
According to her version, oil ignited, which is used for ritual anointing.
And that's after the group attempted to "drive out an evil spirit" from the woman.
The isolation of "witches" support people here without regard to their education.
"Der Standard" quoted a 42-year-old entrepreneur from Burkina Faso: "Eaters of human souls used to be a danger," he said.
While the government in Burkina Faso at least strives to defuse the witch hunt, the Gambian preside over the past year the president himself.
“President Yahya Jammeh sparked a monstrous witch hunt after his aunt’s death, as they allegedly took part in their deaths and would now try to kill the president himself through black magic.”
"To do so, he called on shamans from nearby Guinea who, accompanied by police officers, soldiers and the president's bodial guard indiscriminately kidnapped women and men from Gambian villages and brought them to the president's farm in Kanilai," Amnesty International said in its report.
On the farm, imprisoned people were forced to drink an absud from plants with hallucinogenic effects, which also caused intestinal and kidney problems.
Two of those arrested did not survive staying at the farm.
It was only due to international pressure that the nearly a thousand prisoners were released.
A tenant from Bakala: Although he heats in the oven - still pays higher rent
An older subdued man is just leaving the client center of the company RPG Byty of the "coal baron" Zdeněk Bakala, the largest private owner of rented apartments in the Czech Republic.
Under his arm a folder with three new leases to three apartments: for the own and the apartments of his daughters and his son, who is currently serving a prison sentence.
All three have just been increased the rent within the framework of the so-called "deregulation".
Instead of 3500 kroner monthly rent, the son is supposed to raise 4100 crowns next year for a 2-bedroom apartment with kitchen of the second category.
"And there isn't even running hot water in the apartment and you have to heat in a simple coal stove," he tips angrily with his finger at the son's new lease, for whose signing he has a power of attorney.
"But what - I've signed," he declares half of voice and half-resigned.
The citizens' association BytyOKD.cz, which described the new rental conditions as "incorrect and immoral," advises tenants simply not to sign the new contracts and wait until December 7, when it intends to publish guidance on how to take action against such contracts.
But the man is just waving off.
"One says this, the other says that," he makes a sinister face.
The Ostrava region thus becomes a major "deregulation laboratory".
After New Year's Eve, this deregulation awaits most rental apartments in the Czech Republic, whose tenants so far do not pay market-oriented rent.
Aktuálně.cz therefore decided to visit this "laboratory" personally.
The RPG waiting room in Havířov is one of four in the Ostrava region and already nine o'clock in the morning this Tuesday.
Right next to the door are the bodyguards of a security agency and with her an access device.
"Rent 2011", this is how a special touch panel lights up on the touchscreen.
Briefly tapped and the device spits out a license sheet.
Hardly did we decipher the number "157" and already it beeps on the large display on the ceiling of the waiting room.
It's half a second later it's your turn.
Slightly later, an elderly couple enters the waiting room to ask about the rent, but the rush in the waiting room apparently doesn’t apply to the new rent.
Most people are here to solve the usual problems - repair requests, insurance matters and the like.
From mid-November in the Ostrava region, the envelopes with the new leases flutter into the mailboxes for most tenants of a total of 44,000 apartments of RPG's portfolio.
The RPG Byty proposes a rent increase of about 15 to 38 per cent in the letters.
The special switch for the new rentals in the client center has been open since 18 November.
"The action runs a little over a week, that is, this is just the beginning," the ladies behind the switch answer after a moment's hesitation when the "client" turns out to be a journalist.
Deregulation and Bakala's former miner's apartments are an explosive issue.
Before the elections, Lubomír Zaorálek from the ČSSD from Ostrava had picked up on this issue rather loudly, no wonder that the newspapers are full of them today.
“Twenty years I want a new floor from them.”
"Not even a besch. WC glasses could change them," says the man mentioned at the beginning air.
Until now, according to the employees, pensioners in particular are waiting at the counter to convince themselves whether they will really receive the promised rent reduction, which promises them RPG, if they sign the special offer in time.
Other tenants who sign the new lease by December 15 will also benefit from a half-reduced February rent.
Until now, however, "deregulation switch" in Havířov has been anything but overburdened by demand.
"Of yesterday's 360 clients, only about 40 to 50 of the rent were here because of today's," the counter officials estimate.
RPG offers individual rental agreements both on its websites and wall panels, where it seems necessary.
However, if the rental parties do not agree on the new rent, it will be determined by an independent court.
"This should not be a threat, but should be understood as information about the only possible and completely legitimate legal procedure," explains RPG on its websites.
"No one's going to court here," says one of RPG Byty's employees and a man on the street in front of the clientele's centre at least partially agrees.
"I've got 13,000 (crowns) pension, of which 5,000 remain for me for food, where should I take the money for a court dispute," he says.
Then the employee becomes insecure.
"Let's see."
"Mr. Zaorálek tells them about the media not to sign and so people are still waiting," she admits.
Regulated rent ends in most of the Czech Republic at New Year’s New Year 2011 and, according to RPG Byty, the rent increase is an economic necessity.
"The existing amount of rents is not even sufficient for the maintenance of the residential fund," argues Pavel Klimeš, the director of the housing portfolio of the company RPG Byty and - especially the neglect of the housing fund criticized by the agitated tenants is, in the opinion of the homeowners, the main reason for the rent increase.
While RPG was able to invest four billion in the apartments in the years between 2005 and 2010 out of the rents taken, in the four years from 2011 to 2014 it is said to be 5.2 billion.
On the large-format LCD display on the ceiling opposite the upholstered armchairs in Havířov's waiting room, a few red-glowing information appears in between.
“The rent increases in 2011 by 15% and less affect 31 200 apartments, i.e. more than 90% of RPG’s apartments.”
"Always this means at most CZK 7.50 per square meter per month," can be read on the LCD monitor.
Only 400 apartments from RPG's portfolio increases rent by 38 percent.
"These cases are about raising extremely low rental prices of 19.66 CZK per square square foot," RPG argues.
At 2600 apartments, the rent will remain at the same level next year.
RPG Byty promises that deregulation will not skyrocket rents, but will be gradual within four and eight years.
What's more - the company imposes itself another obligation: All income from the rent increase (see diagram) the company wants to put back into the apartments, especially in heat savings - into new windows and into thermal insulation.
But - primarily these should benefit those people who sign the agreements on the rent increase.
The architects saw one - the office also includes a kindergarten
Avriopoint office building in Brno is the new headquarters of Arch.Design.
But who would be expectinging a mini kindergarten here, with everything that goes with it: colorful walls, creative furniture, a lot of toys and even a mini playground on the outdoor terrace.
This kindergarten has been available to Arch.Design staff since last year.
From the first designs for the new Avriopoint, the design and architectural office Arch.Design, from whose studios a whole series of well-known Brno buildings come, counted the premises for its company mini-kindergarten.
Most of his 90 employees (inside) are baby boom-age and the company is determined by the fact that the projectors return to their workplace as quickly as possible.
Together with the opportunity to work shortened, the kindergarten is another bonus that Arch.Design has to offer to its employees in the concept of their social program.
The civil engineers and architects make it easier to reconcile their demanding profession and children’s care.
The mini kindergarten is an original idea of the employees (inside) of Arch.Design and, with the support of management, they managed to win funding from the European Union and the Ministry of Labour and Social Affairs for their project.
The company mini kindergarten is financed by the surgical programme Human Resources and Employment Policies from the European Social Fund and from the state budget of the Czech Republic.
Thanks to this financing (grants of CZK 2, 8 million CZK), the necessary facilities and equipment could be procured and for a period of 2 years for qualified personnel and for the operation.
After utilisation of the subsidies (2 years), Arch.Design is obligated to continue to operate the Minikindergarten for 3 more years at its own expense.
The preparation of this project took a full year, with the company Economy Rating providing active assistance in the settlement of applications.
The children take care of full-time qualified staff - a nurse and a kindergarten teacher, the kindergarten can visit children without age-related restrictions, although most of them are over three years old.
And since this is about a mini-kindergarten, only 5 children are allowed to be present at the same time, but the day over several children alternate.
The nursery operation is flexibly adapted to the needs of the parents, the children of all employees have the same rights with regard to the use of the kindergarten - without regard to the company hierarchy and their respective position.
Although it is individual education and nursing, the program content in the mini-kindergarten is nevertheless comparable to the supporting program of pre-school education.
The children not only play here, above all they are offered excellent lessons - from basic knowledge to preschool lessons, by the way they also learn English.
The classroom is not only equipped with toys and creative games, but also equipped with a calculator available to the children.
What's more - the presence of young children in the company contributes to a friendly and positive company environment.
The parents can take a look at the children at any time and thus eliminates the stress when picking up the children late from a distant kindergarten.
Parents are therefore using their working hours more productively.
It is no wonder that natural and loyal employer-employee relationships are also emerging here.
Arch.Design thus joined the Czech companies, which offer significant benefits when recruiting new employees, which are family-friendly and strengthen their personnel marketing through such programs.
A bone house still fascinates even after 100 years. Take a look at Gaudi's interiors
The roof looks like the back of a prehistoric lizard, the stairs like the spine of a dinosaur and the balconies are torn fish mouths.
This is the Casa Batlló by Catalan architect Antonio Gaudi in Barcelona.
From the local residents, the house with its organic forms got the nickname "bone house".
Organic shapes, wild colors, the unusual use of stone, tiled splinters and mosaics, the facades reminiscent of sheds and ubiquitous soft lines, all these are typical features of Antonio Gaudi's Art Nouveau.
The façade of the house plays with dreamlike lines, tender colors and textures reminiscent of nature and creatures.
It is only upon closer inspection that one notices that everything is dominated by geometry, rhythm and well-thought-out order.
At night, the whole façade looks even more plastic, more dramatic and the special illumination reinforces the shine of the colors.
The same sweeping lines and intertwined shapes also dominate the interior of the house.
Wave-like-shaped wood copies the window frames, railings, ornamental elements of doors, even peepholes and groins.
The house Batlló in the Eixample district on the bustling shopping street Passeig de Grácia No. 43 was created by the conversion of a house from 1877, which architect Emilio Sala Cortese carried out.
Reconstruction took two years and was completed in 1906.
The investor, the wealthy industrialist Josep Batlló, originally insisted on the demolition of the old and the construction of an entirely new building, but he ultimately agreed with Gaudí's idea for an unusual and also cheaper conversion.
However Antoni Gaudí did not design Casa Batlló.
He collaborated with architect Josep Maria Jujol.
Precisely on its drawing board, the decorations for the representative apartment of the reality owner and the use of mosaics on the façade was his idea.
However, numerous other sculptors and artists also collaborated on the design of the façade.
The joint work on the designs for Casa Batlló was the beginning of a fruitful cooperation between Jujola and Gaudí.
Jujol was also involved in the construction of Gaudí’s Casa Milá house in Park Güell, which also houses other works by Antoni Gaudí.
Shortly after its completion, the house was nicknamed "bone house" by the local inhabitants of its organic forms because of it.
Others, in turn, called it the "yawning house" because of the special window opening mechanism.
It is also said that at the top of the building is depicted the sword of St. George, the patron of Catalonia.
But also other sculptural elements symbolize hidden religious motifs.
Gaudí designed for the house a central heating system with venting ducts and typical fireplaces for the house.
Her 45-degree inclination already seems somewhat bizarre.
In his work, Gaudí consistently ignored all the building regulations at that time and was by no means disturbed by them.
Before the start of construction, he obtained the corresponding building permit from the city, but the information made by him about the planned reconstruction was so vague that two years later the city fathers were so horrified by the result that they demanded a remedy.
Among other things, the councillors also encountered that the building with its pillars even intervened in the public sidewalk.
However, there were no changes, on the contrary - only fifteen days after the district's request in this regard, Josep Batlló submitted an application to approve the rental of the two highest floors in the house - and it was promptly granted.
The homeowner himself moved into the lower floors.
In the gallery on the 2nd floor there was a social salon, on the third floor were the bedrooms and private rooms of the apartment.
The ground floor was leased to a jewelry store.
The entire six-storey building passes through a staircase up to the roof terrace.
Due to the basic elements of the reconstruction, all right angles and straight lines were eliminated and replaced by curved and curved walls.
Gaudí also worked with a modern concept for the mutual connection of spaces.
The social part can be completely connected by the opening of the salon doors and so a larger society could chat comfortably, without having to give up a certain intimacy during more confidential conversations.
Gaudí’s reconstruction also gave Gaudí’s renovation a new façade to a rather unusual roof and two other floors.
Gaudí's play with the light as well as the blue and white glazed tiles, with which the courtyard is clad and the lighthouses, is also impressive.
The playing of these two colors gives all the floors of the house the highest level of light.
In doing so, he simply took advantage of the principle that white reflects the light better than dark colors that, on the contrary, absorb sunlight.
Casa Batlló, which was added to Unesco’s World Heritage list in 2005, can visit anyone today.
On the first floor the most spacious and sumptuous apartment is opened to the public.
The homeowners decided only in the year to this step and this on the occasion of the 150th birthday of the famous creator.
When it was determined that this is a rather profitable business, the visits were maintained, they were also expanded to other common and the attic rooms, including the roof terrace.
However, the price for visiting the architectural monument is not a cardboard handle, it is 16.50 euros, students, pensioners, groups and holders of tourist passes receive a 20 percent discount.
This amount exceeds the entrance fees to other Gaudí buildings in the city.
If you want to get to know the real atmosphere of the house with its dragon turrets, you should try to make friends with one of the residents and visit it at home.
The right mattress and the right bed rust? Women have different desires than men
Not the greatest bed frame, but the quality of the lying surface - so mattress and rust make up the good bed.
However, these must complement each other harmoniously in order to meet the wishes of the specific user.
Many things play a role in the selection - the weight, the height, the state of health and, last but not least, the gender of the sleeper.
Just as important is the interior, in which mattress and rust have to be subordinated.
Typical example?
In a weekend house that doesn't heat regularly, latex mattresses aren't well suited, as they take much longer to "get warm."
In addition, they are significantly heavier than conventional polyurethane mattresses.
And - they should be turned regularly.
Namely, if they do not have enough venting channels in the core and also lie on a solid plate, moisture accumulates: it is therefore not impossible to form mold under it.
Of course, of course, what you are used to.
If you have been abroad for a long time, where very high mattresses with a soft layer are common, it will be difficult to get used to a low mattress with a hard surface.
“Arguably all hockey players who came back from the NHL have complained to me that they can’t sleep properly here anymore.”
“For their worn shoulders, heels and hips, they simply need a soft layer of visco foam (Memory Foam – a shape memory polymer).”
"Among other things, I also recommend these to sugar-sick people who suffer from a disorder of the nervous system, so that they can poorly receive the "information" from the joints and the skin," explains Sylva Hadamcziková, who specializes in upholstered furniture.
That’s why years ago I got many hockey players, but also other customers these mattresses with visco foam (Memory Foam).
"Today this is no longer a problem, because such mattresses are offered, among other things, by the manufacturer Tempur, the Italian company Magniflex or the Czech manufacturer Tropico."
And you don't necessarily have to be a famous hockey player to learn to appreciate the benefits of visco foam.
This "miracle" actually works quite simply.
The foam optimizes its hardness and shape according to the absorbed heat, in other words, it snuggles on the warm body.
Nowhere does it press, at night you don't roll around like after a heavy fight.
You're just sleeping much better.
The viscoelastic foam also provides protection against decubitus (wound fire).
When choosing the mattress size, you should still add the length of the soles of your feet, i.e. about 21 to 34 cm.
Man and woman who are 180 cm in size need such a bed 210 to 220 cm long.
Each of the sleepers should have at least 90 cm lying width available, i.e. 180 cm together.
For single beds, for example for singles, 120 to 140 cm are attached.
For the same of comfort and also for eventual visitors.
Otherwise, the following rule of thumb applies to the ideal bed width: "If you lie on your back, there should still be enough space for the slightly angled arms," advises Eng. Helena Prokopová, the guild master of upholsterers and interior designers, who has been dedicated to the problem of healthy sleep and the ergonomics of furniture for years and also presents this topic at technical schools.
With a double bed, everyone needs their own rust and their own mattress, but pay attention when shopping so that the mattresses are the same height.
Otherwise, there is nothing left but to mount the grates at different heights and this is by no means possible with every bed frame.
Foam mattresses should be at least 14 cm high, mattresses with spring skeleton 15 cm, of which at least three centimeters should be accounted for at least three centimeters on the molding layer and the fine cushion.
"By hand or sitting down, you must not feel the feathers in any case," warns Helena Prokopová.
Each mattress requires a special rust type.
Fully upholstered beds with mattress and underlays such as co-creased siame twins have nothing to look for in the bedroom.
These mattresses cannot be ventilated and you can't wash what's common today.
And since one loses between 0.2 to 0.75 l of sweat in a single night, it is clear that after five to seven years, the mattress is slowly becoming a giant (and clogged sieve).
That’s another reason why professionals reject advertising slogans that promise a lifetime of up to 25 years, even a lifetime warranty for mattresses.
Every thing has its ifs and buts.
And that’s why it’s practical advice from the dealers: For mattresses of 120 or 140 cm width, you should not shy away from the hygiene of the washable, removable covers, but it is still advisable to use a mattress protector, which you can easily peel off and wash.
Even if mattress covers are usually made today, so that you get two halves after opening (immensely important for the washing process and the washing size!), the manipulation with larger mattresses, especially carried out alone, often like the unequal battle between David and Goliath.
Do they have a fixed plate in the bed instead of rust?
Get out of this!
Or at most for a bed for unloved guests and for a single night.
Even so, they should rather provide the plate with ventilation holes (if there aren't aren't in it) and use mattresses with spring skeleton and 15 cm minimum height with so-called pocket spring core and fine padding.
Polyurethane mattresses do not belong on such a substrate.
Latex or sandwich mattresses are suitable for non-adjustable slats with 14 to 18 spring strips (lamellae) (possibly also combinations of latex and coconut), gratings with a larger number of slats, i.e. of 28 or more spring strips, with lumbar support in the lumbar area and the possibility of regulating the slatte hardness.
There are no mattresses with spring skeleton on adjustable slat gratings, as they do not respond to the ergonomic adjustment.
In addition to adjusting the stiffness of the spring strips, special flexible shoulder modules are also offered.
This involves a special fastening of the spring strips, which makes it possible to impress or even angle the spring strips in such a way that they make it possible to sink punctually the shoulder of the sleeper.
Especially for men with broad shoulders an important thing.
There are even mattress manufacturers who have started using "men's mattresses" soft zones for the shoulder area.
It is also thought of the ladies - they are treated to these soft zones in the hip area.
For the most demanding customers, point-eased wing spring systems (Lattoflex) or gratings with point-elastic contact pads with so-called multi-torsion suspension are determined.
The pressure is distributed to several points, which reduces the tension.
But - if you use a bad (too big and full) pillow for the right mattress and proper rust, most of the benefits are lost again.
Here, too, a rule of thumb: the pillow should support the head, its tips should only extend to the shoulders so that the shoulder muscles can relax.
A 50 x 60 cm pillow, as the Scandinavians like, for example, seems ideal.
In the side position, the pillow (together with the shoulder relief zone in the rust or the soft zone in the mattress) should balance the shoulder height.
Our modern mattresses have something in common with the former (hay-filled) straw bags.
Modern manufacturers are increasingly using natural materials or at least their admixtures.
For mattresses, too, the new magic word is "organic."
Instead of petroleum raw materials, natural oils are increasingly used for the production of polyurethane foams, for example castor and soybean oil.
Soy is probably used by all well-known manufacturers.
"Organic means for mattresses, which their core consists of materials of purely natural origin," explains Petr Vašíček from the company Tropico Plus.
However, natural products are also used in the production of mattress covers - one of the first was aloe vera.
However, you can also treat yourself to microcapsules, which spread fresh forest or lavender scent when sleeping.
Here, too, one makes a distinction between man and woman.
While a "cooling" plant extract of birch leaves is used in men so that the mattress upholstery can absorb the heat and cool the body, the covers have "warming" effect for women.
Extracts from red peppers stimulate the blood circulation, so the weaker sex becomes pleasantly warm when sleeping.
And today's favorites?
Mattresses made of cold foam (it has larger pores with destroyed cellular structure and is therefore more breathable than commercially available polyurethane and also much more flexible).
Through the combination of the different stiffness (hardness) of cold foams and visco foaming, multi-zone mattresses are created, which know exactly where the head, hips and heels are located.
For a high-quality mattress, however, you have 7000 crowns and more.
However, health mattresses are usually burdened with lower VAT, since they are considered medical aids.
Gumotex was the first Czech company to receive this advantage.
You can see saving on the mattress and the rust is not so easy.
Every now and then there are special offers from manufacturers and dealers who offer two mattresses for the price of one.
For example, the Magniflex brand offers the Duofreeze mattress quilted Visco Foam for 11,990 CZK.
The second you get for free.
It was the material Tempur that ushered in the era of viscoelastic foams with open cellular structure and high density at an almost cosmic pace.
No wonder, they were designed for NASA, because the astronauts are exposed to enormous overloads at the launches.
Tempur becomes soft with warmth, in a cool environment it solidifies again.
So it adapts perfectly to the human body and optimally distributes body pressure.
Therefore, it is not only ideal for preventing pressure fire, but generally increases sleeping comfort.
Viscoelastic foams are used not only for mattresses, but also for other health products, such as special foot cushions, which are intended to provide relief to the legs and vessels.
Domestic manufacturers, for example, use the Eucatherm C, Twinsaver or similar products.
More and more parents want people monitoring devices to have the kids under scrutiny
The fear for their own children is growing and so more and more parents are considering monitoring them via GPS.
The system, which was originally invented to search for cars, is just as well able to locate people, which would also be helpful in such cases, as recently in the disappearance of nine-year-old Anička Janatková.
The device is currently about the size of a cigarette box, but soon it should not be larger than a wristwatch.
Many people terrified the case of the disappeared Anička, and so they look for ways to better protect their children.
Also for the price of an impairment of their privacy.
"We strive to tell everyone that this is by no means about spying, but about an effective means of reducing the likelihood that it will lead to the tragedy," says Petr Pilin of Macro Weil, one of the suppliers of GPS tracking devices on the Czech market.
“I admit that there can be misuse of such devices in individual cases, for example, if you put it in someone’s pocket without him knowing about it, but we haven’t had such a case so far.”
"Most customers really buy it for safety reasons," Pilin adds.
In his opinion, however, the child should always know what purpose the "box" serves and how to use it in an emergency.
However, child psychologist Dana Bočková points out that if a child doesn't want to wear such a thing, it simply gets rid of it.
“Even very youngle children show great creativity when it comes to losing certain things intentionally if they don’t want to be controlled.”
"Even the mobile phones aroused great expectations among the parents at first, but they can be switched off just as easily as these new monitoring devices," says Ms Bočková.
In their opinion, it is also debatable whether such a facility actually helps in the event of an attack.
“In planned kidnappings, the kidnapper practically always checks whether the child has any electronics with them that it could be located.”
“Today it’s the phone.”
“If such surveillance devices found wider use, they would also look for them, as their sale would certainly be accompanied by massive advertising.”
"In these cases, the criminals of the police are always one step ahead," says the psychologist.
However, it acknowledges that similar devices can be quite helpful in a sudden attack by a paedophile.
Roberta Čech of the company RC Monitoring, which provides locating services for both automobiles and people, can help the existence of such a device even if it is destroyed immediately after the attack.
"The device can be adjusted so that it reports the respective position at certain intervals."
“Even if it were destroyed immediately, at least the last position of the person concerned is available.”
"For more expensive devices, even an inclination meter is integrated, i.e., if you fall down and remain motionless, the device immediately sends a warning, stating the position," claims Mr. Čech.
In his opinion, similar services could also perform intelligent mobile phones with navigation, you only need to install special software.
The problem of the current tracking devices is that this is either about devices in matchbox-size that are similar to trailers or smaller mobile phones.
There’s always a risk of losing it.
According to Mr. Pilin from Macro Weil, devices that look like normal digital clocks are being tested in the present day.
"This has the advantage that the eventual attacker doesn't have to realize at all that it's a monitoring or tracking device.
"Or he doesn't realize this until after a certain amount of time, which gives the police a prejudgment," he adds.
No matter in which direction the development goes - towards bracelets or trailers, the same technology is always used as in vehicle location.
So about the combination of tracking function with the help of the GPS system (just like with the navigation in the car) and the function of a mobile phone that reports the current position.
The army has so many ignition cords that you could wrap the whole of the Czech Republic it could hold you but only two years
The Czech army has bought 445 kilometers of ignition cords, detonated with explosive devices.
In the previous year, the Ministry of Defense had already delivered 224 kilometers of ignition cord to the army camps.
It's already reassurant to know that the armed forces have been clearing for 225 years.
The problem is only the durability of the ignition cords - two, but at most four years.
The army bought the ignition cords from the stock company STV Group, a. s. for läppische 40 million kronor.
In its justification for the purchase of this huge amount of ignition cord, the General Staff MF DNES replied that the soldiers would consume about 60 kilometers of ignition line annually.
According to the research of army pyrotechnicians that MF DNES addressed it, it is clear from this indication that the whole army would have to undergo an extremely intensive pyrotechnic training every year - from all generals and officers of the army command, to the secretaries and cleaning women.
Defense Minister Alexandr Vondra and Army General Vlastimil Picek.
Only in this way could the considerable annual consumption of sixty kilometers of ignition cords be halfway justified.
By the way, this length corresponds to 25,000 detonations.
But even so, these army holdings would last for more than ten years.
If, however, the old rule of experts still applies that two meters of ignition cord is completely sufficient for the training of each new professional solder, then the army has created a reassuring supply for the already mentioned 225 years through these new purchases - also from the point of view of the increased demand of the pioneering and special forces.
Soldiers of the rapid response brigade recently complained to Defence Minister Vondra that they had almost no more cartridges for handguns, so shooting exercises are severely restricted due to the lack of funds.
Not to mention the underpant shortage.
“NEIther does I know why they’re buying such tons of ignition cords from us.”
But that's ultimately their decision, they'll already know what they're needing them about.'
"For us, the army is a good customer, which we would like to meet as dealers to the greatest extent possible," says STV Group sales manager Jan Děcký.
Jan Pejšek, the spokesman for the Ministry of Defence, justifies the purchase of hundreds of kilometers of ignition cord by saying that the army would have stocked itself with this "ammunition" for at least ten years.
Jan Děcký from the STV Group, however, only speaks of a four-year service life of the ignition cords and police and army pyrotechnicians of only two years.
"While perfectly stored, the four years mentioned are probably also possible," admits one of them, who, however, wishes to remain unnamed in order not to be accused of criticism of the army as a soldier.
After expiry of the service life, all ammunition must be thoroughly tested whether it is still reliable and does not endanger the life of the soldiers.
However, these tests aren’t in vain, they cost more money.
Another argument of the Ministry of Defense is the quantity discount for the 445 kilometers ignition cord.
"They cost us sixty percent less than last year's delivery of 244 kilometers," spokesman Pejšek tells us the point of view of the defense ministry's buyers.
Only for comparison: The annual consumption of ignition cords of police pyrotechnicians who liquidate ammunition from the Second World War or the Soviet brother army barely exceeds 1,500 meters for several years.
And the police always order only such quantities of this ammunition, which it can consume before the end of the service life.
Due to the safety of the pyrotechnicians, it also keeps the possibility of flexible purchases of more modern and thus also reliable ignition cords open.
The Ministry of the Ministry of Defense already has huge problems in the liquidation of tens of thousands of tons of ammunition from times of the Warsaw Treaty, when the two-hundread-member Czechoslovak People's Army was preparing for its campaign to the Rhine.
According to the pyrotechnicians who addressed MF DNES, in a few years the Czech armed forces expect the same problem in the liquidation of hundreds of kilometers old ignition cords, which it has bought so "cheaply" today.
To this day, the contacts that I have gained thanks to the competition come to me
Lukáš Pytloun has helped Lukáš Pytloun sale the franchise of his hotel’s victorious placement.
Lukáš Pytloun from Liberec was the winner of the "Small Entrepreneur of the Year competition".
The story of his success seems like a film scenario.
Lukáš Pytloun liked to travel.
Only abroad did he become aware that there is a lack of good overnight accommodation at reasonable prices in Liberec.
From the savings from his holiday work abroad and with the help of a bank loan, he bought a dilapidated house with five apartments.
Considering he was a university student in his sixth semester at the time, this was certainly a courageous undertaking.
"The price-to-quality ratio annoyed me and so I tried to do something about it," says Lukáš Pytloun.
He rented out the first reconstructed apartment to foreign workers.
He immediately reinvesteded all the profit in the house, which he then converted step-by-step to the pension with 54 beds.
Since then, in just seven years of entrepreneurial activity in Liberec, he managed to open four guesthouses, two stylish restaurants and two hotels with a total capacity of more than 300 beds of all accommodation categories.
The most comfortable of them until recently was the four-star hotel "Pytloun Design Hotel".
This year, another first-class hotel was added.
"We have just signed a contract with a hotel in Ostrov u Tisé, to which we have sold our franchise concept and other interested parties in the Pytloun hotel franchise have already contacted us," says entrepreneur number one from last year.
"A large share of this license sale to operate hotels has just been the victory in the "Entrepreneurs of the Year" competition, Mr Pytloun adds appreciatively.
Victory has earned him greater notoriety, even popularity in Liberec and the surrounding area.
"People recognized me on the street, often they even congratulated me afterwards and I have to say that did really well," he recalls.
I really recommend everyone who wants to create something to participate in this competition.
You don’t have to win right away either, every visualization is good.
He says that to this day he draws benefits from the contacts and acquaintances that would not otherwise have occurred at all.
“With some people I’ve met thanks to the competition, I maintain regular business relationships to this day.”
In his opinion, the fame associated with the success is also reflected a little bit on each of its employees.
And there he has at seventy.
“I notice greater loyalty and pride in them that they are part of something that is accorded universal recognition.”
I don’t really see this just as a personal success, no, that’s the result of the good work of all of us.”
"My name has only come to the fore," says Lukáš Pytloun modestly.
I wish my successors a quieter year than we had in Farmet
Victory euphoria was quickly detached by the consequences of the crisis.
Thanks to his determination, Farmet has the worst behind him.
Karel Žďárský, the company owner of Farmet, accepted the prize for the first rank of the Vodafone Company of the Year competition last year.
At the time, the jury paid tribute to the efforts with which he stampeded a company out of the Ground from the small tooling in Česká Skalice, making hundreds of millions of sales.
"I can say that this appreciation has encouraged not only myself, but also all of my employees and boosted our self-confidence in the fight with the competition," says the previous year's winner.
At the beginning of Farmet's company's history was an effort to do something useful.
And then when the state offered his father Karel Žďárský as compensation for the property seized by the communists a small workshop for the lease, he did not hesitate for long, let his career in the company in which he had been employed until then and founded his own company with a few comrades of his father to make plastic tubs.
A short time later, they began to focus on vegetable oil presses.
Within 18 years, they have risen to become the largest manufacturer of automatic press in Central Europe.
But it was only through the success in the aforementioned competition and its medialization that she also entered into the awareness of the non-specialist public.
"Not only did our customers now perceive the Farmet brand much more intensively, our foreign partners also began to the fact of being able to work together with a company that has achieved the greatest possible success in their country," says Karel Žďárský.
However, the past year was not only dedicated to the euphoria at Farmet.
The assessment came from the company’s economic results in 20080 and part of 2009.
And just 2009 was the most difficult year for the sale of tool making products.
The crisis, which at that time shook even less the citizens, but above all companies, just peaked - many companies were forced to discontinue their investment projects and restrict the purchase of new facilities and technologies.
“Of course we couldn’t escape this situation either.”
"Sales declined by 40 percent, we had to restrict production and even resort to redundancies," explains Karel Žďárský
A viable company must sometimes take such unpopular measures.
However, it was possible to get to grips with the situation and to keep the company in the black numbers, even if with lower profits than before the crisis.
For one, the company was unwilling to make smears - on investments in research and development.
"From the subsidy we built a research and training center, we encouraged our presentation at international trade fairs and acquired major prizes at domestic exhibitions," says Žďárský.
Everything testifies that we have overcome the crisis.
Sales are growing again and with them also the number of new employees.
"I wish the new winner a lot of joy in the victory - but in a quieter atmosphere," Karel Žďárský said.
An opinion poll cleaning up with nonsensical regulations
The opinion poll about "absurdity of the year" is part of entrepreneur competition for the fourth time.
Some nonsensical regulations already managed to get out of the world.
The dubious winner of the next vintage of the opinion poll "Absurdity of the Year" increased the obligation to create documents on authorities that are available online.
More than one-third decided by not quite four thousand voting entrepreneurs.
Thus, the social security administration demanded an excerpt from the commercial register, even if the official could have retrieved it through two mouse clicks.
Thanks to the opinion poll, this is now changing.
"In the future, the Czech Social Security Administration will no longer require extracts from the commercial registrie for checks of social security payers," said ČSSZ Jana Buraňová.
The purpose of the opinion poll "Absurdity of the Year" is to examine all regulations and orders that entrepreneurs in the Czech Republic have to comply with and repeal the nonsensical.
For the fourth time this year, it is part of an entrepreneurial competition organized by the tsch. Wirtschaftszeitung (HN) and enjoying growing popularity among the public.
If last year the three-thousand people took part in the vote, there were already four thousand this year.
No wonder, because there are still a lot of legal responsibilities that serve solely to impede corporate activity.
"As regards, for example, the number of income tax forms, we occupy a leading rank in the world," notes Karel Havlíček of the Association of Small and Medium-Sized Enterprises and Traders of the Czech Republic.
Simplifying entrepreneurial activity in recent years has been the proclamated goal of all governments.
Compared to the world, however, the situation in the Czech Republic has hardly improved.
According to data from the World Bank, the Czech Republic ranks 74th in terms of corporate conditions, compared to the previous year, an eight-placer placement.
The Absurdity of the year competition has been proclaimed for the first time in the year and takes place every year according to the same scenario.
Anyone can report administrative and bureaucratic nonsense from April until the end of the competition's editorial deadline at the end of September.
The web address www.firmaroku.cz serves to register. After that, the organizer of the competition selects seven candidates for the "anti-prize" and lets the public vote on the websites www.firmaroku.cz/absurdita and www.ihned.cz.
It wins the absurdity that gets the most votes.
For this year's opinion poll, more than a hundred, more precisely 105 meaningless regulations and ordinances have already been notified.
The tips come not only from entrepreneurs, but also from professional associations and journalists.
Among the nominated "finalists" of the competition are, for example, that maintenance vehicles for highways and expressways have to pay tolls or that entrepreneurs with twenty employees, two offices and two toilets have to prepare every two years.
The directory of this year's "Seven" is published on the pages of the competition.
The proclamation of the competition at the beginning of November on Prague’s Wenceslas Square is part of a happening, in which personalities of political and public life regularly participate.
This year, for example, Eva Svobodová, the director of the association of small or medium-sized enterprises and traders of the Czech Republic and Petr Kužel, the president of the Chamber of Commerce of the Czech Republic, were part of the game at the culmination of the competition.
"Where the state throws sticks between the legs of the entrepreneurs, there can be no real economic upswing, that is why my aim is to pick up at least thirty paragraphs every year, which still unnecessarily complicate their undertakings for entrepreneurs today," said Martin Kocourek, the minister of industry and commerce.
Efforts to eliminate nonsensical arrangements have an effect.
In 2007, the people were most disturbed by the obligation imposed on entrepreneurs to having to report to the trade office at the site of their permanent premises three days in advance the intention of wanting to go to an exhibition or a fair.
This obligation was repealed by virtue of an amendment to the Trade Act.
The obligation, which received the second largest number of votes in 2007 - the obligation to have to have to report business trips from employees up to 30 days - was abolished by the law on "green card".
And - all good things are three:
For the absurdity of 2009, the ordinance was chosen, which imposed on physiotherapists to have a mirror, a refrigerator and two personal scales in the consultation room.
This ordinance was reasonably mitigated by the Ministry of Health this year - the refrigerator duty fell away.
A new category recognizes socially responsible companies
Valuation criteria are humanitarian, environmental, educational or sports projects
A novelty in this year's already fifth year of the competition of the business newspaper (HN) is the category "Responsible company of the year 2010".
The basic idea of this competition is to promote sensible and fair undertakings.
"We would like to support such companies that are aware of the consequences of their entrepreneurial activity on the environment and support socially responsible initiatives," says Patrik Bartholdy, the regional manager of the mobile phone company Vodafon, which is the proclaimer and guarantor of this competition, as well as "Rebell of the Year" competition.
For this year's first vintage of this special subcategory of the main Vodafon Company of the Year's Vodafon Company, for which success is not the only long-term goal.
The story of each of them is extremely inspiring and offers enough material for an independent article.
Although they differ diametrically with regard to the subject of their activities, their efforts nevertheless have a common denominator - support and consideration.
Here, for example, it is about handicapped people, because about personal problems or the health of one’s own employees.
Among the fourteen finalists from all over the Czech Republic, for example, the Prague-based software company Et Netera appeared, which promotes the emergence of non-formal sports teams among their employees, who then participate in numerous tournaments in various sports.
Another example of a responsible company from the Pardubice region is Isolite-Bravo, which has been the main sponsor of a foundation fund for handicapped children for seventeen years.
A paper mill from Poděbrady also takes exemplary care of its health-disabled employees, who account for 70 percent of their workforce, for which she was honored with the title "Responsibility-conscious company of the Central Bohemia region"; in the competition Vodafone company of the year, she took third place within the framework of this region.
In the Olomouc region, the accounting company MiRyz Soft won, among other things, which introduced a flowing and also reduced to an average of 35 hours per week and this without a proportionate wage reduction.
At the title ceremony, the jury also honored the fact that the company mainly buys from Czech manufacturers, supports citizens’ initiatives in their surroundings and works according to environmentally friendly principles.
"We strive to recycle as much as possible in order to minimize the consequences of our entrepreneurial activity and administration for the environment," explains its managing director Miluše Ryznarová.
Responsible IT company Enlogit from the Ústí nad Labem region, in turn, is interested in the health of its employees and why it has acquired ergonomic armchairs and keyboards for all notebooks, it also promotes home work among its employees and in corporate campaigns it uses Cartering services, which are offered by a home-protected workshop.
"In addition, we support the "People in Need" foundation, which provides concrete assistance to the local Roma community," says Ondřej Suchý, the company's representative.
Last but not least, the activity of the cleaning company "Praní a čištění" from the Karlovy Vary region is also worth mentioning, which contributes to their pension insurance in the form of extra bonuses.
"We provide our employees with contributions to catering, the commute, to increasing their qualifications and for the holiday," says Josef Strapáč of the construction company Czasch from Bruntál, the winner in the Moravian-Silesian region.
All district finalists of the Vodafon Company of the Year were automatically registered for the category Responsible Company of the Year, who answered the questions of the questionnaire enclosed with the registration and sent it to the organizer no later than August 15.
Incorrectly or incompletely completed answers in the questionnaire were excluded by the organizer from the evaluation.
"If the representatives of the competing companies left the questions unanswered, they were excluded from the "Responsible Company of the Year" competition, but they were not disqualified in the main competition Vodafone company of the year."
"A separate registration only for this competition is not possible, as it is only a subcategory of the main competition company of the year," explains Karel Goldman of the organizing agency Bison & Rose.
Pilsner Bodenfonds - Arrests of the police in a public tender
In the building of the Bodenfonds in Pilsen, the anti-corruption police made arrests in recent days.
The local media entrepreneur and real estate trader Luboš G. was handcuffed by the detectives.
He had allegedly attempted to manipulate a current public competition for the settlement of restitution claims in such a way that the plots offered in the competition fell to a specific interested party.
Luboš G. is said to have paided half a million.
"At this time, I can only confirm that the police are prosecuting a person for the offence of fraudulent machinations in a public competition," Antonie Zelená, the head of the Pilsner Prosecutor's Office told the Pr�ávo newspaper that she could not give further details with regard to the ongoing investigations.
24 hours later, the 46-year-old entrepreneur was discharged again by police.
In view of the sparse official information, one can only speculate how he could manipulate the public competition.
The spokeswoman for the Lubomír Černá land fund stated on Právo’s question that the police had actually made arrests in the Pilsner extension of the Land Fund and this during a public competition.
"It was about a client and by no means about one of our employees," she clarified.
According to Ms. Černá, public competition had the correct designation "call for the tendering of claims for the transfer of land in accordance with the Land Act" and would take place in cases where several applicants show interest in the offered property.
"These persons offer a higher value of their restitution claims to be settled through the transfer than the declared property value under the proclamated property value."
"According to the entitlements offered, the order of potential acquirers is determined, then the land fund transfers the land to the person with the highest offer," the fund's spokeswoman explains.
Luboš G. was obviously available information about who had registered at the request and since he was himself an authorized person since he had bought a number of restitution claims, he offered that he withdraw from the competition.
Obviously against a fat severance.
This would also be a sufficient explanation for why he had such a large amount of money with him when he was arrested.
Luboš G. has already had too good contacts with the Pilsner Bodenfonds in the past.
He also played a role in the causa of the former head of the land fund Václav Sankota, which was prosecuted because he issued land in replacement restitutions, to which location decisions on construction projects had already been made.
At that time, Luboš G. also acquired land for the construction of public roads from the Land Fund, for example, at the place where the construction of the access magistrale to the Borské pole industrial estate was planned.
Later he exchanged these very cheaply with the magistrate for a house directly in the historical center of the city, which far exceeded the value of the plots.
At that time, the town hall had to give a heavy-heart of little attention in order not to jeopardize the construction project by blocking the plots.
The housing costs of households rise by a few hundred monthly
Households will not only have to expect higher costs for all types of energy from January, but also with higher water and sewage fees and for district heating and in some places, rents are also growing.
From January, families will have to draw several hundred crowns more per month than before.
Electricity will be up to 4.6 percent more expensive, gas by 2 percent and more, in some cities you will have to pay up to a tenth more for water supplies and in some places the district heating supplies will also become more expensive.
Electricity inflation alone costs housing households a few hundred kronor more every year.
The heavy electricity prices have been reduced by all the main suppliers, i.e. the significant promotion of electricity generation from renewable sources, especially from photovoltaic systems.
Gas will only become moderately more expensive, but precisely when people have to heat the most and have the highest consumption.
Here, too, a few hundred crowns more per year are to be expected.
While electricity and gas are becoming more expensive throughout the Czech Republic, the prices of district heating vary from city to city.
Above all, larger places are planning price increases.
For example, households in Pardubice and Hradec Králové will have to pay up to 50 kronor more per month for district heating from January, more than 1000 crowns more per year families in Liberec.
The inhabitants of Prague, in turn, face 9 percent higher water and sewage fees and this in the event that the city increases the infrastructure rent.
If rent doesn't change, it's only 6.8 percent.
Compared to the whole of the Czech Republic, however, the price in Prague continues to be one of the lowest.
In northern Bohemia, where prices are already among the highest, water and sewage fees are expected to rise by 7.4 percent.
Enormous rental price increases can be expected, for example, in 140,000 rental apartments, where the so-called rent regulation is eliminated from January.
For example, the housing company CPI Byty, which owns 2500 apartments in Ústí nad Labem, plans to increase rents by one hundred percent from January.
If you disagree with this rent increase, the company wants to prosecute.
Regulated rents, which are still paid in nearly 300,000 apartments today, are expected to increase by more than 10 percent next year.
Czech children get drunk with Fernet and rum beer is out - it just seems too slow
Fernet, rum and vodka, sometimes a good whisky, which was stolen from the father from the house bar.
These are the beverages, according to the enjoyment of which drunken Czech teenagers under fifteen have to be treated most frequently.
No exception are children, who have to sleep their hangover repeatedly in the hospital.
This is evident from the experience of physicians who are faced with similar cases in the admission of the children's departments of clinics.
“Beer or wine are completely out of fashion because it’s just taking too long to get drunk.”
"Today's trend is clearly hard alcohol," said Právo Luděk Pelikán, the pediatrician's pediatrics department's pediatrocial clinic.
"Whisky, if it succeeds in stealing a bottle from the father's bar, and if not, at least Fernet or vodka.
"Vodka has the advantage that you can mix him with Juice, then he doesn't smell so strong," explains the chef.
The spokeswoman of the faculty hospital Motol Eva Jurinová agrees with him.
“Most often it’s about Fernet or rum.”
“That’s today’s alcohol trend, especially in elementary schools.”
"Occasionally it was also about Slibowitz, which you take with you from home," Ms Jurinová said.
According to chief physician Pelikán, thirteen- or fourteen-year-old drinkers in hospital admissions are by no exception.
In front of the clinic in Hořovice, which he has been working on since this year, he worked for ten years in the pediatrics department of the Motol hospital, where the ambulances delivered drunken students from near and far.
“A girl had to be hospitalized four times.”
"She started her career at the age of 12, when she was first admitted with 3.5 per thousand alcohol in her blood and in combination with "extase,"" the doctor described a particularly serious case.
"She was at some dance party at the exhibition grounds in Holešovice."
"The interesting thing was that it didn't seem strange to her mother that her daughter didn't come home for 48 hours," wonders the chef doctor.
It is precisely the lack of interest of the parents or their incredible tolerance in kick-starting with one’s own offspring one of the main causes of children’s growing alcoholism.
According to experts, it is not about a real dependency in the true sense of the word in children.
“Alcoholism is a long-standing chronic dependence on alcohol, including all the associated phenomena, and this is not the case with these children.”
In those who repeatedly enjoy alcohol, a negative family-social background is usually characteristic," explains chief physician Pelikán.
According to the spokeswoman of Motol Hospital, Ms Jurinová, about forty drunken children were admitted by mid-November to the local department of pediatrics and adolescent medicine.
However, only the cases of severe alcohol poisoning, which required hospitalization, are included in the statistics.
Drunken children who are brought home after outpatient treatment by the parents or, if necessary, by the police will not be registered.
In addition to the cases in which children only drink such a bottle of liquor in the school bench, according to Mr. Pelikán there are three seasonal amplitudes that can be relied on one hundred percent: Walpurgisnacht, New Year's Eve and the end of the school year.
"The latter for two reasons - either out of grief because of the bad testimony or for the joy of the holidays."
"On Walpurgis night, I have already experienced seven drunken children at once in the recording," emphasizes Doctor Pelikán.
A drunken kid who spends the night under medical supervision is paradoxically better off than a child who only struggles with an unpleasant hangover the next day.
The heaches in the morning, which stems from dehydration and lack of glucose, can be expelled in the hospital with a liquid-giving infusion with glucose and nutrients that the organism lacks.
"They're clearly better off, because after the "glorious" experience in the evening, they wake up in a good mood without anything hurting anywhere."
"They're just a little confused about where they are," admits Doctor Pelikán, spoiling the patient's good mood by enumerating them the costs that will cost them treatment.
Because the transport by ambulance and the night in an acute bed come well and gladly on a few tens of thousands of crowns.
"Every now and then I used this as a means of education, when I calculated directly to the children during the visit what cost their drunkenness and that it may well happen that they have to work throughout the holidays to earn the money for a single such party," added Doctor Pelikán.
In his opinion, however, the health insurance companies are not resorting to recourse procedures in order to demand the costs necessary for the healing of the drunken children from their parents.
Two training aircraft L-159 for the first time in foreign hands - in Spain
Two L-159 light warplanes out of a total of five, which the Czech Ministry of Defence has exchanged for the Casa C-295M transport aircraft, flew to the Spanish city of Seville on Tuesday, where the Spanish aircraft manufacturer Casa - EADS settles.
It's the first time that machines of this type have been in possession of a foreign army.
Abroad, they have only been seen at manoeuvres or air shows.
It is about the flightable two-seater version L-159T1, operated by the Czech army.
The remaining three planes are single-seater L-159.
These are currently still preserved in the Aero Vodochody, the intermediary of this business - the company Omnipol - took it over last week.
Jan Pejšek, according to the spokesman for the Ministry of Defence, is not yet known when she physically expeds the planes from Vodochody.
The single-seater fighter aircraft L-159, in turn, were separated from long-term mounted aircraft and without further deposits and investments on the part of the department of the ministry.
"The double-seated L-159T1 have been exchanged in current condition and appearance," Pejšek told the Právo newspaper.
He added that the army will not have to do without two-seater warplanes, as the Ministry of Defense signed a $200 million contract with Aero Vodochody in May 2009 for the conversion of four superfluous L-159 to the two-seater version.
A new plane has taken over the army in July, the second it wants to take over by the end of this year.
According to Pejšek, EADS has pledged to enforcing the warplanes into the equipment of one of the NATO countries.
“In the contract it is very clear that the company EADS is a customer of the aircraft L-159 and that they are intended to be used for the training of pilots of the NATO countries.
"It is therefore up to the buyer in what way he realizes the matter," Pejšek said.
The Wirtschaftszeitung (HN) had previously reported that the Spanish government has no interest in the L-159.
The barter of five L-159s against a Casa transport aircraft and the purchase of further aircraft for 3.5 billion kronor had been endorsed last year by Mirek Topolánek's government.
At that time, the Ministry of Defence did not tendered a tendering procedure for purchasing, but addressed EADS directly.
The defence office argued that this exchange would support the future exportation of the L-159 abroad.
The Czech army bought Aero 72 machines, of which it uses only 24, the rest it wanted to sell.
By the way, the purchase of the planes Casa should also have a judicial aftermath.
The European Commission sued the Czech Ministry of Defence at the European Court of Justice. The reason? It would have had to select the aircraft on the basis of an all-European tender and by no means by addressing a single manufacturer.
The army has already taken over three Casa aircraft, the last one is scheduled to land at Kbely Airport within fourteen days.
A longer index finger as a ring finger means a lower risk of developing cancer
Men whose forefinger is longer than the ring finger are obviously at less risk of developing prostate cancer.
That's the surprising result of a study published by the British Journal of Cancer on Wednesday.
This is supposed to be related to the male hormone testosterone.
"Our results show that the relative length of fingers can serve as a simple test for the risk of prostate cancer and especially in men up to sixty," explained the study's co-author, Ros Eeles, a professor at the Cancer Research Institute at the University of Warwick.
The finger could thus help determine which men should necessarily undergo a prostate cancer test, especially if genetic risks or other risk factors are present, such as familial health burdens, Ms Eeles added.
In the period 1994-2009, she followed more than 1500 patients with prostate cancer with her colleagues in Britain, but at the same time 3000 healthy men.
In more than half of the participants in the study, the index finger of the right hand was shorter than the ring finger.
For these men, there was the same likelihood of prostate cancer as for 19 percent of those men whose ring and index fingers were of the same length.
However, for men whose index finger was longer than the ring finger, this risk decreased by 33 percent, for men up to 87 percent.
The ratio of the length of the two fingers mentioned, which formates already in prenatal age, obviously reflects the different levels of sex hormones to which the child was exposed in the uterus.
Less testosterone corresponds to a longer index finger.
Previous research has shown that testosterone accelerates the growth of prostate cancer.
Václav Pačes, leading Czech geneticist and former chairman of the Academy of Sciences, told ČTK that he was fleetingly aware of this study, yet he does not consider the relationship between finger length and cancer risk to be "completely proven."
Czech Republic in the snow - D1 and D5 are closed
Like the largest part of Europe, the Czech is also under a blanket of snow on Thursday morning.
On the roads and sidewalks there is mostly stuck snow, the winter servants in many places have been on the road all night.
The D5 motorway between kilometer 89 and 90 in the direction of Rozvadov is blocked due to several accidents of trucks and passenger cars and also the D1 at Brno in the direction of Prague "stands".
Novinky.CZ the dispatcher of the Central Auto-Moto-Club (ÚAMK).
In kilometer 91 a truck crashed, at kilometer 93 two trucks collided, in this accident there are injured.
There are also a few cars lying in the road ditch.
There are also problems with kilometer 185 of the D1 motorway towards Prague.
Here, too, two trucks have collided.
The drivers should drive just behind Brno at kilometer 190 or drive over Brno and drive at kilometer 182 at the town of Kývalka.
That’s where miles of traffic jams are formed.
An accident has also happened on the D11, right at the entrance to Prague.
In Prague itself lies a blanket of snow several centimeters thick and the thermometer shows minus seven degrees Celsius.
For example, motorcades form in Prosek district, the street Štěrboholská is also completely clogged.
The safest mode of travel is definitely the metro.
The trams run with delays, for example on the line between Karlsplatz and Friedensplatz.
At eight o'clock in the morning, the Prague crisis staff meets.
There are also problems with the railway and on the airfield.
The Czech State Railway had to cancel seven express train connections due to snowdrifts.
There are problems, for example, on the route Prague - Kralupy or Prague - Beroun.
Travellers should definitely find out about the current situation.
Ruzyně Airport has been closed until 5 a.m. on Wednesday 11 a.m.
Most roads in South Moravia are only passable with increased caution.
Around Vyškov, three secondary roads are completely impassable, in the vicinity of Brno there are sometimes snowdrifts and in the vicinity of Blansko you are forced to use snow blowers on some higher roads.
One of the most affected areas is Výškov.
Three roads are still completely closed there: Rousínov - Slavkov, Bohdalice - Kučerov and also Šaratice - Milešovice.
Here, too, you will have to use snow blowers for snow removal.
When they become passable again, can not be estimated until now.
In the Hodonín region, caution is required on the territory of the White Carpathians.
ČTK cited a blanket of up to five centimeters on the roads, which may only be sprinkled with inert scattering agents for reasons of nature conservation, lies ČTK.
In the Czech Republic, from two to ten centimeters of fresh snow fell, the most in Northern Bohemia - up to 18 centimeters in places.
In the Moravian-Silesian region, up to 15 centimeters of fresh snow fell.
In the Czech Republic, however, it slowly stops snowing.
American soldiers have a new "smart weapon."
The American troops have used a new "smart" weapon in Afghanistan for the first time, which is previously known only by their code designation XM-25.
The generals hope that this new automatic weapon influences the conflict with the Taliban there and shifts the balance of power in their favor.
The new weapon is something like a super-accurate cannon.
Their projectiles are steered to the finish by laser with an accuracy of only a few meters.
The special projectiles of caliber 25 mm, which were developed for this rifle and contain highly explosive explosives, work practically like small grenades.
The projectiles can be programmed so that they can explode not only on impact with the target, but at any point of their ballistic trajectory.
This means that you can use them against an enemy that hides behind an obstacle - a wall or in the trench.
It is usually not protected from above, so the murderous steel splintering rain from above can easily put it out of action.
According to Richard Audett, who participated in the development of the XM-25, the new rifle is the first infantry weapon to take advantage of "smart" technologies.
“The soldier basically proceeds like this: he targets the target and sends out a laser beam that determines the distance, so he gains an exact visor point, after which he corrects the angle of elevation, then he only needs to push off.
“Let’s say they measured 543 m.”
“When you pull off, the gun loads up and shoots the projectile to the distance 543 meters plus minus one, two or three meters.
400 jobs will be relocated
The Fujitsu Group is relocating its subsidiary in Bad Homburg to Frankfurt.
In July next year, 400 employees will relocate their jobs from the previous office building at Marienbader Plack in Bad Homburg to Astro Park.
The Japanese group Fujitsu relocates its branch in Bad Homburg to Frankfurt.
As the industry giant, which specializes in computers and its maintenance announced on Wednesday, 400 women and men will relocate their jobs from the previous office building on Marienbader Platz in Bad Homburg to Astro Park in July next year, which is a large office complex on Lyoner Straße in the Niederrad district of Frankfurt.
A spokeswoman for Fujitsu explained that in the medium term, a further 100 jobs of the Group should be accommodated there, which have so far been distributed among the region.
The rental offer was extremely attractive, in addition, the open-plan offices in Astro Park would better accommodate the working method of the employees.
In Bad Homburg, too, Fujitsu was only a tenant.
The responsible managing director Bernd Wagner said, "with Frankfurt-Niederrad we have found a central location with excellent infrastructure".
The excellent connection to the airport is of great importance, especially for a global company like Fujitsu.
The new relocation of an important company within the region yesterday evoked joy among the winners and disappointment among the losers.
The Frankfurt economics secretary Markus Frank (CDU) said that a "global player" had been won again.
This is a "great confession to location".
Fujitsu fits the city, the settlement is an important impetus for Niederrad.
The mayor of Bad Homburg, Michael Korwisi (The Greens), regretted Fujitsu's decision.
The city had been informed early by the management about the intention.
"Of course, we offered our help to the company," Korwisi said.
However, the friendly contacts could not have changed the computer manufacturer's difficulties with the property's landlord at Marienbader Platz.
Ultimately, economic reasons would have been decisive.
Now it's about preventing a long vacancy in such a prominent place.
"I hope the owner and landlord of the building is cooperative."
With the other buildings at Mariánské Lázně, which was previously used by Fujitsu on Rathausplatz and the neighbouring "Small Vertical", were one of the most controversial construction projects in the spa town with the other buildings on Marienbader Platz ten years ago.
As a joint project of the city and a private project developer, a new city entrance was created between the station and the town hall, which caused criticism both because of the massive development of the previously partly used as a parking lot and the height of the buildings.
In 2001, the investment consulting and rating company Feri, the software manufacturer for travel distribution systems, Start Amadeus, as well as the computer manufacturer Fujitsu, which was still affiliated with Siemens at the time, moved into the houses.
The building used by Fujitsu was acquired in 2006 by the Frankfurt-based Deutsche Immobilien-Chances Group together with the Morgan Stanley Real Estate Fund and resolded a year later to Spain's Falcon Real Estate Investments SA in Madrid.
The Tokyo-based Fujitsu consumer describes itself as the world’s third-largest IT provider.
For example, it provides companies with computers and servers and also provides the necessary service.
The branch in the Rhine-Main area is one of the largest in Germany, but Fujitsu counts far more employees in Munich.
From Bad Homburg the subsidiary Fujitsu Technology Solutions moves into Frankfurt-Niederrad, the other subsidiaries at this location are to be Fujitsu Services and a company called TDS.
7000 square meters were rented in the Astro Park office building.
The twenty-year-old complex - the name alludes to its star-shaped floor plan - the central association of electrical engineering and electronics industries, among others, has its headquarters.
Amazon suspends server - threats against Assange
WikiLeaks is becoming increasingly distressed.
The Internet service provider Amazon has banned the disclosure platform from its servers.
Possibly there is political pressure after the publication of hundreds of thousands of secret diplomatic cables.
WikiLeaks founder Assange reportedly resides in England.
WikiLeaks is coming under increasing fire in the United States.
Internet activists lost access to their former American server and are now looking for an internet home in Europe.
Prominent American Senator Joe Lieberman, meanwhile, called on other countries and companies to boycott WikiLeaks.
And WikiLeaks founder Julian Assange, who is on Interpol's manhunt lists, is said to be staying in the UK, according to a newspaper report.
"WikiLeaks supplanted by Amazon servers."
"Free speech in the land of the free," the revelation activists said via short message service Twitter on Wednesday (local time).
Shortly earlier, the Internet company Amazon had banned WikiLeaks documents from its servers.
The Internet activists had resorted to the Amazon Web Service (AWS) during the publication of the secret diplomatic cables in order to be able to cope with the high numbers of access to the documents.
WikiLeaks tweeted: “Primacy – our dollars are now being spent to employ people in Europe.”
“If Amazon has such a problem with freedom of speech, “they should stop selling books.”
Amazon stopped WikiLeak’s use of its servers after Senator Joe Lieberman’s employees began investigations, CNN’s television station reported, citing Lieberman’s office.
The chairman of the Senate Homeland Security Committee had threatened Amazon with a boycott, the British "Guardian" reported.
"I would have liked Amazon to take this action sooner in light of the previous releases of classified information by WikiLeaks," the senator said.
Lieberman, meanwhile, called for the platform to be denied use of servers in other countries.
"WikiLeaks' illegal, outrageous and ruthless action puts our national security at risk and endangers lives around the globe," it said in a statement.
“No responsible company—whether American or foreign—should help WikiLeaks in its efforts to disseminate stolen material.”
WikiLeaks founder Assange, who is wanteded by authorities in Sweden on suspicion of rape, is on Interpol's manhunt list.
According to a report by London's The Independent newspaper, the 39-year-old Australian is staying in the U.K. that the exact location is known to Scotland Yard.
The Internet activist has always described the investigation in Sweden as the intrigue of his opponents.
WikiLeaks spokesman Kristinn Hrafnsson said in London there had been threats.
"There were even calls for an assassination of Julian Assange."
That's why Assange rightly fears for his safety, Hrafnsson said.
The WikiLeaks founder will therefore continue to hide.
Hrafnsson apparently responded to statements from North America.
In America, the former Republican governor of Arkansas, Mike Huckabee, had demanded that those responsible for the WikiLeaks revelations be charged with treason and executed.
In Canada, an adviser to Prime Minister Stephen Harper - evidently ironically - said Assange should be "killed" and Obama could "use a drone."
Between the White House and Assange, meanwhile, an exchange of blows erupted.
U.S. President Barack Obama's spokesman, Robert Gibbs, called it "ridiculous and absurd" that Assange had demanded the resignation of Secretary of State Hillary Clinton if she is responsible for recently revealed spying calls to diplomats.
"I'm not quite sure why we care about a guy's opinion with a website," Gibbs said.
“Our foreign policy and the interests of this country are far more significant than its one website.”
The U.S. government is now comprehensively testing the protection of its databases.
Russell Travers, the vice-director of the Center for Anti-Terrorism Measures, has been Appointed as Special Representative, the White House said.
Travers was responsible for developing "necessary structural reforms" that became necessary after WikiLeak's disclosure of the embassy reports.
The White House also wants to review the ways the entire administration exchanges and protects information.
The U.S. government also announced that it wanted to ensure the security of dissidents and activists, should they be pendaned by the WikiLeaks publication.
"We've made every effort to get in touch with them," foreign ministry spokesman Philip Crowley said.
“We stand ready to protect them if necessary.”
Ice-University
At Gelato University near Bologna, ice cream confectioners are prepared for better seasons.
The three-day basic course for future Gelatieri has the largest influx and visitors from all over the world.
Where does all the ice come from?
From gelatier and gelataio.
The gelatiere, somewhat antimally the ice-cream confectioner in German, manufactures the cream according to all the rules of Italian ice-making.
The Gelataio retrieves the ice cream from the refrigerated counter with a spatula, spreads it into the waffle and then sells it.
To know that, you don't have to go to Anzola dell'Emilia in the province of Bologna.
But if you want to know more about ice cream, you won't get past the "Gelato-University" of the Italian company Carpigiani.
The ice-university was set up by the manufacturer of ice machines in 2003 so that the ice cream from its machines can meet the high Italian ideals everywhere.
The three-day basic course for future gelatieri has the largest influx and visitors from all over the world.
However, there are also multi-day special courses around chocolate ice cream and internships in the in-house ice cream parlor.
Some are dreaming of a new career in the midlife crisis, others simply venture onto the slick ice of their vague longings.
This time there are 40 participants from 19 countries from all continents - from Brazil to Hungary to Saudi Arabia.
Jacky, an art teacher from California, loves the slow food movement and wants to offer a European quality product in the country of industrial ice cream in the future.
Anke from Berlin is a management consultant and wants to bag something of his own after years in a nerve-wracking job.
Two young Egyptians dream of opening an ice cream parlor in Cairo.
A man from Malta and one from Brazil would like to improve the quality of the ice cream in their bars - and Canadian housewife Julia arrives because her children love Italian ice cream.
Maestro-Gelatiere Luciano Ferrari first explains to the participants the ideal properties of the perfect ice cream.
Decisive is not only good taste, but also the silky suppleness and good consistency.
Luciano doesn't forget the icing on the cake of the national and southern superstructure at all: "Italian gelato is simply the best - and then there is still a lot of romance."
The first step towards manufacturing seems quite simple.
Luciano dumps the basic ingredients milk, cream, milk powder, sugar and stabilizers into a large mixing device that heats everything to 85 degrees with an electronic program and then immediately cools down to four degrees.
Then he twists a tap on the machine, and out comes a pasteurized white base cream, basis for all milk ice cream varieties.
Immediately, the lecturer is attacked with questions: Can ice cream only be produced with natural fabrics without a stabilizer?
How can one let one's lust for experimentation run wild?
Luciano warns against being misled by the zeitgeist.
If you talk about ice cream "only from natural ingredients", you may still have used stabilizers - usually a natural product, such as guar flour from the guar bean.
Of course, you can also make ice cream without a stabilizer, only it has to be sold within a day, otherwise it sinks together, and the customer bites on noisy ice granules.
Certain variations on the basic mass are allowed.
It gets more exciting with the flavors.
For the taste in the ice cream, Maestro Luciano distinguishes between the ingredients that you buy in the fruit department or at the sweets of the supermarket.
There are also powders and pastes.
“The suppliers will all try to turn on you dozens of flavored additions.”
“But you should just experiment.”
Either with the fruit sorbets with sugar and fresh fruits.
Or just with the ice cream from the white basic mass, which becomes a yellow cream with eggs and with cocoa powder the basis for all sorts of chocolate seductions.
Now the students are allowed to the ice machines.
Luciano lets them produce four different types of vanilla, chocolate, mocca and hazelnut ice each - with or without eggs in the base, with more or less vanilla aroma or other natural additives.
The fruit ice varieties are also subjected to the same experiment.
More flavorings, more fruit puree - this isn't necessarily better in terms of taste.
Hazelnut ice cream doesn't necessarily taste nutty if it contains more hazelnuts.
The most popular kiwi ice cream is the working group, which uses more fruits, less water and slightly less stabilizer.
Only a well-balanced mixture raises the taste of a gelato correctly, says Luciano.
Ice production is a computational task: If peach puree is used with a lot of sugar, the sugar content must be reduced in the basic recipe.
An extra portion of sugar is needed for lemon ice cream.
Luciano pulls out a device with which it determines the sugar content of the fruit varieties (winemakers thus measure the Oechsle in the grape must).
Luckily, tables make computing easier.
Making ice doesn't require as much physical exertion as before.
Not only the device for the automatic production of the basic mass helps.
There is also a device in which the finished ice mixture is beaten at the same time and cooled to minus temperatures.
Despite all automation, small breakdowns aren’t missing.
The Brazilian Gustavo licks himself and his classmates from top to bottom with fruit puree when the oversized hand blender gets out of scrutiny.
"In every course," says Maestro Luciano, "it's only a matter of time before something like this happens."
Inspectors in twilight
The European food authority Efsa was subverted by the industry experimenting with genetic plants.
This allegation is now formulated by the expert group Testbiotech, which sees itself as an independent institute for the impact assessment of biotechnology.
"It sounds like a conspiracy theory, but we documented it well," Testbiotech boss Christoph Then said in Munich on Wednesday.
The head of the Efsa expert group on genetically modified plants, Harry Kuiper, and another Efsa expert have also been under contract for years with the lobbyist association Ilsi.
This is paid by corporations such as Monsanto, Nestle or Bayer.
Kuiper played a major role in the Efsa testing regulations for gene plants and is thus in a clear conflict of interest.
The Efsa rules are identical in proportion to position papers of the Ilsi and thus bear the signature of the industry, which is actually to be controlled by the authority in Parma, Italy.
It is already the second time that Testbiotech criticizes a conflict of interests.
For example, a high-ranking Efsa manager has recently been without restrictions or transitional period to the Swiss agricultural group Syngenta - and has thus changed sides.
The Mitteldeutscher Rundfunk (MDR) had also recently revealed personnel links between the controlling EU authority and the industrial lobby, which the Federal Environment Agency, as well as EU parliamentarians, has made noisy.
Efsa, however, cannot detect a conflict of interest.
One draws on all serious data, including those of the industry, as long as they are of high professional quality, it said in a statement.
The experts working for Efsa do not require that they have no contacts with the industry.
You'd have to disclose these, though.
Kuiper concedes his work for the International Life Sciences Institute (Ilsi) in the USA.
This describes itself as a neutral forum for experts from science, industry and politics - among other things for the risk assessment for biotechnology without a lobby order.
In the matter, independent experts such as Then criticize that genetically engineered plants are currently being approved in animals without any feeding test and the reaction of gene plants to environmental influences is not being tested.
"We want to know, for example, what genetically engineered maize is doing on the field," Then says.
Current practice is to test only isolated proteins, but not the whole gene plant, which does not provide reliable results.
"If you don't seek the risks, then how are you supposed to find them?" Then asks accusingly.
Angelika Hilbeck sees it that way.
The scientist has been researching genetic engineering since 1994 and is currently working at the Technical University of Zurich.
In this field, as in the USA, the industry is now increasingly trying to influence or discredit critical scientists.
Therefore, there are hardly any researchers who aren’t intertwined with corporations.
For companies critical studies would usually not be published.
"The industry controls security research," Hilbeck asserts.
The trail of stones
Uli Gsell also got some, a total of about 400 kilos.
On Monday evening he stood in front of the Stuttgart station, the tuba on his back.
It's demo.
It's snowing.
The story is well-known: in September, the excavators had arrived and had begun to abort a large piece of the north wing.
The Stuttgart world was no longer the same: tens of thousands demonstrated against S21, the billion-dollar lowering of the station.
When it came to the construction stop, much of the wing lay in debris.
Broken into 6000, 7000 pieces.
Crailsheimer Muschelkalk, a soft coarse stone, installed between 1914 and 1920.
Gsell is a sculptor in Ostfildern, 43 years old.
He's secured some of the chunks.
“These stones are relics.”
“They must not disappear.”
He enthuses: "A cheap and poor stone, simple and earthy."
When he sawed one, he smelled of cigarette smoke.
What he's going to do with the chunks, he doesn't know yet.
Stones have time.
Uli Gsell, too.
The trail of the Stuttgart stones leads into a forest near Tübingen to a former ammunition depot of the French army, where Manuel Rongen has erected his stone realm.
The 44-year-old trades with old stones.
He has bought all those involved in the demolition in Stuttgart.
"It would be a shame, they'd ground to gravel or disappear into the landfill," he says at his office, a wooden box that was once a guardhouse.
Insulted and threatened him, he says, when it was written in the newspapers in September that he had bought the remains of the north wing from the demolition contractor.
But he's been talking to the S-21 opponents for days.
And he explained to them that he couldn't do anything for demolition.
And when it happens, it would be the most sensible thing, if the stones are used further.
About 200 S-21 opponents took one of the average 80 kilo cuboids with them, unit price 6.50 to 14 euros.
As a souvenir, but some also in the hope of being able to give him back during a reconstruction.
Rongen had bought 500 tonnes, 25 large truckloads.
About half are still stored in the forest, the rest have long been sold.
Garden owners, landscape gardeners, house builders.
They had come from everywhere and dragged away what the trunk and trailers could carry.
The Stuttgart stones of the impetus have long since turned into "Mäuerle" or into boundaries of herbal beds.
"Actually, this stone isn't anything special," Rongen says.
“Wouldn’t be the political component.”
He climbs into his SUV, taking a tour of his 20-acre empire.
He studied and demolished law, later biology, then he became interested in computers and eventually became a stone dealer.
And stone lovers.
He stores 40000 tons of stones.
From all over Germany, he gathers his treasures together.
He says he's the Republic's greatest old stone merchant.
He's slowing down.
In the snow there are artfully hewn sandstones.
"They once belonged to Dresden's Marienbrücke," he recounts.
Elbe sandstone, yellow, warm, he raves.
"My favorite stone."
It goes up the path through the woods.
He points to a heap: red sandstone, formerly part of Karlsruhe station.
And further.
"Beautiful travertine."
Once upon a time, the cuboids belonged to the tribune of Stuttgart’s Neckar Stadium.
Stones and stories everywhere in the snow, Cannstatter Travertin from the Stuttgart Police Headquarters, reddish granite from St. Blasien Cathedral, Tuff from Heilbronn Hospital.
What work, what effort, what sweat.
When Rongen thinks of how the stones used to be hewn by hand and moved with simple pulleys and ox carts, he becomes quite reverential.
“It would be a scandal if you would shred something like that.”
He did not become rich, certainly not rich.
But 14 people are employed by the stone trader today, in addition, stonemasons, a sculptor, a blacksmith, a glassblower and a horticulturalist have settled upstairs in the forest.
"We work hard, but it's satisfying," says the resiductor.
He'll have to go to Cologne soon.
There are rocks to be rescued there, too.
Talent struggle for high-potentials
There is a lot of crowds at the buffet.
16 students crowd around salmon rolls, tomato soup and sweets.
The Technical University of Braunschweig and the car manufacturer Volkswagen have invited the mechanical engineering and design students to a workshop.
In a kind of ideas competition, they are supposed to collaborate with VW developers about the car of the future - and get to know Volkswagen by the way.
In the competition for the smartest minds, many companies now go into close combat and, like VW, rely on cooperations with the universities.
Their mission: to recruit the most gifted graduates as early as possible in the lecture halls before they enter the labour market and are courted by others.
And because the universities do not publish lists about the achievements of their students, one tries to spy and bind the exceptional talent in practical projects.
On the second day of the workshop at the TU Braunschweig, the car company promotes the talents.
A young lady from the human resources department distributes ballpoint pens, gummy bears and folders with VW logo.
She tells the hopeful young talents about internship programs and student jobs and shows them ways into the group.
Nico Selle from TU Braunschweig supervises the project with VW.
He sees the benefits for both sides: "The students get in touch with the industry and gain experience."
"VW can get to know students at the same time, take a closer look at interesting candidates and consider who would be eligible as employees."
Such collaborations, according to Selle, aren't uncommon.
What’s new is that the students are fought so offensively.
"Otherwise, the focus was more on the professional project," says Selle.
The fight for the newly graduates will probably get even tougher.
In many industries, companies can no longer choose their employees from a large number of applicants.
Instead, they have to advertise themselves and concentrate in particular around highly skilled workers.
Especially graduates from the engineering disciplines are in an excellent negotiating position.
According to the Association of German Engineers VDI, over 40,000 engineering positions remained vacant in October.
And the so-called engineering gap will become even bigger, according to VDI forecasts.
Roman Dykta is going on talent hunting for the auditing and consulting firm KPMG.
He organizes elaborate recruiting events such as the KPMG-Race, a kind of modern scavenger hunt, in which the participants have to find their ways on the basis of GPS coordinates.
The company intends to send eight promising students across the USA next year.
By the way, the participants can visit KPMG branches and get to know the company.
If you are allowed, you decide in two selection rounds in advance.
Only the crème de la crème of the many applicants flies to the USA.
According to Dykta, the guns are getting bigger and bigger when punching around the high potentials at the universities.
"We want to stand out from the crowd with the race."
"Only an easy sailing trip was too little for us."
For talent hunter Dykta, the bill seems to pick up.
“To this day, we still have contact with all participants of a similar action last year.”
"Some are on the verge of graduation and want to apply to us soon," he says.
In addition to the direct queuing on campus, especially small and medium-sized companies are also popular at professional trade fairs.
"We're trying to reach graduates at trade fairs."
"For example, at Intersolar," reports Sitha Stübe, human resources manager at solar technology manufacturer Solarworld.
The Bonn-based company employs around 1500 people in Germany.
Stübe knows about the problem of young talent.
There are currently 35 vacancies advertised on the company website.
“It’s very hard to fill vacant positions right now.”
"That costs a lot of time and money," she says.
Another way is the contact through the university professors.
Solarworld cooperates with various universities, such as the university in Freiberg.
"The professors give indications that you can do internships with us or write your diploma thesis."
Expensive promotions aren’t announced by the solar specialist.
For this purpose, the company tries to advertise itself with other advantages: personal atmosphere, flat hierarchies and easy communication.
"Often, a large corporation can't offer this," says HR manager Stübe.
Money no longer plays the leading role in job search.
Flexible working hours, childcare or good opportunities for advancement are other decisive factors.
In a study by the Great Place to Work Institute, Solarworld ranked 55th among Germany's 100 best employers.
The institute draws up an annual leaderboard of German employers.
To this end, it asks employees how satisfied they are with their work and their job environment and looks at the framework conditions for the employees.
Personnel manager Stübe hopes the placement will alsove receive advantages among job seekers.
"I think that applicants are especially looking for external assessments about a potential employer," she says.
Wolf Reiner Kriegler from the German Employer Branding Academy advises companies that want to build up a good reputation as an employer with a long-term strategy.
He advises his customers to be more authenticity.
And that, according to Kriegler, means above all internal measures.
"The best way to make yourself attractive as an employer is through one's own employees," he says.
In industry jargon, this is called internal employer branding, i.e. employer’s branding within its own workforce.
“Satisfied employees are the best ambassadors.”
If you treat your people well and they identify with the company, they would advertise their employer to friends and acquaintances all by themselves.
"That's like a game with gang," Kriegler says.
Obama halts new oil drilling
There will be no new oil drilling off the coast of the US state of Florida in the Gulf of Mexico in the coming years.
A corresponding ban has enacted the government in Washington - and thus definitively revoked its previous plans.
Even off the Atlantic coast, new drilling remains prohibited for the time being.
This was announced by Interior Minister Ken Salazar on Wednesday.
The trigger for the strict ban, which is to apply for at least the next five years, is the explosion of the "Deepwater Horizon" drilling platform in April.
Ahead of the misfortune that killed eleven workers and leaded to U.S.'s biggest environmental disaster, President Barack Obama had signaled his readiness to allow new drilling in the eastern part of the Gulf of Mexico, under pressure from the opposition and industry.
The proponents want to reduce U.S. dependence on oil shipments from abroad.
Anyone responsible for the devastating oil spill in Gulf has not been conclusively clarified to date.
At the heart of the criticism is Britain's energy company BP, which is confronted with billion-dollar demands.
A commission of inquiry on Obama's behalf is due to present its findings over the course of December.
In October, Interior Minister Ken Salazar had lifteded the drilling halt imposed after the explosion.
Subsequently, various oil companies immediately submitted applications for new drilling projects.
With tighter controls and stricter security regulations, Obama and Salazar want to rule out another disaster.
Currently, the competent supervisory authority, which had fallen into disrepute after numerous allegations of corruption, is being fundamentally reformed.
In parallel, Obama has announced plans to close lucrative tax loopholes for the oil and gas companies.
The industry is now publicly warning that the production volume in the Gulf would be significantly lower due to stricter regulation than before.
"The government's plans are the biggest brake on the growth of the oil and gas industry," according to a recent industry survey by consulting firm BDO - ahead of Wednesday's drilling ban.
The American Energy Alliance also laments the uncertainty in the industry: "No one knows exactly what's going to change with regulation."
Investments in new funding projects would be postponed until further notice - at the expense of growth.
Ogoniland has burned down
The water in the rivers is oil-brown, it smells like at the gas station: billions of liters of leaked crude oil have destroyed nature in Nigeria's Ogoniland.
The people live on theft, corruption and illegal oil cooking.
Celestine Akpobari means well.
"Don't plague yourself," the eco-activist reassures the journalists: "You're going to get enough oil sludge in front of the lens today."
The 37-year-old Nigerian has followed an attempt to photograph a boy wading to the chest in the water with the streaks surrounding him: In the rainbow-coloured water gloss, the scene looks almost pretty.
It cannot be seen that brown crude oil swaths are floating beneath the surface.
Esther Badom on the banks of the Bodo-Jetty River used to collect shrimp here.
In the meantime, the sluggishly flowing water is completely dead.
Five years agove, a bristled pipeline poured into the huge wetland.
Fishermen from the nearby town of Bodo now have to row for six hours to get into fishy waters: Ogoniland, located in the heart of the Niger Delta, is considered one of the world's worst polluted regions.
On the drive through the mangrove swamps crisscrossed by rivers, the full extent of devastation becomes apparent.
Instead of light water, the motorboat stirs up brown broth, who dives his hand into the water, pulls out oily fingers.
The roots of the mangrove trees protrude like black liquorice sticks from the ground, sadly occasional palm trees hang their fronds.
Swarms of birds, as are otherwise customary in wetlands, are searched in vain: As soon as the outboard is silenced, ghostly tranquility spreads in the former paradise, which smells like a gas station.
"What is happening here is ecological warfare," says Celestine Akpobari sinister: "We live in constant danger to health."
The war comparison is appropriate.
The Ogoni settlement area is a battlefield that has been contested for decades: the first explorers from Europe invaded more than 60 years ago, here in the 1980s with the later executed writer Ken Saro-Wiwa organized the first resistance, here the Shell Group had to stop its production in 1993, because it became too dangerous for its oil workers.
Even today, the company is denied the return to Ogoniland: While more than two million barrels of black gold are pumped from the entire delta region of the three-fold size of Switzerland with more than 5000 drilling wells and 7000 kilometers of pipelines every day, the oil fields in Ogoniland are collapsed, the situation is too unstable for the Group.
Every few hundred meters, steely racks like bizarre sculptures protrude from the water: "Christian trees" the heads of the boreholes are called far too tender.
They are up to 50 years old, most corroded to the marrow.
Again and against such a drilling head breaks apart, Akpobari says, then a constant stream of crude oil runs into the water.
According to expert estimates, more than two billion liters have flowed into the ecosystem of the delta in 50 years: year after year, the amount of oil as it penetrated into the sea off Alaska 21 years ago - one of the greatest environmental disasters in the history of humanity.
Who is to blame for the ongoing eco-GAU, is argued about.
While Shell Group blames sabotage and organized oil theft for more than 90 percent of pollution, environmentalists see bristling drilling heads and licking pipelines as the main culprit.
Shell sets much lower safety standards in Nigeria than usual in the world, the US scientist Richard Steiner accused the group in 2008: The rate of defects on the lines alone is “many times higher than elsewhere”.
In a place that looks remotely like an abandoned, oil-smeared workshop under the open sky, figures emerge from the mangroves: they are splinter-naked and oil-smeared up to their necks.
Under one of the many barrels, which can be partly connected to tubes, partly arbitrarily scattered on the site, flickers a fire: an illegal small refinery.
In the barrel, the crude oil above the fire is heated for several hours, explains Michael, the group's chief executive.
At first, gasoline penetrates through the pipes into the collecting pot, later trickles kerosene at the end of the line and finally diesel.
Michael and four employees produce up to four barrels of diesel every 300 liters every day: "This brings us 34,000 Naira" - around $22.
Delta residents are taking advantage of the chronic fuel shortages in the largest petroleum state in Black Africa.
Nigeria has a single refinery.
At just under €40 cents per litre, the illegal cookers can still undercut: because they get their raw material for free.
It comes from "bunkers" who tap the pipeline network across Ogoniland: In this way, according to experts, at least 16 million liters of crude oil are lost every day throughout the Niger Delta.
However, only a vanishingly small part of the provisionally intermediary raw material is illegally ennobled: Organized criminal networks, which include politicians and high-ranking militarians, fill the lion’s share into tankers and sell it on the world market.
Activist Akpobari considers the debate as to whether the environmental catastrophe is due to the activities of the population or the lax mode of production to be purely academic.
"If the oil companies were not perceived as invaders and if they had not so thoroughly destroyed the livelihoods of fishermen, there would be neither sabotage nor theft and illegal refineries."
In the meantime, however, the relationship of the Ogoni with Shell has been so destroyed that only a withdrawal of the group would be possible: "Maybe other nationalities such as the Chinese turn out to be better."
That's where Michael gets nervous.
"It's time for you to disappear," says the muscular oilman.
“Every minute the soldiers can come.”
If the illegal oil refiners are caught by the special unit of the armed forces, they are threatened with no arrest, but a painful bribe.
Like all Nigerians, the soldiers, who come mainly from the country's north, also want to benefit from the raw material.
"This damned stuff has put our country on the dog in every way," Akpobari says.
Investigators detect dangerous construction fault in A380 engines
The A380 apparently has a major safety problem.
Investigators have discovered during the investigation of an emergency-landed machine a possible construction fault, which could lead to a "catastrophic engine failure".
The airline Qantas now wants to sue the engine manufacturer.
The passengers of the emergency-landed Qantas-Airbus at the beginning of November were apparently much more lucky than previously known: The Australian aviation security authority has identified a major safety problem with the engines of some A380 aircraft in its investigation - and now warns of a possible construction fault.
The potential defect would have serious consequences: With an oil hose connection, it could generate fatigue cracks and leaks, it said in a statement published this Thursday by the authority ATSB.
This could have led to "catastrophic engine failure."
Affected engines manufactured by Rolls-Royce in 20 A380 aircraft.
Underneath is the Qantas-Airbus, which had to make an emergency landing in Singapore on November 4.
The Australian airline has now taken legal action against Britain's turbine maker.
She filed a plea in an Australian Federal Court in Sydney, which allows him to take further legal action against the company.
Qanta's chief executive Alan Joyce said his airline would demand damages for the incident in early November.
In the fire of the engine, parts had loosened and damaged the wings.
Rolls-Royce accounted for an oil fire as the cause of the damage.
After the incident, up to 40 engines have to be replaced worldwide.
Australian investigators announced it would carry out further safety tests on the superjumbos, which are equipped with the affected engines.
The safety recommendation was the result of a collaboration with Rolls-Royce, ATSB spokesman Martin Dolan said.
The security problem was considered sufficiently important to inform all those affected.
After the emergency landing of the Qantas plane, the airline had kept its A380 fleet on the ground for weeks.
Singapore Airlines temporarily left about three of its eleven A380s on the ground.
Lufthansa replaced the engine on an A380 as a precautionary measure.
The double-storey aircraft is the world's largest passenger aircraft.
Nationwide snowfall triggers traffic chaos
Germany all in white: Overnight snow has fallen almost all over the country.
On many roads there is chaos, trains and flights fail.
At least three people were killed in car accidents.
The winter frostes Germany: In freezing temperatures, almost the whole republic has snoweded into Thursday at night - which in many places leads to traffic chaos.
In the north and to the east there are sometimes 15 centimeters thick blankets of snow.
On several highways and many roads, traffic is severely restricted.
On the A7 near Flensburg, the winter service was in continuous use, but did not cope with the constantly reluctant snow.
In addition, violent gusts swept over the Baltic coast.
The Federal Maritime and Hydrographic Agency (BSH) is expectinging storm surges with water levels of 1.2 metres above normal on the Baltic coast of Schleswig-Holstein from the morning.
In Saxony, snow drifts on the highways and federal roads led to considerable problems.
According to the severe weather center Meteomedia in Saxon Switzerland, alarm level was therefore very popular - the highest level ever.
Sometimes 30 centimeters of fresh snow fell there, fresh and strong winds caused drifting.
Several trucks and cars got stuck there during the night.
Also in Saxony-Anhalt, the police authorities reported major disturbances on the streets.
On the A2, snow and wind led to stalled traffic and congestions.
In Thuringia, the A4 and A9 were affected by the snowfalls.
According to Deutsche Bahn, there are considerable disturbances in Saxony, Saxony-Anhalt, Thuringia and northern Bavaria.
Snow on the tracks, overturned trees on the rails or destroyed overhead lines and turnouts are the main problems.
In Saxony, long-distance traffic had to be discontinued from Leipzig to Nuremberg.
An ICE had to stop its journey to Munich in Saalfeld in Thuringia.
The 165 passengers were provided by firefighters and rescue workers with wool blankets, food and drinks on the train.
Snow winds swept across the streets.
At major German airports, the snow winds lead to numerous flight failures.
Some cancellations have already been reported in Munich, according to a spokeswoman for the flight information headquarters.
In Frankfurt am Main, 40 flights have already been canceled, according to a Fraport spokeswoman.
The winter service is in continuous use.
Although there are only a few stranded passengers at the airport, there are still almost 400 cots available for them.
Accidents occurred on many roads on Wednesday evening.
In the evening, a truck driver died in a collision with a train at a level crossing in Ostholstein.
The engine driver was slightly injured.
In the vicinity of Nuremberg, a similar accident occurred due to snow-covered roads: Here, too, a truck was captured on a level crossing because the vehicle got stuck.
The driver died.
Four passengers were injured on the train - the route from Bayreuth to Nuremberg was therefore closed until the morning hours.
In addition, a 30-year-old woman died after she knocked herself over with her car near Aschaffenburg on a smooth road.
However, the meteorologists have good news: Although the snow will still be loose and powdery and will therefore stir up with light gusts.
But the wind will weaken.
Only the Baltic Sea is hit by fast and storming winds.
However, it remains very cold - locally up to minus 15 degrees.
Deutsche Bank is looking for a buyer for its twin towers
Deutsche Bank wants to separate itself from its landmark: According to a newspaper report, the money house is looking for buyers for the two office towers at its Frankfurt headquarters.
The property could bring in up to €500 million.
Deutsche Bank has just made its corporate headquarters pretty: the twin towers in Frankfurt's banking district have been modernised; meanwhile, the bankers were allowed to return to their old workplace.
But now the financial group apparently wants to get the costs for the renovation out again - and is looking for a buyer for the two high-rise buildings, as the "Financial Times Germany" writes, citing a spokesman.
Reasons for the planned sale did not specify the spokesman.
"We are always checking whether it makes more sense to own or sell real estate ourselves and subsequently rent it back," he said.
Germany’s largest credit institution thus releases its headquarters after only a short time.
In mid-2007, the bank took over the double towers for €272 million from the investors of an in-house fund and then extensively refurbished them.
According to the data, the energetic conversion swallowed up around 200 million euros.
A sale would probably bring a lot of money to the bank.
According to the newspaper, industry experts estimate the value of the towers now renamed "Greentowers" at around 500 million euros.
Whether the buildings are resolded to a fund owned by the bank or to an independent investor is currently still open, the spokesman said.
Thousands grieve for dead pals
More than 10,000 people have commemorated the buried miners in New Zealand who have been pronounced dead after several explosions at a mine.
The corpses could still not be rescued, because the security situation in the misfortune shaft is still precarious.
Almost two weeks ago, a violent explosion occurred in the Pike River Mine near the town of Greymouth.
29 miners, aged between 17 and 62, were spilled underground.
A probe had been lowered into the scene of the accident, but had discovered no trace of survivors.
Due to the high concentration of toxic gases and further subsequent explosions, no rescue workers could descend for safety reasons.
After the second violent detonation, the authorities and experts had assumed that there was no longer a chance of survival for the buddies.
The bodies could not be recovered so far.
Now relatives and friends commemorated the dead with a memorial service.
Tens of thousands of people had gathered in bright sunshine at Greymouth on New Zealand's South Island.
For two minutes they remained silent.
In honor of the buried miners, 29 tables were set up on which helmets, lamps and the names of the buddies could be seen.
Pictures and personal belongings, including garments and surfboards, also found their place here.
In front of the country's government buildings, the flags wafted at half-mast.
New Zealand had hoped that the miners "from the depths of the earth" would come back to the surface, New Zealand's Prime Minister John Key told the commemoration.
“But they’ve never come home.”
He could not conclude with the death of his son, as long as he had not seen his corpse, said Lawrie Drew, whose son Zen was buried in the pit.
In addition to the prime minister and relatives of the victims, several foreign diplomats attended the commemoration.
In the first explosion on November 19, miners of British and South African origin had also been spilled.
Rescue workers began extinguishing a fire inside the coal mine on New Zealand's South Island.
As soon as the explosive gases are displaced, it will start to cool down the glowing temperatures inside the mine, Police President Gary Knowles told journalists.
Hell, warriors!
Does splatter fit the art cinema?
The director Nicolas Winding Refn explores old genre boundaries with his fighter epic "Walhalla Rising".
Which audience might the Danish director Nicolas Winding Refn have thought of when he shot "Walhalla Rising"?
A captivated Viking warrior with only one eye (Mads Mikkelsen) frees himself from his tormentors and puts his opponents around with almost superhuman powers - sounds ideal for young male spectators in search of bloody action, precisely because our hero likes to use rabid killing methods and tears the intestines out of the living body of his enemies when he has a bad mood.
On the other hand, as it is actually called, doesn’t say a word about the whole film, and the other characters can only be carried away to one or two sentences or a few excruciating death screams.
Otherwise it will be silenced and reveled in the spooky hilly landscape of Scotland, which provides the backdrop to the Viking world of about a thousand years ago.
Ideal more suitable for adult cinematographers interested in existentialism and very quiet camera rides.
It’s nice that if a film has multiple target groups, it’s only a shame if they’re mutually exclusive.
Director Winding Refn has been regarded as a kind of Danish version of Quentin Tarantino since his wild and styled drug dealer trilogy "Pusher," but stoically refuses to make his works accessible to the arthouse with a little more humour or a little less brutality.
Most art cinema friends are likely to be deterred by the brain mass splashing around and the intestines.
The action fans die of boredom at the latest, when one-eyes for freely interpretable reasons with a troupe of violent Christian missionaries via ship to Jerusalem and is stuck for an eternity in lull and fog, before the crew apparently arrives in America and remains silent there.
No wonder, then, that "Walhalla Rising" has not found a cinema rental in Germany and has now only appeared on DVD.
This is especially because of the magnificent pictures, but also because behind the pretentious to blood-dripping façade hides a sometimes exciting piece of cinema that deserves some attention.
This film isn't about dialogue or a stringent plot, it's all atmosphere, a film-made fever dream.
And there's no escape from that for the viewer.
Refn hardly gives him a moment of relaxation and with his icy pictures and the sinister trance-like soundtrack very slowly close his throat, but above all with the incomprehensible hatred that strikes him from the hearts of the characters.
Only a little blonde boy, who stands faithfully one-eye, keeps something like innocencence, everyone else is driven by anger.
Most of all the one-eyed hero, whose motives are never explained, but who for enemies and temporary allies eventually becomes a warrior from hell, possibly the ambassador of the devil himself.
He means perdition, but perhaps only for the already depraved.
"Walhalla Rising" is an enigmatic film that you can hate and admire, to annoyance, excitement and amazement.
It's an experience.
Mysterious earthworks
The Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) has new indications of a secret nuclear program in Syria.
However, the country defends itself from taking a position on this - it still allows checks.
Around midnight from September 5 to September 6, 2007, at least four Israeli F-15 I warplanes attack a mysterious, cube-shaped building in Syria.
It stands on the banks of the Euphrates; the nearest larger city, 30 kilometers away, is Dair as-Saur.
Her name emerges in International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) documents when it comes to the reactor that Syria allegedly built with help from North Korea in the cuboid, hidden in a valage that departs from the electricity.
According to a briefing by the U.S. foreign intelligence agency CIA at the end of April 2008, the reactor, also common under the name of the nearby village of el-Kibar, would have been well suited to generate plutonium - from which nuclear weapons could be built.
For other purposes, on the other hand, the technology from the fifties is hardly suitable.
The CIA presented a video presentation with blurred satellite images.
By computer animation, it blended into where what components of the reactor are said to have been before the destruction, which was a scaled-down copy of the North Korean model from the Yongbyon nuclear center.
In addition, she presented photos that show the building up close or even the interior.
The reactor had been on the verge of commissioning, it said, but had not yet been equipped with fuel rods.
Today, more than three years after the bombardment, little more is known than then, yes the puzzles became rather larger over time.
Former U.S. President George W. Bush recently revealed in his memoirs that the CIA was quite certain before the attack that Syria was building a reactor in el-Kibar, but saw little evidence that there was "an active nuclear weapons program" in the country.
Some intelligence officials in Europe prefer to be even more cautious and speak only of an "alleged reactor," even if they consider the CIA presentation "plausible".
They are lacking hard evidence, as does the IAEA.
Asked how safe he was to have a reactor in front of him, a senior U.N. diplomat in the summer only lapidarily replied that the IAEA had "enough material to ask questions."
The Syrians hurriedly managed the rubble of the alleged "unused military building" aside and thoroughly leveled the site.
Nevertheless, in June 2008, an IAEA team under former chief inspector Olli Heinonen found traces of uranium in el-Kibar that had been made by humans.
In addition, the IAEA received satellite images from commercial providers directly after attack through a Member State, which should be more meaningful than those presented by the CIA.
However, these recordings are not available on the market because the unidentified client - perhaps Israel - bought them exclusively.
The IAEA therefore cannot independently verify its authenticity.
Syria has always claimed to have never operated a secret nuclear program or built a reactor.
The uranium comes from the Israeli weapons, Damascus explained.
However, the IAEA dismissed this as unlikely after analyzing the particles.
Damascus has since refused to discuss it - as well as the satellite images.
It also denies the inspectors a second inspection in el-Kibar and access to the debris of the bombed building.
But Israel is also silent.
"We will never comment on this," Premier Ehud Olmert, acting at the time of the attack.
Since June 2008, for example, the investigation of the case has been unsuccessful and "is increasingly developing into a serious credibility problem for the authority," as a senior Western diplomat admits at the IAEA headquarters in Vienna - after all, a serious violation of the Non-Proliferation Treaty, over which the IAEA monitors.
As a key to progress in reconnaissance, the IAEA apparently sees three other facilities that are supposed to be "in functional connection" with the presumed reactor - and unlike it still exists.
So far, the inspectors have neither disclosed which attachments are involved, nor what function they have or what makes them appear suspicious.
According to information from the Süddeutsche Zeitung, at the IAEA, they are internally referred to Marj as-Sultan, Masyaf and Iskandariayah, again derived from nearby cities.
The independent Institute for Science and International Security in Washington (Isis) has identified these locations and located on satellite images a building complex near Masyaf, which is most likely one of the suspicious objects.
It may be located in or directly next to a military base - precisely with this justification Syria denies the IAEA access to the three places.
In Vienna, it is said that the Syrians feared a fishing expedition - that the IAEA would be sent forward by the U.S. to spy on military installations.
Because the nuclear detectives had become aware of the facilities by a tip from the U.S. intelligence agencies.
The IAEA has indications, including satellite photos that material has been transported back and forth between the three locations and the cuboid at the Euphrates.
A senior Western diplomat in Vienna said at least one of the facilities could be "related to fuel production."
However, there is “no evidence of sensitive activities”, i.e. uranium enrichment or refurbishment; processes that are necessary to build nuclear weapons.
The type of reactor is operated with uranium, which is not enriched.
But where the uranium should have come from and where the plutonium for the bomb should have been severed later, there are plausible assumptions on these central questions.
It is also unclear whether debris, which could recover valuable traces, was stored in the three locations.
As if the hundreds of lorry loads had disappeared from the ground, no one wants to have information about where the rubble has remained.
Located just a few kilometers from el-Kibar on the other side of the Euphrates, al-Tibni's salt mine, which Syria examined for its suitability as a storage facility for radioactive waste in 1998, is considered unlikely by the IAEA.
Former IAEA Inspector Robert Kelley recently linked them to the reactor in an analysis of satellite images for Jane's Intelligence Review magazine.
Footage from orbit, however, shows that Syria carried out earthworks at the three suspicious locations and removed large containers after the IAEA demanded access.
This can be a coincidence, but has hardly helped to clear up suspicions.
In addition, diplomat circles, the IAEA has spent several months to develop a plan for inspections there, which should allow Syria to maintain any military secrets - the effort alone shows how great the interest of the inspectors is.
However, Damascus also ironed out this proposal.
Western diplomats in Vienna had appeased a year ago that the reactor was destroyed, so the matter did not have a high urgency, for example U.S. Ambassador Glyn Davies has for a few months brought so-called special inspections into play, one of the sharpest means that the Viennese nuclear authority has to enforce controls.
IAEA chief Yukiya Amano, who would have to propose these inspections, has already spoken of "an option I'm open for in the future," but still hesitates.
There is a high risk in this way: On the one hand, it requires a majority in the Board of Governors of the IAEA, and some of the 35 countries represented there are anything but eager for escalation.
On the other hand, as one diplomat points out: "What if they don't find anything in the end?"
Love in times of war
Since Angelina Jolie started working on her nameless directorial debut in the summer, there has been trouble.
She wrote the screenplay herself, a love story between a woman from Bosnia and a Serbian man.
You don't know much more about it - but that's enough for restlessness.
Rumour persists that the woman, a Muslim, falls in love with her Serbian rapist during the Yugoslav war.
Bosnian women's associations immediately protested, whereupon some shooting days planned in Sarajevo were moved to Hungary at short notice.
Meanwhile, the film is filmed in Hungary and in post-production, but it is still being protested in Bosnia.
Bakira Hasecic of the organization of female war victims wrote about the UN refugee agency UNHCR to Jolie and demanded a meeting with the actress, a Bosnian newspaper has now reported - Jolie is herself an ambassador of the UN refugee agency.
She isn't diplomatically - she already offered the women a meeting in Hungary weeks ago, but they rejected it.
The meeting must take place in Bosnia, Hasecic said.
There were no love stories in rape camps, and Jolie’s attitude towards the victims told her enough about the script.
Her organization, according to the U.N. Refugee Agency, has recommended dismiss Jolie as an ambassador, the Guardian reports.
But what exactly did Jolie write for scenes?
The Bosnian Ministry of Culture, which goes to read her script, has granted a filming permit for Sarajevo after some back-and-forth - it's not entirely clear why Jolie didn't come anyway.
The young author herself asserts that she does not pursue political intentions with her film, has worked with a lot of effort into the Yugoslavia conflict, wants to show the terrible consequences of the war and give a voice to all sides involved.
However, it remains vague in terms of content.
A second synopsis of the film circulates on the net, which then sounds quite different: The lovers knew each other even before the outbreak of war and meet again when she, a Bosnian Muslim woman, is trafficked to a Serbian camp, in which he works as a guard.
Which sounds considerably different from the variant "rape victims have a crush on torturers".
That Jolie's film actually begins even before the war - that much is certain.
The pressure on them, Jolie said, was "unfair and based on misinformation."
Now only the hackers of Hollywood can give her peace: By illegally putting the script, like so many before, on the net.
Nothing like getting out of here
This evening, it’s finally going well for the Irish.
There's no trace of sadness.
For this, the home team dominates its guests from Sweden, Finland and the USA too much.
The fans jost for joy with crashingly loud music, they stomp with their feet.
The Bailey pub in southern Ireland's Cork quakes because the players throw table tennis balls over a three-metre distance into beer cups - and hit.
"Bierpong" is called the bizarre spectacle, to win 500 euros victory bonus plus airline tickets to Las Vegas for the beer pong world championships in January.
Cian Tanner is a quiet guy; this shrill drinking game and the restrained computer scientist, that doesn't really fit together.
And yet the 26-year-old is trying to amuse himself in distracting himself from the drilling question of what the future will bring.
On his plan is not game fun in Las Vegas, he will hold job interviews in New Zealand in January.
"I have a queasy feeling about it," he says.
“It’s so far away from home and I don’t have a job there yet.”
After all: his girlfriend is already waiting for him in Auckland - "she had jerky, shruck her desired job as an event manager, maybe a good sign," says the young man, smiling.
Because of poor prospects at home, two of Tanner's three siblings have already searched for their fortunes abroad.
His sister Yvonne, a social worker, is in Sydny, his brother Colin now has a job as an IT adviser in London.
The boys are leaving: As with the Tanners, it looks on the island in many families.
100,000 Irish people will probably leave the country in the near future, experts from the economic and social research institute ESRI in Dublin predict.
By 2015, they even expect 200,000 emigrants.
For the small country with only 4.5 million inhabitants a heavy loss.
If the forecasts are true, an emigration wave is comparable to that that Ireland suffered from in the 1980s.
After a decade of the upswing, which brought great prosperity for the Irians, tristesse is now spreading again.
The economic crisis unsettled above all the young, well-educated Irish people, for whom it has only been uphill so far.
Job loss and social decline seemed absurd in the boom years.
Everyone was needed, there was money in abundance.
In the meantime, however, three out of ten men under thirties are without work.
In the Irish Times, there are many letters from young expatriates complaining: "Everything is going down in Ireland."
"There should be an uprising against politicians and bankers - but whoever can, goes away and our country loses an entire generation with it."
“The last one please turn off the light.”
Jurassic student Leanne Caulfield, president of Ireland's Student Union, sees a bleak future raising because the government is only eye-catching and no longer offering young people any prospects for the future.
"They're reducing minimum wages, but they don't have a strategy on how to create new jobs," says the 20-year-old.
She observes her fellow students shifting from one unpaid internship to the next.
Her biggest concern, she says, is having to emigrate herself in four years.
“This thought is sad and depressing.”
Men like Stephen McLarnon are trying to fill the issue positively.
McLarnon organizes the "WorkingAbroadExpo", a trade fair that offers an all-round service on the subject of work abroad.
"For young professionals, working abroad is a great opportunity to take the first steps on the career ladder," says the manager.
On its website, photos show surfers in Australia and snowboarders in Canada.
The message is clear: working abroad is not a galley service, but also brings freedom and fun.
Seán O'Donovan has known for half a year what it feels like to build something elsewhere.
Because there was nothing more to do for the 29-year-old engineer in Ireland, he is now working with 15 Irish colleagues on construction in Germany.
In Gelsenkirchen, they're pulling up a fashion house right now.
In January we will continue to Hanover or Berlin, to the next project.
O'Donovan rarely sees his homelady in the meantime, because he makes extra shifts in order to be able to pay off the loan for his house at home.
"The situation is already a bit crazy," he says as he steps across the threshold of his house for the first time after a long time.
“I make money in Germany for a house in Ireland where I’m almost never again.”
He laughs somewhat embarrassed and walks through chilled-out rooms.
The small house stands in a fine residential area with well-kept front gardens, large, expensive cars are parked in the driveways.
Together with a sister, O'Donovan bought the house four years ago for 380,000 euros - 100 per cent credit-financed.
"It's been crazy times, there's been simple money without a lot of questions," he says.
Is he afraid it could go wrong with the loan?
O'Donovan ponders for a while, pours hot tea and says: "I don't have children that I have to feed through and I'm young; even if everything goes wrong, I can start all over again."
The civil engineer doesn't like the lamentation that he often hears at the moment.
"No one has to go hungry - our situation is by no means as dramatic as in earlier times here on the island," he says.
“Working abroad for a while, that’s not the worst thing that can happen – maybe that makes us feel like Irish people.”
He laughs and then tells of his father, who went to England in the last major economic crisis.
“After seven years he was back and life went on in Ireland, quite simply.”
Cian Tanner likes this thought.
To leave the future open and just see how it goes with life elsewhere.
A few years of work in London he has already done and why shouldn’t things go well in New Zealand for him and his girlfriend?
But there is one thing that worries him these days: the celebration for his 27th birthday on Friday in Dublin.
He's afraid it could degenerate into a farewell party.
"Don't have too many emotions," he says, drinking a big sip of his beer.
“Goodbye, that sounds scary.”
"Let's call it a walk-away party, quite loosely, with some friends, a few drinks, a few games."
Democatric routine
The democracy movement in Latin America is becominging stronger, just like the support of private companies.
Crime is a bigger problem than unemployment.
In many parts of the region, Brazil's influence is greater than that of the United States.
These results were the Latinobarómetro survey conducted in 18 countries and published exclusively in The Economist.
This survey has been conducted on a regular basis since 1999 and thus shows the change of opinion in this region.
Two facts are particularly catching the eye in this year's September and October survey.
First of all, the good mood in Latin America
and second, the growing interest in democracy and its most important institutions.
In numerous countries on the South American Pacific coast, the democratic movement has increased sharply (see Table 1).
In Peru, for example, where the economy has been growing by about 6% annually since 2002, the number of democracy advocates rose from 40% in 2005 to 61% this year.
Even in Mexico, where the economy has been recovering since last year's sharp slump, such a trend can be seen.
Yet only half of respondents in Mexico and Brazil, the region's most populous countries, are convinced Democrats, which lowers the regional average (see chart 2).
Approximately 44% of respondents stated that they are satisfied with their country's democratic practice. That's as many as last year, but 25% more than in 2001 (Diagram 3).
About 34% trust their country's Congress and 45% of their government. Both values correspond to those of last year, but show a significant increase from 2003 (17% and 24%, respectively).
A decade ago, Latin America's economic crises undermined efforts for more democracy.
That continued even during the Great Depression is possibly due to the fact that Latin America's countries only experienced a brief period of recession and recovered quickly.
In addition, the poors have been absorbed by a stronger social network.
(According to reports by the U.N. Economic Commission for Latin America this week this week, the poverty rate rose only marginally in 2009 and has fallen by 32% again this year.)
However, there are strong differences in mood from country to country (see diagram 4).
Brazil's strong economy and the popularity of outgoining President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva is reflected in a widespread thinking of progress.
In Mexico and Central America, where the recession was more noticeable and violent crimes have increased, this is less the case.
Only for the second time since the beginning of the survey, many respondents in Latin America see a bigger problem in crime in their country than in unemployment (see diagram 5).
Approximately 31% say that either themselves or a family member were the victims of a crime in the last year. However, compared to 38% in the last year, reaching the lowest level since 1995.
The increasing economic performance is reflected in the stronger support of the private and market economies: about 71% of the respondents nationally stated that the private sector is indispensable for development, compared to 56% in 2007 (see diagram 6).
Approximately 58% declared that only the market economy can bring progress to their country, compared to 47% last year.
Interestingly, 62% of respondents in Venezuela have maintained this view (and 80% are in favour of a private sector), although their left-wing president, Hugo Chávez, nationalized many private companies.
Brazil has the largest weight in the region, according to 19% of respondents (18% last year), followed by the United States (9%, unchanged since last year) and Venezuela (9% compared with 11% last year).
Respondents in Mexico and most countries in Central America, on the other hand, see the most influential country in the U.S., with this role being taken over in the Dominican Republic and Nicaragua by Venezuela.
An Electoral Floppy
No one was expecting the November 28 elections in Haiti to be perfect.
But the facts far exceeded the fears of election workers rushing to the battered country for help.
Overcrowded ballot boxes, votes cast several times, ballot papers in the gutter, destroyed polling stations and intimidations were only part of the problems.
Polling stations opened with hours of delay.
Many Haitians, many of whom had become homeless as a result of the January earthquake, roamed in search of a choice.
Some finally gave up completely.
A dozen presidential candidates, including some suspected front-runners, demanded the lifting of election results due to an alleged fraud.
There have been partially violent protests throughout the country.
Nevertheless, Edmond Mulet, the head of the U.N. mission in Haiti, described the election "as quite good in many respects."
According to a cautious statement by observers from the Organization of American States and the Caribbean Community, "these irregularities, as serious as they may not necessarily invalidate the process."
In other words, the show goes on.
The electoral authority, which is allegedly committed to unpopular outgoing President René Préval and his candidate, Jude Célestin, will announce the preliminary results on December 7.
The expected run-off election is scheduled for January.
There's still hope in the election.
The two leading opposition candidates, the rap musician Michel Martelly and the former first-lady Mirlande Manigat, have withdrawn their initial invalidity lawsuit.
If Jude Célestin, who finds little or no approval among the voters, declares from the electoral authority the winner of the run-off election, this could lead to problems.
Due to the cholera epidemic and the fact that more than 1 million of earthquake victims still live in tents, a low turnout was feared.
On Election Day, many Haitians were prevented from voting by a lack of organization.
Shortly before the start of the election, about 250,000 ID cards, which are needed for the election, had not yet been distributed.
There's a lot at stake.
Donor countries see a legitimate, recognized government as an important step in rebuilding Haiti.
The Haitians themselves are of the same opinion.
Secretly into the middle
In the weeks after your husband's sudden death, Argentina's president, Cristina Fernández, experienced a wave of sympathy pushing up her popularity rate by 20 points to over 50%. That's the highest figure since her election in 2007.
Whether this will help her to re-elect next year, however, depends on whether the visibly grieving Cristina Fernández is more willing to compromise than her late husband, her predecessor and political partner Néstor Kirchner.
The signs of this aren't unequivocal.
Néstor Kirchner, who steered economic policy even after his wife's election, frequently drew up budget plans that didn't fully take into account tax revenues, economic growth and inflation.
This allowed the presidential couple to spend the extra proceeds at their own discretion.
On paper, the Fernández administration lost a majority in Congress last year.
However, the opposition is divided and Kirchner's death following a heart attack on October 27 led to further quarrels.
Some regime critics from the ranks of the ruling Peronists stood behind Cristina Fernández.
However, after chaotic sessions (at which one parliamentarian punched another in the face and others accused the government of bribery), Congress failed to pass the budget for 2011.
"I'm not going to discuss it," the president said.
The 2010 budget now also applies to the next year, giving the president even more opportunity to spend money at her own discretion.
The economy is running hot: it is likely to grow by up to 9% this year.
The official rate of inflation currently stands at 11.1%, yet numerous private estimates - and wage demands of the unions - are driving the value to almost 30%.
Kirchner, who took office in 2003 when Argentina was shaken by economic crises, declared growth and poverty mitigation his main goals, rather than inflation.
He refused to cooperate with the IMF, harass the private sector, and purged the INDEC National Institute of Statistics and census, which then garnished the official inflation statistics.
Can Fernández take a more moderate path now?
She has called on the private sector and the union leaders for talks.
And according to official statements, wage increases are to be limited to 18% next year.
The high world market prices for Argentina's agricultural products have given a strong boost to the economy.
However, inflation undermined the spurt of growth through a major devaluation in 2002.
Cristina Fernández may want to rejoin the rest of the world with Argentina financially for this reason.
Earlier this year, the government reached an agreement with securities holders who had rejected the country's previous debt restructuring in 2001.
Now Fernández seems to be pushing for a deal withing donors from the Paris Club, to whom she owes about $6.7bn.
The Kirchners had always rejected a review by the IMF, as is customary elsewhere.
However, last month, Economy Minister Amado Boudou announced that the fund will provide "technical support" in creating a new national consumer price index. It looks like this would be the first step towards purging the INDEC.
It is not known whether Fernández will be politically active even without the support of her husband after the end of her term.
If so, it needs the support of the middle class, which has largely had enough of Kirchnerism.
When Argentinians return from their summer vacation in March, the wave of sympathy that has been brought to her will have ebbed.
If she wants to win another election, she has to decide whether to quietly overturn many of her husband's political decisions.
Weak applause for China
Taiwan's voters are slowly approaching a closer connection to the mainland.
What does Taiwan think of its big brotare beyond the strait?
On November 27, more than 7.5 million voters, i.e. 70% of all eligible voters, set out to elect mayors and councillors in the five boroughs. This is seen as a broad assessment by President Ma Ying-jeou.
By establishing groundbreaking business relations, including a partial free trade agreement, Ma himself is a symbol of relations with the mainland. His policy has permanently changed the relationship with the Chinese motherland for the first time since 1949.
Ma has passed the exam, but only narrowly.
Although his Kuomintang party (KMT) represents three of the five mayors, it did not receive a vote majority.
Instead, the Democratic Progressive Party (DPP), which advocates greater independence of Taiwan, received nearly 50% of the votes, the KMT received 45%.
In a presidential election, this would have meant problems for Ma.
Since his election two years ago, his party's share of votes has fallen by about 8%.
For the first time, the DPP even moved into the city councils together with the KMT.
During the election, Taiwan's usual events such as truck convoys with riot makers, flag-waving mass parades and only sparsely clothed dancers occurred.
There was even the obligatory, mysterious assassination attempt.
Sean Lien, the son of Lien Chan and honorary chairman of the KMT, gave a speech to a KMT candidate on the eve of the election.
when a man from the crowd shot into his face, Lien was wounded and a bystanding spectator was killed.
Bizarrely, it's not clear whether the shooter wanted to kill Lien or settle a dispute with the candidate.
Regardless, according to Tsai Ing-wen, the leader of the DPP, has affected the election and put the DPP in a bad light, giving the KMT more votes.
Possibly China was the biggest winner of the campaign.
Remarkably, relations to the mainland were not addressed.
Instead, the candidates debated plans for social housing and job creation.
The Taiwanese love their factual independence and reject China's military threat.
For this reason, the KMT doesn't like to put its relations with the government in Beijing in the foreground.
But this time the DPP also avoided stoking anti-Chinese sentiment too much.
She responded to the new awareness that Taiwan's economy can't survive without China.
For its part, it would like to be confirmed that the majority of Taiwanese are endorsing its China policy.
The government in Beijing will continue to pursue its strategy of luring Taiwan back into the community with economic incentivities.
China immediately sent a negotiator to Taipei to prepare a summit on investment security and medical cooperation.
Certainly it also wants political integration, but the most important thing first.
It prefers to wait until after the 2012 presidential election.
The DPP has reinvented itself by now.
Two years ago, she was severely weakened: former party leader Chen Shui-bian was imprisoned for corruption.
During his presidency from 2000 to 2008, he vehemently advocated absolute independence.
But under Tai’s regency, a new generation of moderate politicians developed and the DPP is fighting for the voices of professionals and the youth.
Tsai found great approval as the DPP's presidential nominee, like Su Tseng-chang under Chen.
Her party is now doing everything possible to show that she, too, is capable of building close ties with Beijing.
Tsai is planning a think tank as a point of contact between the DPP and China. However, this presupposes non-prejudiced discussions.
Tsai publicly stated that her party offered a "trustworthy alternative" to Ma's hasty rapprochement with China.
Chen seems very upset about this change.
From his cell, he declared that the DPP's candidates had done better to insisting on Taiwan's independence.
He even urged pragmatist Su to renounce the presidential nomination.
Even without Chen's interference, the DPP struggles to choose its possible candidate.
The KMT will have to undergo its own self-analysis.
Ma's values are bad, despite rapid economic growth; the KMT candidates wanted to run their election campaign without him.
The main reasons for his unpopularity seem to be unemployment and the ever-increasing scissors between rich and poor.
According to a KMT spokesperson, he will reshape his cabinet as well as the local facilities.
The DPP's strong demeanour suggests that Taiwan advocates reapproximation to a limited extent.
At the same time, most Taiwanese see economic cooperation with China as inevitable.
According to Antonio Chiang, a prominent columnist, "there's already no turning back."
Grammy nominations: Jazz singer Esperanza Spalding has surprisingly been honoured in the Newcomer of the Year category
If you're performing at the request of a president, people will notice.
President Obama himself asked Esperanza Spalding to perform at the 2009 Nobel Prize ceremony. Now, the 26-year-old jazz bassist and singer has been surprisingly nominated for this year's Grammy in the Newcomer of the Year category.
Given the fact that Spalding has been an acclaimed star in the jazz scene since her album "Esperanza" in 2008, the nomination appears a bit unusual, according to the Grammy rules, according to which an artist may have released at most three albums before the release of the album with which he became known in the general public.
Their 2010 ambitious album, "Chamber Music Society," is certainly a step forward. And with her buoyant voice and a good sense of pop, funk and Latin music, you can calmly compare Spalding to Norah Jones in 2003, whose great success comes closest in 2003 as a jazz interpreter in this category of Spalding's nomination.
Furthermore, the album "Historicity" by the Vijay Iyer Trio was nominated in the category of instrumental albums. This darling of the critics experienced a huge success as a result.
Iyer's competitors include "Moody 4B," veteran saxophonist James Moody and "Positootly," keyboardist John Beasley.
Trombone Shorty, a pracious favorite from New Orleans and frequent guest in HBO's "Treme," has been honored in the contemporary jazz category, which is usually more likely to include albums by Stanley Clarke, John McLoughlin and Jeff Lorber.
The Secret Societ of the wild steampump musician Darcy James Argue received well-deserved attention in the category. He thus competes against the Billy Childs Emsemble with his chamber jazz and the adventurous Dave Holland and his octet, whose life-ablum "Pathways" is a good example for a bassist as a democratic bandleader.
Grammy nominations: Michael Daugherty, Steve Mackey lead in the classics categories
The classic nominations for the 53rd Grammy Award include a colourful mix of musicians and composers from all over the world.
The list is led by Michael Daugherty's "Deux Ex Machina" for piano and orchestra with five voices, and Steve Mackey's "Dreamhouse," an eclectic orchestral work, with three voices.
Among the nominees with connections to Southern California is the Estonian composer Arvo Pärt with his Symphony No. 4 "Los Angeles". This piece was commissioned by the Los Angeles Philharmonic and premiered at the Walt Disney Concert Hall in 2009.
L.A.-based composer Avner Dorman has also been nominated for his mandolin concert.
Classical nominations are divided into 13 categories, from orchestra, opera, choir to solo recordings.
Five artists have been nominated in the category of classical music producers this year - Blanton Alspaugh, David Frost, Tim Handley, Marina and Victor Ledin and James Mallinson.
Among the nominated classical music albums are Bruckner's Symphonies No. 3 and 4 by the Royal Concertgebouw Orchestra; Daugherty's "Deus Ex Machina" and "Metropolis Symphony" played by the Nashville Symphony Orchestra; Steven Mackey's "Dreamhouse" by the Boston Modern Orchestra Project and Synergy Vocals; "Sacrificium - the Art of the Castrati" performed by various artists; and Verdi's
In the opera category, Berg's "Lulu of the Orchestra of the Royal Opera House; Adolf Hasse's "Marc Antonio e Cleopatra" by Ars Lyrica Houston; Kaija Saariaho's "L'Amour de Loin" by the German Symphony Orchestra Berlin; Rodion Shchedrin's "The Enchanted Wanderer" by the orchestra and choir of the Mariinsky Theater; and Arthur Sullivan's "Ivan"
Pärt for Symphony No. 4, Daugherty for "Deus Ex Machina," Hans Werner Henze for "Appassionatamente Plus," Magnus Lindberg for "Graffiti" and Shchedrin for "The Enchanted Wanderer" were nominated for a Grammy.
Pärt's Symphony #4 premiered and recorded by the L.A. Philharmonic on January 4 at the Disney Hall under the direction of Esa-Pekka Salonen.
The approximately 30-minute piece is titled "Los Angeles" and is dedicated to the former oil magnate Mikhail Khodorkovsky, who is imprisoned in Russia.
Dorman's "Mondoline Concerto" received a nomination in the instrumental solo category with orchestra.
The composer from L.A. stated that he had written the 15-minute piece specifically for the mandolin artist Avi Avital, who performed the concert with the New-York Metropolis Ensemble.
"I've used sounds from the Middle East and numerous North African tones and rhythms," Dorman explained in a telephone interview.
Conductor Pierre Boulez, 85, received a nomination in the orchestral performance category for a recording of Stravinsky's works with the Chicago Symphony Orchestra under his direction.
Pianists Nelson Freire and Marc-André Hamelin, organist Paul Jacobs, violinist Julia Fischer and harfinist Sarah Schuster Ericsson were nominated as instrumental soloists.
Only women have been nominated this year, including Anne Sofie von Otter, Cecilia Bartoli, Lucia Duchonová, Vivica Genaux and Measha Brueggergosman.
In the classical crossover category, soprano Jessye Norman was nominated for her recording "Roots - My Life, My Song".
Mosque in Temecula wins approval of planning committee
The construction of a mosque in Temecula founded after a five-hour, often-heated public hearing, the local planning committee's unanimous commitment.
Critics accused the project of promoting both terrorism and traffic congestion.
However, according to a statement from the commission on Wednesday evening, the new religious center complies with local regulations and will become an aesthetic eye-catcher.
"The project is supported by a lot of good people - by Christians, Jews, Bahais, and many others," Hadi Nael, chairman of Temecula Valley's Islamic Center, declared Thursday morning.
“As an American, that makes me proud.”
"It shows that justice prevails."
"There are few opponents," he added.
“They’re just very loud.”
"There are wonderful people living in this valley."
The meeting began at 18:00 and the discussions for and against the construction of the center lasted almost until midnight, with voices on both sides being fairly evenly distributed, according to Press-Enterprise.
The debate continued on the newspaper's website.
"There's no America anymore," one comment read.
"It's a different planet of apes, and the Statue of Liberty lies dead in the sand."
“America is at an end.”
Planned Islamic centers have also led to protests in other parts of the country, e.g. near Ground Zero, the scene of terrorist attacks on the city of New York on September 11, 2001.
In the Temecula Valley, Imam Mahmoud Harmoush has called for nonsensity.
His community, with about 135 families, has lived peacefully in Riverside County for years, but they wanted a larger and better place than the warehouse they rented together with a manufacturer of water filtration systems.
The new, approximately 2,300 sqm mosque in Mediterranean style should have two minarets and become about 13m high.
It is to be built on the approximately 17,500 m2 plot on Nicolas Road near the Chaparral High School.
Opponents can still object to the city council against the project.
Arsenic-resistant microbe could enable new life forms on Earth or elsewhere
The discovery of an alien bacterial species that can live on arsenic, among other things, opens up new possibilities for new forms of life on earth and elsewhere.
Although scientists discovered this unusual bacterium here on Earth, in their view it is a sign that a life is also possible without the elements previously considered vital.
“This organism has two options.”
“He needs either phosphorus or arsenic to grow.”
"This makes it something very special, even though it's almost a kind of 'foreign' way of life," explained Paul C.W. Davies of Arizona State University and co-author of the report, which appears Thursday in the online edition of the journal Science.
Until now, six main elements have been considered vital - carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, oxygen, phosphorus and sulphur.
However, as the scientists found out, the bacterium in California's Mono Lake grows even further when it replaces phosphorus with arsenic.
"One wonders what else is possible," said co-author Ariel D. Anbar of Arizona State University.
The discovery is an important step in the search for extraterrestrial life, as scientists must recognize life and its forms, according to Anbar.
The study looks at microbes that have been found on Earth.
The announcement of a press conference in which no details of the discovery were announced gave rise to widespread speculation on the Internet that the report could deal with the discovery of extraterestrial life.
That's not the case.
The discovery "shows that in other planetary systems organisms can initiate biochemical processes with the help of other elements and that the 'standard elements', which in our opinion are absolutely vital, are not necessarily strictly predetermined," explained Charles Cockell, professor at the Planetary and Space Sciences Research Institute of the Open University of Milton Keynes in the United Kingdom.
Cockel wasn't part of the research team.
"This work is a novelty, because it shows that, in basic biochemical processes and structures, one element can be replaced by another," Cockell added.
This discovery wasn't accidental.
Felisa Wolfe-Simon of NASA's Astrobiological Institute, who led the study, chose Mono Lake because of the high arsenic content.
Since arsenic and phosphorus resemble each other chemically, she assumed that microbes exposed to both could potentially replace one element with the other.
“The toxicity of arsenic rests above all because it behaves chemically similar to phosphorus.”
“This makes it difficult for organisms to distinguish one element from the other.”
"However, the difference is so large that arsenic acts differently than phosphorus. It enters our biochemical system, where it smears virtually everything," explains Anbar.
The researchers subjected the bacterium GFAJ-1 to a rising arsenic concentration. It adapted and grew.
Although the microbe grows better in phosphorus, the fact that it can also survive in arsenic shows that an arsenic-based life form can occur naturally, either on Earth or on another planet or moon on which arsenic occurs more frequently.
According to Jamie S. Foster, lecturer in microbiology at the University of Florida, the idea that phosphorus can be replaced by arsenic isn't new yet.
Arsenic occurred more frequently in the early Earth years, she says, that's why science has assumed that early life forms used it.
"This gives reason to the assumption that life may also have evolved differently than in the early Earth Age," according to Forster, who was not a member of Wolfe-Simon's research team.
The research work has been supported by NASA, the Department of Energy and the National Health Authorities.
Remaining stolen exhibition dogs found in Compton
The remaining two of the four exhibition dogs stolen in front of a Bellflower motel were found Thursday's Los Angeles County Sheriff's statement.
The four exhibition dogs - two Akitas and two Pembroke Welsh Corgis - disappeared on Tuesday when unidentifiers stole the 2006 Chevrolet Express van in which the animals were housed outside a Bellflower Motel 6 at night, according to authorities statements.
Two of the dogs were spotted Wednesday afternoon, and the last two on Wednesday evening, explains Steve Whitmore, a Los Angeles County Sheriff's Department spokesman.
"The good news is that the dogs are well up," Whitmore said.
“They’re in good condition.”
“They have been returned to their rightful owners.”
Whitmore did not give precise details of the dog's site nor the circumstances that led to their discovery.
“The investigation is still ongoing.”
"We don't want to say more about that," Whitmore explains.
"We're chasing some tracks."
The dog owners, Kristina Rickard and David Peek, were not available for information on Thursday.
They received their two dogs on Wednesday afternoon at a county animal shelter in Downey.
Sheriff's representatives found the van and the two dogs at different locations in Compton.
The Akitas - Trace, a 3-year-old male, and Didi, a 10-month-old bitch - were uninjured, according to authorities.
Yet the Corgis Bunny and Peter, which Rickard and Peek wanted to present at the competition this week on behalf of their Japanese owners, remained missing until Wednesday evening.
Peek and Rickard, who live in Redding, were in the city this weekend because the AKC/Eukanuba National Championship in Long Beach because Trace was supposed to participate in the competition.
As Peek told the Times on Wednesday, the other dogs were scheduled to take part in other competitions on the days leading to the AKC/Eukanuba show.
Southern California is a stop on the couple's near-month-month tour from dog show.
On Monday night, Peek and Rickard checked in at the motel.
They frequently stay in motels and park their van in front of their room window.
They took three other dogs - a boxer puppy, a Parson Russell terrier and a Brittany - into their motel room.
The thick-wrisped Akitas and Corgis remained in the van because it was cooler there.
They left the window a crevice wide open so that the dogs had fresh air.
Peek and Rickard reported their car as stolen at 6:00 a.m. on Tuesday.
Peek promised a finder's wage to anyone who could give an indication of the corgis. The animals were implanted microchips for identification.
On Thursday, it was not yet clear whether a finder's wage would be paid.
The lion roars again after judicial approval of MGM restructuring
After 18-month turbulences and uncertainties, MGM finally got the green light for a new start.
A New-York bankruptcy judge on Thursday officially blessed the "prepared" credible plan to repay the $5bn debt and pave the way for the 86-year-old entertainment company for new films and television shows.
According to Thursday's statements, the plan meets the requirements of U.S. bankruptcy law, so nothing stands in the way of the reboot of MGM under the direction of Gary Barber and Roger Birnbaum, the founders of Spyglass Entertainment. Barber and Birnbaum will preside over the somewhat slimmed-down studio as chief executive.
The number of employees is reduced from more than 400 to about 320.
For MGM, Chapter 11 will actually not come into effect until JPMorgan Chase has completed a $500 million planned bond to finance the new MGM productions.
At the moment, a consortium of the investment bank, which itself contributes $75 million, manages the company. The financing is expected to stand by mid-December.
The studio's restructuring began in May 2009 with the order to the investment bank Moelis & Co, the company's management, which struggled with interest payments of $300 million/year to help solve its financial problems.
In August, managing director Harry Sloan was replaced by Stephen Cooper, a former CEO at Enron and a restructuring expert, who became known for his work at Krispy Kreme Doughnuts.
Moelis & Cooper's efforts to solve MGM's financial worries and pay out the studio's more than 140 believers lasted longer than expected.
After months of discussions, the parties decided at the end of 2009 to offer the company for purchase.
Yet the auction attracted few bidders and none wanted to pay the $2 billion demanded by creditors.
In the spring of this year, it all started over when the MGM team began the restructuring plan, which after months of debates and internal disputes was finally signed by creditors at the end of October.
If MGM has officially weathered bankruptcy over the next few weeks, Moelis and Cooper will receive a multi-million-dollar bonus for their work.
Moelis & Co., which has already received $1.2 million, is $9.5 million.
Cooper, who was paid $375,000 monthly to manage the company, will receive a "success bonus" of $4 million.
Barber and Birnbaum are planning to resume production at MGM. This year, the studio released only one film and created an annual plan with seven or eight films scheduled to hit theaters in early 2012.
In addition, they are planning two films after "The Little Hobbit" in co-production with Warner Bros.
The studio needs a separate bond of between $265 million to $275 million for its financial stake in these two films, which are projected to be created under the direction of Peter Jackson.
Investor Carl Icahn, who funded about 18% of MGM's loan, attempted a merger of the studio with Lions Gate Entertainment, whose largest shareholder he is.
However, the recent negotiations between the two companies did not lead to any result.
Tech Toys keeps your gift budget in balance
This year's favorite among gifts is the iPad, possibly followed by a Microsoft Kinect video console. Both gimmicks can rip a sizeable hole into your gift budget.
An iPad costs as much as five BMX bikes for $99, while the price for a Kinect game equals about 10 classic skipping ropes or about 48 cans of Play-Doh.
But you can save your gift budget.
Recently, in search of three electronic toy toys under $80, I visited three national toy retailers - a Wal-Mart, a Toys "R" Us and a BJ's Wholesale Club.
I immediately realized that Moore's law had come into effect. This year the technology toys are more powerful and at the same time cost slightly less than last year.
And although fewer new toys with their own website are appearing, there is a wider range in the most well-knowledgeable of this kind, Webkinz and Club Penguin.
There are more and more button cell-powered toys coming onto the market, and many contain rechargeable batteries.
Here are some recommendations.
(Unless stated otherwise, all prices from Toys are "R" Us.)
Sing-a-ma-jigs (Mattel, $13, for children from 3 years old) are plush figures, as big as guinea pigs, who sing a note when you press on their belly.
Once pressing corresponds to a note, by pressing several times you can play a song.
Several Sing-a-ma-jigs sing in harmony.
Attention: If you have started pressing once, you will not be able to stop.
For a combination of sandy and dance, the Dance Star Mickey (Fisher-Price $60, for kids from 2) is the best choice.
Based on Elmo Live technology developed last year, the animatronic puppet offers interplays such as 'Simon says', and thanks to the new hinge feet with wheel blocking, it dominates the moon walk.
Children who tested the device use Mickey as a dance instructor, but their parents complained about the lack of volume control.
If you're a child of the '80s, you're certainly reminiscencing Alphie, one of the first computerized educational toys.
Now he's back (Hasbro/Playskool, $40, for children from 3), with a whole bunch of question cards with which a car ride can become a lesson, with a know-it-all with auto-corrective function as a teacher.
If you don’t want to entrust your child with your smartphone, here are three independent alternatives.
MobiGo by VTech ($60 for children from 3 to 8) is the cheapest and most user-friendly solution, equipped with a qwerty keyboard. However, the games included on it are not as instructive as those on Leapster Explorer ($70, for children from 3 to 12), which is equipped with a new, brighter screen after Leapfrog's first major overhaul of the Leapster series.
None of the versions is compatible with older cassettes.
iXL by Fisher-Price ($90 for children from 3 to 7) uses a system similar to iTunes for the games and eBooks, and thanks to the SD card drive, family photos can also be viewed.
Last but not least there is Scrabble Flash ($30 from Hasbro, for children from 8). The five blocks are no bigger than a matchbox and have their own LED screen.
The game is all about putting the blocks together into words before time runs out. It's great for an improvised spelling competition.
More and more toys have a kind of online connection.
If you go alone according to the offer in the toy shops, Webkinz and Club Penguin are the most important.
To give your child an online animal at Club Penguin, for example, you can buy a Disney Club Penguin 2" Mick 'N Match character package ($5 for children from 6 to 12) that contains a coin with a secret code.
If you enter the code online, your child will receive enough credits for two more parts in the Club Penguin Store.
These can be roller skates or a puffle toy.
The latest head-cell-powered microrobot, Hexbug Nano ($10 for kids from 5) is no bigger than the head of your toothbrush.
The beetle quickly moves on 12 vibrating silicone legs in any direction.
It's fun to send him through a maze of blocks.
And he's withstanding a lot.
Ours fell off the kitchen table and even withstood some time in our dog's mouth.
Police operations in China are becoming gentler
Like a training officer, Tang Shenbin walked across an urban square, checked his nervous subordinates and issued orders in the process with military authority and subdued voice.
He wanted his female members of the Chengguan - China's robust guardians of the urban order, dreached and despised for their arbitrary raids and their penchant for violence - impressing viewers.
"Stand right now!"
“Look straight ahead!”
"Show them," he whispered to them "how pretty girls are like that!"
Four girls, barely outgrown their teenage age, with white gloves and identical olive-green jackets and pants stood tight.
Four pairs of black pumps stood in a straight row.
Four neat hats sat perfectly on the hair held with blue-and-and-white striped bows.
"To me, they look on average," notes Mr Tang disdainfully.
“Models are pretty.”
Several governments have already attempted to polish the image of China's city inspectors.
In one city, all new applicants must have a university degree.
Guangdong province introduced a bright blue uniform instead of the grey-green.
Wuhan in central China replaced violence with low-rigidity: In 2009, 50 officials reportedly circled an improperly erected food truck and stared at the operator until it packed in after half an hour and left the square.
Xindu, a borough in Chengdu with 680,000 inhabitants, wants to fundamentally change the image.
Since 2003, 13 women have been strengthening the urban police strips. They have been specially selected for their appearance, their good figure and their youth.
They are supposed to give a softer, feminine aspect to the gross-sleeping police.
But unfortunately Scarlett Johansson would hardly be able to improve the bad image that the Chinese have of these city squads.
Rightly so, according to the critics.
Unlike the police, these officers are allowed to enforce municipal laws only through fines or other administrative penalties.
Yet the Chinese media routinely show a different picture.
In January 2008, officials in Hubei province beated a spectator to death when they filmed her using his mobile phone as they crushed a protest action against a dump.
Last year, a training manual by Beijing officials was stolen and published on the Internet, describing how offenders can be beaten without bloodshed.
This year, a Shanghai watermelon trader suffered brain injuries after a scuffle involving five officials.
A violent video game that can be downloaded from the Internet is showing Chinese-trained officials during an attack on street vendors.
"Chengguan scares the government," lamented the national newspaper China Daily last year after further controversy regarding tactics.
The newspaper demanded a "truly thorough purge."
Skeptics find the current approach for significantly insufficient.
When the district searched for eight female recruits in October, an editorial in the Beijing Evening News questioned whether the women had real tasks or should only serve as an optical distraction.
It's probably both.
According to the job advertisement, women aged 18 to 22 were searched with good figure and "harmonious facial features."
They should be above-average - over 1.5m or 5 feet, 2 1/2 inches.
The employment relationship ends with the age of 26.
According to the officials, the activity for women over 25 is too physically exhausting.
"The most important thing is their image," an unnamed district official told the quasi-government website Rednet.com.
“Whether the candidates get the job depends first on their outward appearance, such as height, weight, facial features, etc.”
Then come temperament and "inner qualities."
"Female Chengguans are like flower vases," he said, adding, "but beyond that, they have other duties."
Zheng Lihua, deputy head of the city administration of the district, does not wish to comply with this description,
However, it admitted that in China’s size specifications are not uncommodutable in job advertisements for both genders.
The same applies to even facial features.
Whether that equates to good looks is debatable in China.
But people with disabilities or physical disfigures do not need to apply.
"Unfortunately, we can't use lames or hunchbacks here," Mr. Zheng said.
“Their image wouldn’t be good.”
Liu Yi, who patrolies in Baoguang Square near a monastery, is 22, has apple bakes and a well-formed mouth.
She didn't perceive as sexist that so much emphasis is placed on her appearance.
"Do you think I look sexy in this uniform?" she asks with an ironic look.
Her colleague with the dimplines, 21-year-old Xu Yang, comments, "We have the task of representing the city's image."
According to them, they don’t regret the short attitude, as they have more ambitious career plans than simply scaring merchants through the alleys to prevent them from doing business.
Every morning, the patrol deals with about twelve street vendors who try to sell as many buns and tofu dishes as possible on foot or on a bicycle before being chased away.
“Mr. Wang, please get out of here.”
"We've told you this so many times before," explains Ms. Xu to a dealer who had fled on foot and had temporarily abandoned his noodle kitchen attached to a bicycle cart.
According to the officials, the activity is monotonous rather than exhausting.
"It's the same almost every day," explains Huang Jing, 20, who studies sales in leisure.
"All routine."
One reason for this is that the female officials are not allowed to confiscate the goods of the merchants, like their male counterparts.
They can only threaten offenders by reporting them to their male superior.
This is intended to protect them from public hostilities, to which officials in China are often exposed.
This year, hundreds of residents of Kunming, the capital of Yunnan province, rioted after rumors circulated that Chengguan officials had killed a merchant.
More than a dozen police officers and Chenguan officials were injured in the nocturnal incident, 14 government vehicles were knocked over or set on fire.
Xindu has so far been spared from such riots of violence.
However, unrest cannot be ruled out.
Just two blocks away from quiet Baonguang Square where female officials patrolled that morning, over 50 people gathered on a street corner.
Officials had confiscated a motorcycle that, contrary to the regulations on the sidewalk, was repaired instead of a workshop.
The owner shouted Zeter and Mordio.
After 15-minute hesitation, the officers made their way towards their vehicles with a grim face and continued with the motorcycle and its owner.
Li Xuedong, 40, a coordinator of the male patrol, stayed behind. In the process, he flipped his white name tag to remain anonymous.
Like her colleagues, the coordinators - men over 40 - play a purely supporting role.
Yet they have not been trained to present a clean image.
“Sometimes we proceed verbally.”
"Sometimes, however, also physically," Mr. Li explains soberly.
“Most of the time, people start the fight.”
Prosecutor won't accuse Senator Ensign
Prosecutors at the Justice Department have acquitted Senator John Ensign of the accusations raised against him following his affair with a former election worker and his attempt to give her husband a lobby, the senator's lawyers announced Wednesday.
This decision puts an end to the heaviest legal accusation against Senator Ensign, the Republican from Nevada. Until the scandal, which made headlines nationwide and put a damper on his career, he was considered a possible presidential candidate of the Republicans for 2012.
As Senator Ensign's office announced, he was "highly pleased" about the verdict after the one-year investigation.
However, the Senate's ethics committee could still impose a disciplary sentence.
According to Ensign's lawyers, they were informed by the Department of Justice that the investigations against Senator Ensign have been discontinued and that there are no further charges in this matter.
The Justice Department did not want to comment on the case.
The verdict was a surprise to many ethics lawyers outside the Department of Justice. In their opinion, there was sufficient evidence that Ensign disregarded the lobbying ban.
Interviews, emails and other documents first disclosed in the New York Times in October 2009 gave rise to the presumption that Ensign Douglas Hampton, one of his election workers, helped to lucrative assignments to buy his silence about an affaire with Hampton's wife Cynthia.
Cynthia Hampton worked as treasurer during an election campaign by Ensigns.
Ensign contacted some political friends to get Hampton's work. According to the documents and interrogations, he took over Hampton's lobbying work for them.
Since Hampton was still on Ensign's electoral team recently, it wasn't allowed to take on lobbying for his former employer or the entire Senate for a year.
According to ethics experts, the unusual agreement between Ensign and Hampton, who were close friends before the affair, violated the so-called ban on revolving-door lobbycracy.
According to the circumstantial evidence, "it appears to be collusion and the violation of the revolving door statutes," according to Stanley Brand, a Washington lawyer who specializes in defending clients who are in the government's visor.
While the Justice Department officially declined to announce the reasons for the decision in the Ensign case, Mr. Brand and other ethics lawyers said the ministry appeared to be very wary of picking up political indictment after a variety of defeats persecuted by the media with great interest, including last year's decision to drop the indictment against former Senator Ted Stevens for misconduct on the part of the prosecutor.
Prosecutors "are obviously terrifying," said Melanie Sloan, the chairwoman of "Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington" (an organization whose goal is to fight corruption in U.S. parliamentary and government circles), a group of lawyers who have brought various charges against Mr. Ensign to justice.
"This fundamentally means that senior government officials can now assume that the laws do not apply to them," she added.
Paul Coggins, Mr Ensign's main defender, said in an interview that he had received a phone call from a prosecutor on Monday and another the next day in which he was told that the ministry would not file lawsuits against his client.
Mr. Ensign's defense lawyers had cooperateded with prosecutors to convince them that the senator, as his office reiterated again in a statement on Wednesday, "acted in accordance with the law."
Mr Coggins declined to explain more precisely the legal arguments he had put forward to prosecutors.
"I don't think I'm going into the conversation details between the Department of Justice and me, but we're saying we're going to think we've made the right decision," he said.
Last month, the Federal Electoral Commission decided to discontinue the investigation into a separate lawsuit by the ethics group, after which a payment of $9,000 from Mr. Ensign's parents to the Hamptons violated the federal election law.
The Election Commission said there had not been enough evidence that the money had been anything other than a gift.
NV Energy, a large company in Nevada that supported the senator for a long time, was one of the firms where Mr. Hampton found lobbyist work thanks to the help of Mr. Ensign.
An e-mail from December 2008 proves that after the company took Mr. Hampton forward as a lobbyist, Mr. Ensing's chief of staff, John Lopez, had an interview with Mr. Hampton telling him that he was "knocking" at the Home Office because of a cause.
Mr. Ensign had also been scheduled for lunch with Mr. Hampton and another customer from Nevada, Allegiant Air, and asked the transport secretary to arrange a meeting on behalf of the airline after she had hired Mr. Hampton for lobbying.
When information about this affair first leaked to the public last year, Mr. Ensign apologized to his constituents and resigned his position in the Senate, where he had finished fourth in the Republican ranking.
After he hadn't noticed for many months, Mr. Ensign told reporters two weeks agove that he was seeking a third term in the Senate.
Announcing that Mr. Ensign is no longer the target of policy investigations, his press spokeswoman, Jennifer Cooper, the senator hoped that the ethics committee would very quickly follow the example of the Department of Justice and discontinue its investigations.
"Senator Ensign looks forward to continuing its hard work in the service of the people of Nevada," she said.
Cheshire appeal draws attention on Twitter
Half a dozen of them tapped away in the riots in the courtroom.
Every minute, they sent their reports of grisly testimonies via Twitter.
In the end, according to the defense, there were more than 140,000 Twitter messages about the trial surrounding rape and children chained to beds in a house that went up in flames.
On Thursday, Steven J. Haye's closely persecuted trial of Steven J. Hayes, who was charged with killing three members a family from Cheshire, Connecticut, will come to an official ending if the judge's verdict is dropped.
Yet Mr. Haye's lawyers have already filed applications in court that set out reasons for an appeal and which will keep the courts busy for years to come.
One of the most provocative reasons is that the intensive coverage of the trial - also through the extensive use of Twitter by average reporters - there has been a "circus atmosphere" and such a widespread, prompt penetration of inflammatory details, so that the jury had been inadmissibly influenced by the public indignation.
This objection could cause the appeals courts to deal with the question of whether the new technologies make new rules necessary in the courtroom.
"Just as I witnessed it during this process, I believe it's really a danger if the entry of new technologies into the courtroom affects every individual's right to a fair trial," said Thomas J. Ullman, Mr Haye's main defender.
The presidinging judge, Jon C. Blue of the Supreme Court, last week dismissed the motions by decision briefly and succinctly when he said that the new media would be "carefully controlled" and that there was no proof that the jury had been driven by passions.
However, this was certainly not the last word on this matter.
For decades, court appeals have found that reporting on infamous cases raises doubts about the fairness of a trial.
In its historic 1966 decision, the U.S. Supreme Court overturned the verdict against Dr. Sam Sheppard, an osteopath from Cleveland who is said to have killed his wife, and said in its decision that there had been a "carnival atmosphere in the courtroom" because of the new medies.
Some believe that the television series and the feature film "On the Run" is based on this crime.
Some lawyers say tributal appeals looking at Mr. Haye's sentencing half a century later could follow the argument that new rules have become necessary to regulate the lines of communication from the courtroom that were still unimaginable at the time of Dr. Sheppard.
"There is a good reason to be disturbed about the influence of the New Media in trials persecuted by a broad public," said John J. Donohue III, a professor at Stanford Law School, who investigated the death penalty in Connecticut. According to his report, it is arbitrarily imposed.
Professor Donohue said some appelation courts might be fascinated by the idea that restricting immediate communication out of the courtroom could make the trials fairer.
The Twitter matter is just one of many that Mr Haye's lawyers set out to debate for years and thus delay a sentencing.
Defenders, for example, also claim that Mr. Hayes may have already been prejudged when Judge Blue hadn't allowed them to test the reactions of potential jurors by showing them grispy crime scene photos during juror selection.
Some of the jurors have said photos of the victims, Jennifer Hawke-Petit, 48, and her fugitor, Hayley, 17, and Michaela, 11 years old, would have filled them with revulsion.
The defence lawyers say they would also question the constitutionality of the death penalty constitution, arguing that the judge had mistakenly exchanged a juror who had been a critic of the prosecutor's office, and that he would have inadmissibly put pressure on the verdict when he had made the unusual decision to put the jury free over the weekend.
The jurors weren't isolated.
Yet their arguments about the influence of detailed coverage of the process are perhaps attracting the greatest interest.
In a sentencing they filed last month, they found that the case had dominated the news in Connecticut and was being debated by the candidates during the election campaign for the governor's office and for the American Senate.
They argued that the new media had continuously rekindled the flames of emotions over the case.
"Such a day-to-day rush of media beepers, cameras and reporters outside the courtroom," the lawyers wrote, "you've not experienced before in New Haven and maybe even nationwide."
Her focus on Twitter's reporting is controversial.
In interviews, some journalists said the claim that these direct messages are more incitential than reports being made more slowly is an anti-technical look that imposses reporting on future processes.
However, some lawyers said appelation courts were often confronted with the argument that societal and technical changes needed adaptation by the courts.
Mr. Hayer's lawyers reinforce that claim.
In their appeal, they referred to the Supreme Court's decision of 1966, in which the sentencing of Dr. Sheppard was overturned based on reporting and made it clear that the New Media requires new rules.
"This case," they wrote, "representing Sheppard against Maxwell of the 21st-century."
Can politeness connect the separating?
According to a campaign of swear words and negativity, little courtesy broke out in Washington this week.
Is it the omen of a true two-party cooperation or a moment of illusion before quickly returning to the struggle of the parties in the capital?
Tuesday's meeting between President Obama and heads of Congress did not bring a breakthrough on the big issues to deal with during the so-called "lame duck session," the session at the end of a government period in which no more decisions were made.
After all that one hears, it was neither a polite discussion between two different sides nor an attention to their shared accountability to rule, but rather an empty episode of a partisan point-gathering.
Wednesday's public meeting of the president's Deficit Commission was equally civilized, despite the political differences that persist between members.
It seems clear that a close interaction over a period of months creates a mutual respect that obscures the ideological divide that continues to separate members.
This is hardly enough to claim that a new era of cooperation has dawned.
Both sides are now in a test phase - in which one of the other demands signs of willingness to cooperate and compromise, while one tries to weigh the boundaries of public opinion and the patience of the voters after a renewed party warfare.
Therefore, Tuesday's meeting itself, and its assessment by participants, was limited to a minimum of demands or recriminations.
The philosophical differences between Obama and the Republicans are too great and too deep to expect a rapid shift in the atmosphere that has prevailed over the past two years.
Whether it's taxes, donations, health or entitlements, the two sides are miles apart.
Last but not least, the November election of Washington voted mildly, at least for a short time.
"No question, the tone has changed," said Pete Wehner, an official representative of the Bush administration who now works for the "Ethics and Public Policy Center."
“That’s inescapable after elections.
Elections arouse partisan passions that die again.
And that’s good.”
On some issues, especially those that are on the "lame duck session" agenda, Republicans and Democrats have little choice but to find a solution."
Bush's tax cuts end at the end of the year, and no one endorses that.
Both sides want to find a way to preserve some or even all, whether temporarily or permanently.
Likewise, a compromise on the increased unemployment benefits is to be expected, if only because Republicans can't afford to believe the favored tax cuts for the rich while denying support to unemployed workers.
After Tuesday's meeting at the White House, optimism has risen that one can agree on these issues during the "lame duck session" and at the same time also ratify the New START Treaty.
Yet William Glaston of the Brookings Institution warns that a successful collaboration on urgent issues of the lame duck session" does not necessarily have to lead to permanent cooperation of the parties when the new 112th Congress is convened in January.
Instead, he says, a period of disagreement is more likely, perhaps even unavoidable.
"After the things that need to be done are done, this short-term psychology will turn into confrontation," he said.
Wehner is also skeptical - and this scepticism is shared by many - the courtesy leads to true cooperation.
"I see a lot of what's happening from today until 2012, as pre-fighting, or shadow boxing, for the main event of 2012," he said.
The reasons for this have a lot to do with the presentation of the new Republican majority in the White House and with the tea party voters, who served their formation.
No matter how concerned Republican leaders may also be about playing poker too high, they know they need to try to keep their promises to cut government spending and override health care reform.
These party lines are already evident in the initial reactions regarding the recommendations of the Deficit Commission, which include tax reform and raising some taxes, as well as the raising of the retirement age and reducing government spending.
Deputies Alan Simpson and Erskine Bowles have taken on one of the most difficult jobs in Washington and, according to their own estimations, have pushed so much to put the deficit issue on the public agenda that they can no longer be ignored.
But they need 14 of the 18 votes within the Commission to make their plan an official recommendation and they are struggling very much to even get close to that number.
The greatest resistance can be found in the elected representatives of the committee, since there is little room for manoeuvre or little sympathy for compromise in financial matters, at least today.
Galston predicts two phases in the battle between the White House and the Republicans in Congress.
The first fight will take place next year in the form of party clashes when Republicans challenge Obama regarding the state budget, taxes, and the health care system, followed later by a period of greater bipartisan cooperation.
What will lead to this change?
public opinion.
Proved that, despite the partiality of the current election, the majority of Americans still want the two parties to work together.
If confrontation leads to an impasse, as happened in 1995, then both parties must pay careful attention to the public reaction.
Whoever is condemned to lose will have to change course and seek compromise.
This may indeed be an optimistic point of view.
Particiality and polarization are greater today than they were in the mid-1990s, and they appear to be a bigger ideological divide between Obama and congressional Republicans than it was between President Bill Clinton and the Republicans.
At the same time, the activists of both parties see 2010 as a pure prelude to a much more far-reaching election in 2012.
Viewed in this way, the courtesy that will be flaunted this week may be welcomed by many Americans.
However, it will take much more effort on both sides to find a common denominator on the issues that still separate the two.
Leaked U.S. document portrays Moscow as port of corruption
The city's scrutiny is in the hands of the "cleptocracy" and it passes on a portion of the bribes and protection money it collects to the Kremlin. For example, the American embassy in Russia reported in a memo in February.
Both police and the state security service are extorting hefty protection funds so they can maintain their expensive standard of living in Moscow.
They collect money not only from legitimized dealings, but also from organized criminal gands.
Every layer of bureaucracy - what Prime Minister Vladimir Putin has called a "vertical of authority" - takes its part of the money that wanders the ladder upwards.
The memo, which was sented to Washington under the name of Ambassador John Beyrle, was posted by WikiLeaks on its homepage on Tuesday.
The memo is based on sources whose names have been redigated.
It has been written while Yuri Luzhkov was still Moscow's mayor, and it accuses him of corruption.
He was fired by President Dmitry Medvedev in October, but there is hardly any evidence that has been cleaned up in the meantime.
Luzhkov and his wife, Yelena Baturina, who owns a well-knowned construction company, have repeatedly denied allegations of corruption for years, and he has won numerous defamation lawsuits against such accusations.
In September, he explained to a Russian television station when asked about a documentary suggesting he's corrupt: "It's crazy, it's dirty, it's dirty."
> An incredibly large number of Russians believe that their society is corrupt. That's what polls have revealed.
But it is uncommon that, as happened in the ambassador's report, individual persons are named, and it is unheard that this happens through a diplomat of a foreign country.
"The Moscow's government's direct connection with crime has led some to call it 'disturbed' and conclude that the government acts more like a kleptocrat than a government," according to the memo.
“Criminal elements enjoy a “kryshna” (a term from the world of criminals/mafia, which literally means “roof” or protection) that extends across the police, state security (FSB), the supervisory authority (MVD) and the prosecutor’s office as well as the entire Moscow government bureaucracy.
Experts have discovered a three-layer structure within Moscow's criminal world.
“Luzhkov is paramount.”
"FSB, MVD and the militia are on the second stage."
“Lastly, the ordinary criminals and the criminal inspectors follow at the lowest level.”
“This is an incompetent system in which criminal groups close a gap in some areas because the city offers no help whatsoever.”
The report goes on to say that the FSB collects money from the largest companies and that the police arrest small businessmen.
One source, it says, "declared that Moscow's business owners understand that it's best to get protection from the MVD and FSB (and not from organized crime) as they not only have more weapons, resources and power than the criminals, but also because they're protected by the law."
“For this reason, protection by criminal gangs is no longer as it used to be.”
The memo notes that, while collecting the funds is comprehensive, the protection itself is very modest none; even those who pay are sometimes arrested.
Those who do not pay find their business very quickly closed - due to one or the other pretext.
Medvedev has talked several times about fighting corruption, but at the same time noted that he has made little progress.
The message's memo includes descriptions of men going to the Kremlin with suitcases presumably full of money.
"In its fight against corruption, Medvedev must rely on the bureaucrats," Georgy Satarov, director of Indem, a Moscow expert commission, said in a recent interview.
“But he’s part of that bureaucracy.”
"It's not a part of the political class because a political class no longer exists in Russia."
Without politicians, without an opposition, without a separation of powers, he says, corruption is inevitable.
Nationwide, according to Indem, corruption costs Russia more than $300 billion a year.
The country ranks 154th in a more recent investigation into the global perception of corruption by the non-governmental organization Transparence International (countries are listed from "least corrupt" to "most corrupt").
The revelation of the memo of the American embassy, which happened late in the evening after Moscow, will almost certainly trigger displeasure within the Russian government, since Luzhkov can be accused of Moscow’s mistakes, it may also be a possibility for the Kremlin to claim that one solves the problems.
In an interview with Larry King for CNN, Putin claimed that the WikiLeaks documents could be a forgery and reacted angrily to the revelation that American diplomats called him Batman and Medvedev Robin.
"The truth is that it's about our interaction, which is an important factor of domestic policy in this country," he said.
“But to be honest, we didn’t expect this to happen with such arrogance, with such pressure and in such an unethical way, you know.”
Khmer Rouge veterans forced to confront the past
In a dimly illuminated, concreted classroom with dirty and flaking walls, the director of Anlong Veng High School recalls the man who built him, the later Khmer Rouge guide Ta Mok.
“Everyone here loves Ta Mok.”
"He was a good leader and he took care of his people," said Sreng Kor Ma, 42.
Because of its brutality during the Khmer Rouge rule known as "the butcher," the commander remains popular in this remote former Khmer Rouge stronghold in northwestern Cambodia, where he built hospitals and bridges, developed infrastructure and where thousands of former soldiers still live.
Yet this year, 12 years after the Khmer Rouge's capitulation to the government, longtime supporters in Anlong Veng are being challenged.
In April, a local truth and reconciliation forum allowed the victims public confrontation with the people who once supported the regime.
In June, the government handed out a textbook that for the first time tells children of former soldiers the story of the Khmer Rouge.
And in July, a community tribunal of the U.N. and Cambodia first condemned a former member of the Khmer Rouge when it sentenced the former head of the infamous Tuol Sleng torture chamber, Kaing Khek Iev, better known as Duch, to 35 years in prison.
With each of these developments, the fear among the Khmer Rouge Veteran Alnlong Vengs grew, complicating their efforts for reconciliation and reintegration into Cambodia’s society.
"There's still rejection and fear in the former Khmer Rouge, but they don't have any power to do anything," said Chhang Youk, head of Cambodia's Documentation Center, an independent organization that explores the regime.
“For them, life under the Khmer Rouge was glorious, but the regime has become a symbol of evil.”
“It divides families itself.”
During the Khmer Rouge rule in Cambodia, between 1975 and 1979, an estimated 1.7 million people were executed or they died from disease, overhaul or from starvation.
When the Vietnamese invaded and overthrew the Pol Pot-Ied government in 1979, the remnants of the regime and its military fled to Thailand.
Here they spread until December 1998, when the last movement surrendered, turmoil.
When Pol Pot, Nuon Chea and Ieng Sary waged guerrilla warfare from their base in the west of the mountains and jungles of Cambodia, Ta Mok attracted the next generation in Anlong Veng.
But in the mid-1990s, when a U.N.-promoted peace agreement led to the country's first democratic elections in 1993, large groups of Khmer Rouge fighters began defecting to the government, leading to Ieng Sary's capitations in 1996 and Nuan Chea and Khieu Samphan in 1998.
Pol Pot died a natural death in 1998, and Ta Mok, who did not yield, was arrested by government forces at the Dangrek Mountains in 1999.
He died in 2006 at Phnom Penh's prison where he was awaiting his trial.
When their patrons no longer protected them, most former Khmer Rouge fighters fled into the gruelling poverty of rural Cambodia and many kept the movement in nostalgic memory.
Even though a few Khmer Rouge's elitist officials retained their local government posts because they laid down their weapons, the ordinary soldiers remained poor, uneducated peasants.
"These people have benefited very little from surrender," said Sok Leang of the Center for Justice and Reconciliation, which holds public forums throughout Cambodia, including in Anlong Veng
“They are anchored in the utopian agricultural ideology of the regime.”
“They grew up without any professional knowledge.”
Sor Lim, 55, who had joined the Rhmer Rouge as a teenager in 1974, struggled in the jungle around Anlang Veng before settling in 1998 to live a life as a poor rice farmer.
"Life under the Khmer Rouge was good," he said.
“Ta Mok gave everyone something to eat.”
“But now life is hard because we have to take care of ourselves.”
The ongoing Khmer Rouge Tribunal has also raised concerns here.
Early next year, the court will try to put Nuon Chea, Khieu Samphan, Ieng Sary and his wife, as well as Ieng Thirith, another former Khmer Rouge minister, on trial.
The court's order is to convict the old leaders and those who are mainly responsible for the atrocities, but it is not said whether it will prosecute further acts beyond these four instances.
That has done little to mitigate the fears in Anlong Veng.
Current media speculation has focused on Im Chaem, 64, who had been district chief in Manteay Meanchey province during Khmer Rouge rule in the late 1970s.
In 2007, she had told representatives of the Cambodia Documentation Center that she had led the construction of the Trapeang Thma Damm, a project in which thousands of forced laborers presumably died.
On a hot evening Im Chaem came back from working in the fields to her wooden house on a dirty road outside Anlong Veng.
As the sun threw long shadows on the withered grass Im Chaem declined to discuss her past at the Khmer Rouge.
Should the court subpoena her, she would refuse to leave, she said.
"Cambodia lives in peace and stability," she said.
“If there were no more persecutions, there would be war here.”
Prime Minister Hun Sen, who fled the Khmer Rouge in 1878, has also repeatedly warned that the situation would become unsafe if the court would indict more suspects, even though experts and historians are discussing.
"Cambodia has to dig a hole and bury the past," he said.
But the confrontation with the past is what Cambodia needs to do to be able to move forward, said Chhang Youk of the Documentation Centre.
"Reconciliation with the Khmer times means gathering the broken pieces," he said.
"It's our duty to put these broken parts together, so that how anything can understand."
The center created the first government-approved textbook about the Khmer Rouge, the 75-page "History of Democratic Cambodia."
It distributed the book in Anlong Veng in June as an appendix to the history book for the Ministry of Education's high school, which contains less than four pages across the Khmer Rouge.
As often in Cambodia, the young people in Anlong Veng know very little about the Khmer Rouge, despite the city's links to the regime.
Touch Valeak, 19 years old, a student at Anlong Veng High School, said the new textbook helps students understand an important part of their history.
But his parents reacted sceptically when he brought home the book to read it.
"My family rejects many parts of the book and the judgment," he said.
“They’re distrusting because they’ren’t sure how many people are convicted by the court.”
Such resistance makes the reconciliation process complex and complicated, Sok Leang said.
But the public forums, the textbook and the court are gradually gaining influence," he said.
The Khmer Rouge still has a powerful attraction here.
Upstairs in the Dangrek mountains, a path overgrown by shrubs and dirted with plastic waste leads to a rectangle of black soot, covered with a rusty tin roof.
Pol Pot's body had been burned here after his death in 1998 on a pile of tires.
Nuom Sothea, 31, a roadside mobile phone saleswoman, said she didn't know much about the man who had been burned here.
"But he has a strong spirit and a lot of people here go to him to pray," she said.
It was Nuom Sothea's birthday and later in the day she wanted to go to Pol Pot's last resting place, where she'll drop off a bush of ripe bananas - hoping he'll bring her luck.
Belarusia agrees not to accumulate weapons-gradable uranium
In a surprise victory for President Obama's campaign to secure nuclear materials worldwide, the government of Belarus announced on Wednesday that it would no longer collect highly enriched uranium, which is a critical component of nuclear weapons.
The deal was announced in a joint statement following a meeting of Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton with Belarusian Foreign Minister Sergei Martynov in an additional article of the security summit.
The U.S. government had spent years trying to convince Belarus to no longer collect highly enriched uranium and appeared to have reached a hurdle last spring.
Yet U.S. government officials told the Washington Post that in the past two months, two secret agreements have been reached with Belarus to remove 187 pounds of weapons-grade uranium from a Belarusian research institute.
That set the stage for Wednesday's agreement to remove the rest of the material - about £500, according to an official employee who remains anonymous due to the sensitivity of the issue.
The agreement appears to be one of the most important outcomes of Obama's "Nuclear Security Summit of the 47 Nations" from April, which excluded Belarus.
And it was obviously good enough to ensure the former Soviet republic's invitation to the next 2012 summit.
“I was really excited by the fact that they wanted a seat in the Nuclear Security Summit (of 2012).”
"This proves to me that it works," said Ken Luongo, president of the Global Security Partnership, a group to promote nuclear security.
Clinton called the agreement "a very significant, important step" by Belarus.
Obama pledged in April 2009 in Prague to secure all precarious nuclear material within four years.
Since then, the U.S. government has helped six countries get rid of their highly enriched uranium.
Belarus will be the seventh.
A dozen countries did it under George W. Bush's administration.
At the time of Obama's nuclear security summit, Belarusian President Alexander Lukashenko has declared that the nation will never give up its uranium.
"How retain highly enriched uranium - hundreds of kilos, which is fundamentally weaponizable, and less-enriched uranium," Lukashenko said at the time.
“That’s our raw material... We’ren’t going to make dirty bombs out of it and we won’t sell it to anyone.”
“We use it exclusively for research purposes, that’s all.”
Belarus nevertheless approached the U.S. a few months ago when it indicated interest in meeting an agreement.
The first attempt to removal the uranium of Belarus in October was particularly complicated because the material was slightly irradiated, officials say.
It has been loaded into a special train that has more than 1,300 miles to a Russian factory for storage and disposal for, officials say.
The second attempt was decided on Monday and was carried out by plane, according to the officials.
The U.S. government pays $14 million for these efforts, with much of the money flowing into Belarusian research with weakly enriched uranium, which cannot be used for nuclear weapons.
"This has been extremely carefully coordinated and coordinated to build trust," which leaded to the agreement signed by Clinton, Andrew Bieniawski, a representative of the Department of Energy working on global danger reduction.
The James Martin Centre for Non-Proliferable Studies estimates that Belarus had at least 88 pounds of weapons-grading uranium this year, as well as hundreds of pounds of highly enriched uranium.
Belarus wants to dissolve its camp by 2012, according to the joint agreement.
Relations between Belarus and the U.S. were strained for years, primarily because of Lukashenko's harsh disregard for human rights.
But Wednesday's statement suggests a possible rapprochement.
“Welcominging the progress of these global security issues, the United States of America and Belarus announce that increased respect for democracy and human rights in Belarus remains a central issue in improving bilateral relations and it is essential for the progress of the country and its citizens,” she explains.
No objections from the Spanish government against CIA flights
The Spanish government had no objections to the CIA flights with Taliban and Al Qaeda prisoners across Spanish territory to Guantánamo, according to a telegram from the U.S. ambassador in Madrid.
This is reflected by WikiLeaks and forwarded to the newspaper "El País" (by cable). It is added that former Deputy Prime Minister María Teresa Fernández de la Vega demanded that the Spanish government should be informed "to demonstrate that foreign planes flying over Spain will be adequately monitored."
In June 2006, following the publication of a report by the Council of Europe accusinging the aid for human rights-provoking practices in a dozen countries, including Spain, the former North American ambassador Eduardo Aguirre and Fernández de la Vega.
The deputy prime minister admitted that the Council's report "met Zapatero on the sidelines" and reiterated that Spain had nothing to hide in the matter.
"De la Vega underlined that Spain has no objections to intelligence flights over Spanish territory."
"They just want to be informed" in order not to be "unpreparedly surprised," Aguirre reports by cable.
Former Foreign Minister Miguel Ángel Moratinos also pointed out to the ambassador that the Spanish government "does not want to pay much attention to this matter, even though the government has very little influence, as these are in the hands of the judges."
Prosecutor Vicente González, for his part, told the embassy in January 2007 that he would not block the judicial initiative to declass the documents of the Spanish Intelligence Headquarters (CNI) and the Secretary of Defense about the CIA flights.
"These reports do not contain any incriminating or sensitive information," the ambassador explained after a conversation with the prosecutor.
Even so, the U.S. was more concerned about the "apparent coordination between the Spanish prosecutor and German prosecutors" than about the declassified information of the CNI and the Ministry of Defence.
"This coordination between independent explorers will complicate our efforts for discrete handling between governments," Aguirre laments.
At a lunch in March 2007 attended by U.S. Ambassador and Spanish Interior Minister Alfredo Pérez Rubalcaba, the latter commented that "although the Spanish judges are insanely keen on their independence and did what they deem appropriate," he had given instructions to his associates to "avocacy rhetorical comments in order to cause the tension to subside."
The ambassador also expressed his concern about the PP's approach to this matter.
“Despite their generally pro-American stance, our experience teaches us that that the PP will not doubt it, from an indication thating that the U.S. government has tolerated or intentionally ignored as possible human rights violations, even if these revelations could negatively affect the U.S. government,” he emphasizes.
The child murderer confesses that she had complained on the Internet about the "toil" of motherhood.
The young woman spoke of the child when she was already dead and she had a blog: "Terror in a Galician way"
Mónica Juanatey Fernández is "Muki" on the internet.
Under this epithet, which she leads from an early age, she maintains contact with a cousin and at least one friend. Both are from Noia, a village in the province of La Coruña where she was born.
The notices are from 2008.
To understand these, one must know her love life, which the daily ABC reconstructs on the basis of testimonies of her family members.
The 30-year-old Galician woman established a relationship with Iván Túñez, an industrialist.
She lasted a year.
Shortly after she disintegrated her relationship, it was revealed that she was pregnant.
He wanted to know if the baby was from him, but his alumni never let him do a DNA analysis.
Shortly thereafter, she entered into a different relationship.
The Chosen One was Alberto, a young man from Lousame, a neighboring community.
She lived with him for a long time.
César lived with them.
When the young woman went to the Balearic Islands to look around the labour market, the little one stayed with her partner, who even accompanied her to Palma for a week.
But from the content of their news it can be concluded that their love relationship broke down and they separated.
In March 2000, she's alone in Menorca and writes: "Alberto is very peculiar and pissed off because I've come here to work."
“When I told him this, he found it good, but now of course he realizes what it means to be alone with the boy all day all day and not be able to do what he wants.”
She later mentions a message she's just received on her phone: "I want you to come back this month or give me an address where I can send the boy to you."
She complains: "If he doesn't like leaving the boy with my mother, I'll pick him up when I'm back."
“This week I’m going there, but I’ll only stay there until 19, because I’ll have to introduce myself to a work on the 20th if I don’t want to lose it.”
She explains that she'll take her first-born if all goes well.
"He's going to be at where he has to be, that's with me. Never in my life will I leave him with anyone except my mother," a dubious intent when considering their bad relationship.
There's another email in July.
When she writes this, it is assumed that the boy, who came to the island on the 1st of the month and was allegedly murdered on the 10th, is dead.
“On the one hand, César is satisfied, on the other hand not.”
“On the one hand, he’s with me and he likes the place a bit, but on the other hand he has to go to school every day.”
She's talking about a move to Mallorca.
“There he (the boy) will attend a summer course, so I have less expenses for the nanny.”
On the 17th: Memories and Complainations.
"It's a big kiss from César asking me when you're coming."
"Alberto says he emailed me any videos. You see, if he wants to, he finds me, such a crap."
On the 30th more: "The boy is doing well. He's going into a summer course to learn Catalan."
From Víctor, her current partner, there's one: "He's shooting me money."
On the Internet, in anime and mangablogs, there are messages from her.
She even had her own blog in MySpace, which is now locked, and in which she called herself "Muki, the Jail Dismisser."
"Terror a la gallega (heading) ".
The last man who lived with her, a sworn-in hunting guard, declares to have remorse for feeling guilty.
He can't believe that everything happened because she hadn't told him that she's a single mother.
Mónica was transferred yesterday by the Centre for Prisoners in Menorca to the provincial prison in Palma.
The defender, Carlos Maceda, declared that she could not remember the course of the facts, "and she had no intention of harming the little one."
Previously, however, she had confessed to drowning César in the bathtub and stowed his body in a suitcase.
Defense and prosecutors requested a psychiatric opinion.
Partido Popular won 4 seats in Congress and PSC lost 9
With the results of 28 November, the PP could play a decisive role in the cities of Lérida, Tarragona and Barcelona
After the significant increase in votes and MPs in the last regional elections, the PP is already turning its attention to the parliamentary elections - preferred or not. Their expectations are more than positive, because the number of votes this party achieves in these elections is usually twice as high as those in Catalonia's elections.
For example, the Catalan People's Party received 610,473 votes in the 2008 parliamentary elections, while this figure was only 316,222 in the regional elections in 2006.
Last Sunday, the PPC scored 384,091 votes.
If this rising tendency - the result of redirection of the PSC's share of votes and the bond of the PP's electorates - should continue, the PPC's multiplies would rise from 8 to 12 members of parliament in the parliamentary elections, while CiU would also gain four (now 10) and the PSC would lose at least 9 deputies (out of 25).
These calculations demonstrate Catalonia’s fundamental importance for PP and PSOE.
According to the People's Party's analysis, the ERC would lose an MP - currently it has three - while ICV could hold or win one.
However, should the electoral calendar be maintained, the next date with the municipal elections falls into May 2011. Even then, the People's Party (PP) can be the tongue to put an end to socialist supremacy, namely in four provincial capitals and with its majority in the city council of Barcelona, one of the city administrations with the largest budget: 661 million euros for 2011.
Both the Nationalists and the People's Party agree that the PSC will lose the cities of Barcelona, Tarragona, Lérida and Gerona, in which the Socialists can neither achieve a majority nor resort to their traditional allies (ERC and ICV).
After the Republican debacle, it is not absurd that this party will lose its representation on Barcelona's local council, in which it would have to score 5% of the vote.
The PP, on the other hand, recorded an increase in its share of votes in the four provincial capitals and could become the decisive force in Barcelona, Lérida and Tarragona.
The nationalists are convinced to be able to play the leading role in Tarragona's transition. There they had ruled before the current mayor, Josep Félix Ballesteros, along with the PP.
En Lérida, the PP is only 500 votes behind the PSC. The mayor, Àngel Ros, maintains good relations with Mariano Rajoy - during the last election campaign they met several times.
The People's Party also recorded a significant increase in other important places, such as Badalona, Castelldefels or Reus.
PP is currently represented in 34 city administrations of Catalonia and is striving for the formation of lists in as many places as possible.
Catalan cell concerned Al Qaeda thousands of passports
In addition to the seven arrests in Barcelona there were three more in Thailand
The seven Pakistanis and Nigerians, arrested by the police of the Barcelona police, formed the jihadist cell controlled from Thailand, the different Al Qaeda groups, including the "Movement for the Liberation of Tamil Eelam" (LTTE) and "Lashkar e-Taiba " (Let) - the latter was the author of the assassinations in Bombay, which claimed 106 deaths in 2008.
The Spanish interior minister assigns great importance to these investigations, which goes so far that he assures "Operation Kampai" had "weakened" Al Qaeda's forgery apparatus worldwide and "thus their ability to act".
The researchers assume that in the two years in which the cell has been active in Catalonia, thousands of passports have been sent to Thailand, where they were forged and then sent to the groups from which the criminal galaxy Al Qaeda is composed, so that their "fighters" could cross the borders of European and other Western countries without impunity.
The uncovered cell isn't the only one of the jihadist network that has been active in Europe. That's why, in addition to the Thai investigative bodies, other European police units have also participated in the investigation. It can't be ruled out that there will be similar assassinations on the territory of the EU over the next few days, to those carried out in Barcelona.
In coordination with this operation (the Pakistanis Junaid Humayun, Atiqur Rehman, Jabran Asghar, Malik Iftikhar Ahmad, Mohammad Saddique Khan Begum, Tanveer Arshad and the Nigerian Babatunde Agunbiade were arrested in Thailand, where the Pakistanis Muhammad Athar Butt and Zeeshan Ehsan Butt were arrested.
They had run the cell established in Spain and other European countries from Bangkok.
The Catalan network - part of which had been nullified in 2009 - stole tourist passports in particular according to the specifications of the so-called "International Islamic Front."
This means that the documents were issued in the names of men aged 25 to 45 years and still had to be valid for several years; they were not allowed to have visa stamps.
Investigative circles underlined that until now no connections could be detected between those arrested in Catalonia and the Bombay attack, although the author of this assassination, "Lashkar e-Taiba," was one of the recipient groups of passports stolen in Spain and subsequently forged in Thailand.
Nevertheless, it should be recalled that a few days before the assassination of Catalonia, several thousand dollars had been sent by means of a money transfer company, "invested" in the purchase of prepaid cards for mobile phones, which were later used by the members of the "command," which put an end to the lives of 106 people.
The police have seized extensive documentation.
PP asks Mas to adjoure the tax agreement
PPC's chairwoman, Alicia Sánchez-Camacho, took the opportunity yesterday to personally present Artur Mas in support of taking office as prime minister of the Generalitat. These do not differ from those already set during the election campaign and can be combined with the primacy of the economic resurgency and the omission of regionalist independence adventures, such as a financial compensatory agreement that the regionalist leader wants.
The leader of the Catalan People's Party appealed with certainty to Artur Mas, who awarded her the 18 deputies she had won last Sunday - a numeral that is more than sufficient to feel indispensable. Because the candidate from the bourgeois-regionalist party CiU depends on that at least one of the opposition parties abstain from the vote when he takes office.
The future alliances that the regionalists want to enter into in the upcoming parliamentary elections in Congress depend on a possible Entente with the PP. However, the talks that Mas is currently holding with the other party leaders revolve around Catalonia.
As for the party leaders of the PPC, it demands that in support of taking office "all discourses that affect the sovereignty and independence of Catalonia" as well as an agreement on financial equalization between the state and the Autonomous region - similar to the Basque Country - and which the party leader of CiU even wants to subjugate to a referendum.
Both political leaders have in common that they want to streamline the established political apparatus of the regional administration and abolish the inheritance and gift tax.
On the part of the PPC Jordi Cornet and Dolors Montserrat deal with the details of the negotiations to take office.
Mas has already met with Joaquim Nadal (PSC) - who announced tough opposition - and Joan Herrera (ICV). Today he will meet with Joan Puigcercós (ERC), Albert Rivera (Ciutadans) and Joan Laporta (Solidaritate Catalana).
The elderly nursing home reaffirms that the deaths are not due to 'lack of measures'
The owners of the old people's home in Olot don't rule out their carer killing over eleven
Those in charge of La Caritat's nursing home in Olot (Gerona) yesterday gave a press conference in which they pointed out the damage the criminal actions of Joan Vila Dilmé - the nurser who had killed at least eleven old people he was in charge of during his work - had done to the facility.
The head of the nursing home, Joan Sala, accompanied by his lawyer, Joan Cañada, appeared in judgment and announced that "La Caritatit would like to accuse the person responsible (for deaths)," but "the law and our duty of liability compele us to accept the trial and civil liability."
The institution not only has to accept a loss of societal reputation, because it had not determined the criminal acts of the carer in time, but now also pay a large amount of compensation, which is calculated by the legal representatives of the family members of the victims.
Joan Vila Dilmé confessed that the first crime dates back to August 2009 and that he had continued to assassinate ever since.
The judge ordered the exhumation of eight bodies - former patients of the nursing home for the elderly - because he suspected that no doctor had personally examined the deceased, but the formalities due to their ages and illnesses were handled by telephone.
The head of La Caritat denied this point and declared that "missing measures may not have been the reason for the incidencents at any time."
La Caritat's lawyer added that "there was no evidence in the litigation of negligent acts by a person other than the accused."
The man, who was already baptized as an "angel of death," confessed eleven murders the day before yesterday and declared to have committed the first crime on August 29, 2009, and murdered the second old lady a month and a half later.
In this case, he killed with an overdose of insulin, in contrast to the first crime, in which he had administered a mixture of various sedatives.
La Caritat's head explained staff's access to the drugs was difficult to control.
The insulin injections and sedative cocktail were used in six out of nine crimes committed in order to free the patients with death from their suffering.
But the results of the labor examinations will point out whether Vila was lying and actually chose a less gruesome death for his first victims or whether he burned it out from the inside by giving them bleaching liquor, according to the confession in three women whom he murdered between October 12 and 17.
In La Caritat, you don't believe the nurse's statements, not even the number of confessed murders, which might well be more. This attitude is justified by the fact that Vila had made very different statements in judgment and that it makes no sense to suddenly change its course of action after eight successful murders.
They say they made them available to the judiciary for this reason and to clarify the scope of these incidents.
European Central Bank (ECB) threaten
Investors yesterday reacted sharply to ECB President Trichet's threat, who warned "markets shouldn't underestimate our responsiveness."
The risk surcharges of the euro-peripheric countries have risen dramatically and the shares of European banks by more than 5%.
Nevertheless, we should not underestimate the possibility that the ECB can be deceived and that the euro has hardly increased points, which leads to concern.
Yesterday's events are typical of an illiquid market that is recovering to the extent that it collapses, but as a result of the closure of short and highly speculative positions.
The institutional investor won’t lose its fears.
A month agove today, the European Central Bank urged us to undo our liquidity measures, according to which the ECB will have to withdraw tomorrow for the fourth time in two years, and probably several more times in the future.
The Bundesbank called for the termination of the plan to buy sovereign debt, as it was ineffective, as well as the lifting of all exceptional temporary liquidity measures. Only one-week indefinite access should be maintained.
Following the Ireland crisis, we expect the current liquidity measures to be extended "at least until March" and to maintain quarterly tenders with unlimited access at the end of the quarter to ensure liquidity until at least June.
We also expect that the programme for the purchase of bonds will continue and issue a strengthening signal.
If the expectations created aren’t met, yesterday’s markets’ excellent reaction will be thrown back and if stiff-heartedness is insisted that we take back our liquidity measures, only God can help us!
Just as at the ECB meeting in May, at which the bank reaffirmed that it would not consider buying government debt and the markets collapsed as a result, that history could repeat itself tomorrow.
The ECB must be credible and persevering.
In May, she showed her teeth and on Wednesday she said she had already bought a lot, provocating a new collapse of the market.
Yesterday, the auction of one-year state titles in Portugal was quite weak.
The state wealth management spent only 500 million at 5.28%, compared to 750 million to 4.81% in the previous auction.
Nevertheless, interest rates in Portugal fell by 30 basis points across the curve, reflecting a change in the perception of the markets according to Trichet.
In Ireland, figures from the debt hole of Allied and Bank of Ireland have been published.
The sum is estimated at about 10 000 million short-term and possible another 15 000. There is time until February to procure private capital or these banks will be nationalised.
Although Ireland has completely lost its credibility after failing to recognise the issue with July's stress tests, the figures under the bailout plan are acceptable and helping to lessen uncertainty.
However, the government needs to get parliamentary approval of its new budget next Tuesday. Since it still does not have sufficient support, Ireland remains a source of uncertainty.
Angela Merkel made her discourse more moderate with regard to the summit of heads of state and the government on December 14, where political unity is required to show that we are playing the same score together.
The problem is that Germany wants to play Wagner and the other countries the ninth symphony of Beethoven.
Spain's president yesterday unveiled the action plan, which is due to be adopted by the Council of Ministers on Friday.
Pension reform, transparency in the Autonomous regions such as financial institutions, reduction of the government deficit, liberalization, privatization and job creation measures. Measures that sound in the ears of investors like Vivaldi's Four Seasons.
The doubling effect Zapatero and Trichet explains why the Spanish stock market has gained 4.5 percent, twice as much as the rest of Europe's exchanges.
Compared to February, the reaction of the government and Spain's central bank was quick and convincing, and we benefit from the surprise effect.
Today there is an authoritative auction of a three-year government bond that will take advantage of the Trichet effect. That's why we are counting on strong demand that will eliminate all doubts about our debt restructuring capability.
For Spain, applying for an aid plan would be self-sacrificing.
We would get liquidity that we don't need. The Treasury has €40 billion available, three times as much as in March. Interest rates are exceedingly expensive and access to the markets would be closed without notice.
Nevertheless, our strategy should correspond to Caesar's wife's motto: "Now not only being counts, but also appearance."
Courage, we can do it!
Eulen's Chairman of the Executive Board contests Vega Sicilia's Shareholders' meeting at the request of his children
The storm in the open conflict of the Álvarez family, owners of Owls and El Enebro societies, does not dwindle.
David Álvarez, founder of the corporate group, will contest El Enebro's shareholder meeting, which will take place at the request of five of his seven children on the 16th, as he considers them "absolutely voilent." His children will try to gain a majority to control the company's administrative organs - owner of Bodegas Vega Sicilia.
David Álvarez and his children Jesús David and María José are preparing the legal measures for a new judicial chapter in their dispute over the control of part of the group of companies.
The founder of the largest holding company for business services seems unwilling to give in in his litigation over control of El Enebro's organs.
He was driven out of the Board of Directors by five of his seven children, who were appointed members of the Board of Directors at the Shareholders' Meeting on 18 January of this year, which is already registered as contested in the Commercial Register.
El Enebro's Shareholders' Assembly, convened by Madrid's Number 2 Commercial Court judge for the 16th, has sparked a new thunderbolt in the Álvarez family's lap of the Álvarez family.
The appointment of the Judgent to arrange the Assembly is the only legal way that ensures the convening of the shareholders’ meeting.
The final strategy of Juan Carlos, Pablo, Emilio, Marta y Elvira Álvarez Mezquiriz is interpreted by David Álvarez as a "desperate remedy of the applicants - his five children - in order to, by means of legal quirks, fraudulent machinations and legal abuses, to recognize the assembly of January 18, as official spokespersons explained.
This reasoning is the basis for contesting the assembly in hindsight to declare it "absolutely void."
The same protagonists, who wrap themselves in discrete silence, tried unsuccessfully in June to convene a meeting judicially.
At El Enebro - asset company and owner of Bodegas Vega Sicilia, among other assets - David Álvarez's seven children control 100 per cent of the capital.
On the other hand, according to family protocols, the founder of the service group demands the right of use over 51% of capital.
This is the real background of the matter, which the judge has to decide on in the hearing on the treatment of the appeal, for which no date has yet been set.
Red Bull wins in the F1 with a secret weapon from Siemens
The cars are improved in real-time software.
Since Red Bull entered the world of Formula One in 2004, its history in this exquisite universe of speed has been characterized by surprises and successes.
Initially, no one paid attention to the newcomer and, despite his steeply ascending career during the last seasons, everyone relied on Fernando Alonso's victory at the final sprint last November.
Without wanting to reduce the skills of the German driver and world champion Sebastian Vettel or the performance of Renault's engine, the "secret weapon" that the Multi Siemens Red Bull put into its hands, and thus created a winning team, has become a legend.
In reality, this is a high-performance software for simulating processes, which the German company launched in 2007 for purposes that are not related to car racing.
Known as PLM, this computer science program has been used for the development of assembly chains in factories around the world, energy plants and high-precision instruments.
The aim is for a faithful reproduction or simulation of a product or process before it is actually built in order to avoid errors and superfluous costs.
PLM has facilitated the development of Canon's cameras, Sukhoi's planes and Nissan's most famous commercial vehicles.
Siegfried Russwurm, head of Siemen's industrial division, explains that with the use of this software in the Formula 1 factory Red Bull "in a few hours, almost in real time," the changes and improvements detected on the races and during the tests can be designed and carried out.
Russwurm argues that an average of ten technicians worked in the racing stables in 1992, who were able to introduce a maximum of three or four improvements in one season.
Red Bull’s team now employs 180 highly qualified young people, with high-performing IT systems and performing all sorts of changes to the race car.
The Siemens manager said the PLM software was able to analyze and improve all components of Red Bull's racing cars, with the exception of the engine for which Renault is solely responsible.
The materials, fuel, aerodynamics and safety elements are modified.
The changes can be transferred from the simulator’s virtual reality into reality within less than 24 hours thanks to advanced production facilities and high-tech autoclaves.
The only limit set by this crazy technical perfectionism is the $40 million that a Formula 1 team is allowed to spent on its races in a season.
Red Bull has set up its high-tech factory for the development and manufacture of its racing cars in a place 50 kilometres from London.
These are two buildings in which visits - especially by journalists - are severely confined.
All that happens there is Top Secret.
The workforce comprises 580 people, including the two pilots, world champion Vettel and Mark Webber.
But Red Bull's true Formula 1 hero is undoubtedly the latest world champion Sebastian Vetell.
Behind the enormous technological resources that the energy-drink manufacturer puts into the services of its participation on the racetracks, hide other protagonists, whose support is invaluable and whose notoriety is zero.
The plant's technical director, Steve Nevey, stresses that five years ago it could still be said that advances in R&D for racing cars contributed 50 percent to the victory and the pilots were responsible for the rest.
Today, share values have changed. The technology accounts for 85 percent of the success, while pilots' contribution is estimated at 15 percent.
Nevey acknowledges that Vettel and Webber can never be replaced by IT, because they "are unpredictable gladiators."
HP will open twenty own stores with local distributors in two years
Hewlett-Packard (HP) inaugurated his first exclusive store in Barcelona yesterday in collaboration with his partner Ront Reyes.
It is the first of a sales network consisting of twenty stores with 80 to 100 square meters each, which the Multi intends to open in Spain over the next two years.
The Company agrees this initiative with the already existing 16 sales divisions in multi-brand operations, such as PC City, Carrefour, Saturn and El Corte Inglés.
HP España's vice-president, Helena Herrero, cited as an example the converted shop on the Gran Vía in Barcelona, owned by Pont Reyes, with whom HP has been working for thirty years.
The North American Group intends to set up 200 outlets in Europe by 2010, of which 20 are branches in Spain.
The first were inaugurated last week in Bucharest (Romania) and Belgrade (Serbia). Barcelona is the third store.
Herrera did not want to betray the upcoming reopenings, but did not rule out that Madrid could be the next destination.
The vice-president, who is also head of HP Personal Systems, assured that the philosophy of opening the store is always the same and relies on a local distributor with experience and industry knowledge.
The North American group of companies advises them and adopts the exclusive shop fittings (with interactive products to touch). In Europe, all stores will look the same.
In addition, HP is responsible for the training of store staff.
Helena Herrero said it was not a franchising system and emphasized that with the exclusive point-of-sales network it intends "to optimise customer service and advising small and minor-enterprises on purchasing and after, even packaging."
The store in Barcelona has a pioneering pilot program that solves the technical problems of HP devices with warranty.
An online service establishes a connection to the technical headquarters in Maastricht (Hollandy).
“In this way, we can solve problems in ten minutes that sometimes take up to two days.”
"This system was developed in particular for small and small enterprises as well as private customers, because the large companies already have their service contracts for technical customer service," Helena Herroro explained yesterday.
Sabadell wants to 'steal a million customers' from his rival in three years
Banco Sabadell's managing board member, Jaime Guardiola, is more optimistic than other managers. He believes 2001 will be a tough year for the banks, but initially rejects a new liquidity crisis and prophesies the end of the debt war.
Nevertheless, he foresees a slump in interest rate margins that banks are trying to offset with increases in fees and prices for loans.
Sabadell expects to capture one million private customers in three years - a subject that is still too existing.
Sabadell has found the way to exploit the next three years and thus survive in a very complicated economic environment.
The bank will introduce a new three-year plan in 2011.
The goal is not only to survive without using the public aid from FROB, but also to increase the market share by one million private customers - 700 000 if you subtract the customers that the bank will lose. This is the great challenge facing this bank, because until now it has paid more attention to its business customers than its private customers.
This year it has added 250 000 private customers and 50 000 companies.
Their market share among private customers is 3.5 percent, while business customers account for more than 10 percent.
A further principle of the 2011-2013 business plan is to increase productivity, i.e. austerity and cost reduction, and it will present the challenges that the bank is confronted with it together with an annual report.
Few banks dare to set and publish targets in these difficult times, as due to the financial crisis, all the plans of the banks worldwide have been confused.
Sabadell will complete its Optima four-year policy plan this year, which was initially designed for three years, but as a result of the market position it was extended by a financial year.
The customers who will leave the savings banks are the bank's main goal.
At a breakfast organized by the APIE, Guardiola stressed 2011 would be a "difficult" year "with long reach."
The manager assumes Spain would only come "slowly" out of the crisis.
In addition, it would be "very difficult" to "create work" in two or three years - fundamental for the survival of banks.
Even if it assumes that the war for liabilities - which punishes the interest margins - will subside, the risk surcharges that banks and savings banks have to pay for their issuancies to obtain liquidity are already passed on to the price of loans as compensation for dwindling profit margins.
“The issues of liquidity and financing are of a structural nature and they will put pressure on the loans.”
"We haven't turned off the tap, but the interest rate differentials are very high," Guardiola admitted.
Banesto on Thursday already issued an order to its branch network to restrict long-term loans and increase interest rate differentials (cf. Cinco Días of 22 and 27 November).
The manager also confessed that the banks were currently pursuing the strategy of "increasing the fees," but "within a competitive framework."
Sabadell's guiding plan is based on organic growth, even if, as always, the group doesn't rule out corporative deals.
It is not for nothing that he assures that "larger companies are necessary" in Spain.
"In the coming year, everything will change," he assures.
He stresses that the need for capital is currently "increasing enormously" and does not rule out that some Spanish financial institutions will slip into red numbers.
He is of the opinion that FROB can replace the money market in terms of capital increases.
Having assured that Sabadell could also survive a temporary closure of the markets, he invalidates the argument of some bankers who are already prophesying a new liquidity crisis.
He stresses that nothing can be circulated, but in contrast to the incidents in June, one could achieve short-term financing via repo transactions and thus intercept the crisis.
CECA rules out a second wave of savings banks
CECA's director-general José Antonio Olavarrieta dismissed yesterday's possibility that there could be a wave of mergers of the savings banks and stressed the speed with which the industry would be restructured.
When the concentration process of the Spanish savings banks began, several savings banks insisted on not participating in the first phase. They reserved for the second round.
On the other hand, José Antonio Olavarrieta, director-general of the Spanish Savings Bank Association (CECA), assured that this second round would not take place.
"I believe that most of the mergers have been completed. The inclusion of Cajasol in the Banca Cívica group cannot be considered as a second round, it was rather adjustments."
"Most part is completed. It may be that we have thirteen or fourteen instead of sieve-toed, but all the important processes have already been completed. They have been controlled by Banco de España, who strongly supported all operations."
At a breakfast organized by the Association of Managing Directors and Managers of Aragon (ADEA), Olavarrieta stated that the concentration process "was carried out as soon as possible and that we must be satisfied with the timely expiration, which was determined in earlier times, not now, within the framework of the business plans with Banco de España," he stressed.
"The transition from 45 savings banks with an average of 28 000 million assets to 17 with 75 500 million each is a historically incomparable process. The number of financial institutions has been reduced by 62 percent and they are now much larger, with which we have fulfilled the Basel Agreement," he emphasized.
Olavarrieta explained that "there would be a number of unabashed opinions about the slowness of the process, which revealed unseriousness and nonsense," because "Banco de España exhorts us to conclude on time before the end of the year."
CECA's director-general José Antonio Olavarrieta assured yesterday that Spain had one of Europe's most rigorous financing systems and "maybe "maybe" all over the world, adding that those who doubted this "without deeper knowledge."
The manager noted that in the current economic stimulus, we do not have to worry about the situation of financial institutions and savings banks, which have undergone a "very significant" concentration process that would be carried out "as soon as possible."
He also recalled that the Spanish banks had passed the stress tests carried out by the ECB in July very well.
Zapatero and Germany's red line
Berlin has opteded for maintaining the euro, but at the same time set conditions for the entire European Union.
Tensions in the markets have sparked a dual response from the government.
On the one hand, José Luis Rodríguez Zapatero has presented new adaptation measures, starting with tax reductions for small and micro-enterprises, the regulation of temporary employment companies or the cancellation of €426 in support for the unemployed who do not receive unemployment benefits, to the privatisation of AENA, the management of Barajas and El Prat airports, as well as 30 percent of the state lottery.
On the other hand, with the help of former Prime Minister Felipe González, Deputy Prime Minister Elena Salgado, supports the criticism of Chancellor Angela Merkel as those responsible, who has summoned the current crisis.
How are these processes to be understood?
It is anticipated that Merkel's position is not a mistake of her public relations.
On the contrary, it is a new round in the difficult process of redefining the euro.
In doing so, Germany occupies a fundamentally new perspective for shaping our future, which conflicts with the maintenance of the traditional traditions of much of its partners.
In view of the resistance in Europe, there are three possible ways out of the crisis in Germany.
The first: a strong euro with Germany, Austria, the Netherlands and other countries, while the south would have to leave the euro area.
The second: the reintroduction of the Deutsche Mark - although this currently sounds more like science fiction - supported by much of public opinion, about 50 percent of the population.
The third: a transformation of the euro according to German proposals. This means: more macro-economic control; strict financial management; punishment of the countries that transgress the requirements; possibly the payment setting of some countries as well as the extension of the costs of potential adaptation measures to the private sector.
The German position is unequivocal and has been looming for some time, which is why there are no excuses now.
First in 2009, when both the IMF and the European Commission created incentivities for deficits and the German government withdrewed a constitutional reform to prevent this from 2016.
That was the first sign of drifting apart between what Germany considered necessary (strict tax plans and stable public finances) and the practices of its European partners, including France.
The second sign set the Central European country when Merkel painfully postponed the decision to save Greece.
At the time, this was heavily criticized, but the fact is that the political consensus in Germany, the public and the published opinion were absolutely against a rescue of Greece.
Finally, in the last days of October, Merkel comes out again with a unequivocal position. She is calling for a reform of the agreements of the European Union in order to allow the rescue of a country and at the same time oblige private lenders to bear some of the costs.
This position was interpreted as anti-European, as it promoted the Irish crisis and all the following.
However, their proposal, although somewhat sweetened, has been adopted. Over the next few months we will participate in the reform of the European Union, which is intended to enable these measures from 2013.
Germany has drawn a red line that doesn't intend to cross it.
It tells everyone that - even if unpopular measures now have to be taken - there will no longer be the old functioning of the euro from this date.
2013 will be the year of birth of a new currency area in which the infringing countries can be severely punished, forced to declare themselves insolvent and, in this case, the private sector will have to cover part of the costs.
Germany shows us that we have to take a hard growth course, with high savings and investment rates, moderation of consumption, improvement of productivity and competitiveness as well as positive foreign economic balances.
The other alternative, which rests on debt and construction with huge holes in the balance of payments, just as spearheaded by Spain in boom times, will simply not be tolerated.
The offer is clear.
And we'd do bad about not understanding it.
It's not just a matter of reducing the government deficit to three percent by 2013.
The demands are much tougher.
And the worst thing we could do under these conditions is deceiving ourselves.
I hope the current turbulences pass into more calm.
And that we will take advantage of the opportunities offered to us by the spring of 2013.
It seems that Zapatero understood this as well, and his double announcement (the promised reforms after the discussion with the big companies last Saturday and the announcement of new measures yesterday) points out that the hypothesis of creating order in-house has paved its way.
But the red line drawn by Germany is there, and it doesn't look like anything would change about that.
Among the possibilities envisaged, that we would have to leave the euro area, which is fully stuffed with little reputable countries, cannot be ruled out.
In this case, Germany could still tell us it warned us.
Sports and regulancing circumvention
Tricks like those of Alonso and Ramos in the game against the Ajax show inventiveness, but not the will to bypass a sanction.
The alleged exclusion allegedly instigated by two Real Madrid players (Xabi Alonso and Sergio Ramos) in the November 23 match against the Ajax in the Champions League led to a lot of ink being spilled about whether it was an unsportsmanlike action or not, and whether possibly both players should be sanctioned by UEFA.
As is generally known, the two players lost time a few minutes before the end of the game (their team had already clearly won) and played several fouls so that the referee showed them the second yellow card.
In this way, and since the Real Madrid would lead his group, the two players will meet the prescribed sanction in the next league game against the Auxerre and thus enter the next phase of play without cards.
Finally, UEFA's disciplinary committee punished the club's coach, José Mourinho, with two games and the players involved with varying amounts of money.
Provocating a yellow card under such or similar circumstances is not a new practice, it is more commonly encountered in the world of football.
It is so prevalent that it is part of the ethos - the unwritted but followed in this sport.
Part of the emerging debate surrounding the represented case is due to the fact that a team from the Real Madrid football club (which, in its anthem, pretended nobility and honour in its anthem and had punished its players for similar incidents) played the leading role in this, and to the dramatization by Mourinho, who added an episode more to his long history as a theatrical and provocative technician.
In any case, the debate revolves around whether this behaviour, which is not expressly prohibited by the statutes, contradicts the principles of sport, i.e. whether these actions are a rule circumvention. In fact, this procedure adheres to the statutes of sport, but seems to circumvent its meaning.
In this matter, the damage caused to the sportsmanship should be investigated.
The sportsmanship defends the physical integrity of opponents, an aspect that was not jeopardized by these actions, because it was not an aggressive game.
Another aspect of a clean game is to avoid the infringing players taking an unwarranted benefit from the opposing team, and that didn't happen either.
The paradox of the action of the players of the Real Madrid is that it does not correspond to what we usually mean by false play or ruling, because circumvention of the rule has not been carried out secretly in order to avoid a sanction, but it should be as conspicuous as possible for it to be punished by the referee.
The players waited and joyfully embraced the sanction.
Another question is what kind of advantage the violators drew from the deliberate fouls.
Here it should be emphasized that that the rival was neither brought into an unfavorable position nor benefits for the game played, because the obvious result of this action was that the own team had to continue playing with fewer players.
It's also not a useful strategy for the next game, because the club doesn't have these players then.
The advantage, obviously, is that the players will begin the next phase of the Champions League without cards that could threaten their participation in a future match.
But it's a long-term, to a certain degree uncertain advantage, as the team could also retire at some point. In addition, players get a sanction with this procedure and move into the second card cycle, in which they retire because of card accumulation with a card less than in the first cycle.
With that said, it's not so clear whether it's really an advantage.
Ultimately, such approaches are not directed against the sportsmanship, which also includes strategic decisions in order to derive the greatest possible benefit from the regulations.
In this case, the players did not want to circumvent any sanction (that would actually be a clear case of regular circumnavigation), they were more likely to want to choose when to undergo it.
They made a strategic decision without dodging or fleeing from a penalty.
Although the line between inventivity and regulatory environment is blurred, I believe that this matter is a clear case of invention.
I have the impression that the polemic has arisen as a result of two factors: because it was about two self-exclusions and because of the kind of provoked rule transmission.
The damage inflicted on the principles of sport through these actions is less ethical rather than aesthetic.
Offensive for customer catching for group Christmas dinner in Barcelona
The dictatorship of the calendar this year drops an avalanche of reservations on the days 16 and 17 December.
The celebrations paid for by employees themselves are spreading. They don't want to spend more than €20 to €30.
Like the Turrón and the Christmas lottery, there are also group dinners in the run-up to Christmas, which are supplying oxygen to Barcelona's restaurant and leisure industry. But as a result of the economic crisis, they are triggering a price offensive to capture the customer segment, which represents the large mass: almost everyone wants to spend between 20 and 30 euros for a lunch or dinner, the surveyed entrepreneurs and associations confirm.
In order for accounting to become even more complicated, the calendar (New Year's Eve falls on a Friday) has dropped all orders into the week of December 13, with the result of an avalanche of reservations of dinners on two peak days (the 16th and 17th) and almost no orders in the week before.
Since 2008, when the bad times began, many companies have ceased group dinners for household reasons.
In order not to have to do without this tradition, many workers pay it out of their own pocket.
Whatever the economic situation, lowest prices are demanded, confirms the chairman of Barcelona's restaurant committee, Gaietà Farràs.
The representative of the governing body does not yet dare to predict what the holidays will look like this month.
"People think about it 'tough times before they reserve."
"They would like to go out, but they pay close attention to prices," he insists.
The business will go well with "offer the quality at good prices with good service," he adds, reminding us that customers save in times of crisis, which is why prices are most in demand around 25 euros.
In a survey in various centrally located restaurants, it turns out that the evening of December 17 leads the providers to collapse.
Sotera's group says demand for this Friday with the intention of spending less than €30 would be booming. The group has adapted its menus with full offers of between €25 and €35 to the economic situation.
With another leading group, Andilana, which maintains 16 restaurants in the city (Hostal de la Rita, Market, La Fonda and others), the peculiarity of the calendar has led to early reservations, even from early November.
"We have made the impossible possible to maintain last year's prices with menus for every taste from 19.95 to 30 euros," he explains.
AT AN Grup, which manages El Mussol, Attic, QuQu and a total of 23 venues, you don't lose the optimism after revenues have been flourishing again since the second quarter and are looking forward to good, albeit focused on a few days, prospects for the Christmas business.
Sources of the group of companies highlight that the 18th will also be an overactive day with groups of friends pre-celebrating Christmas.
To the six group menus, a Christmas menu has been added for €30 or more as a price hit. You get the impression that customers are willing to spend 10 percent more than in the previous year and, in return, want more details, such as Cava.
In some companies you can also see green sprouts sprouting, which is why the employees are invited again. This gives the impression that the group meals are back on the upswing.
On the part of the umbrella association Fecasarm (with mergers such as the pub, restaurant and leisure association of Barcelona), the deputy chairman, Enric Gomà, trusts to see the end of the tunnel and notices that the reservations overall are quite "lively," even though the customers "slap out a maximum."
However, the fun of partying has not declined, no matter who pays.
Pakistan's nuclear programme raises great concern in the U.S.
Islamabad manufactures nuclear weapons "faster than any other country."
WikiLeak's revelations confirm Taliban-Pasian relations.
The content of correspondence between the North American embassy in Pakistan and the U.S. State Department, unveiled by WikiLeak's Internet portal, confirms the enormous concerns of the Pakistani nuclear program Washington.
On the verge of clarifying and hefty details offered by the documents about the content of the talks and the mindset of accredited North American diplomats in Islamabad, they reveal that cooperation between the two countries in terms of nuclear security is not intensified, but, on the contrary, "decreases," and that the unstable Asian country manufactures more nuclear weapons "to a faster rhythm than any other country."
In particular, the peculiarity of Pakistan's nuclear program - that the weapons aren't assembled, but their individual parts are stored to complicate their destruction in the event of a possible attack - are preparing sleepless nights.
Anne W. Patterson, the ambassador to Pakistan counts the following risks in a report prior to the visit of former National Security Council James Jones: "The proximity of some nuclear facilities to the territory attacked by the Taliban, the presumable dispersal of nuclear material and susceptibility to the transport of nuclear material."
The large number of people implied in the nuclear arsenals and missile programs is no longer regarded as sprawling and reason to exercise caution not only by the U.S., but also by Russia.
"Pakistan has to hire strictly devout personnel to guard the nuclear facilities; due to the low level of education and cultural, extremist organizations have more opportunities to reclude individuals working on nuclear or missile programs," expert Yuri Korolev from the Russian Foreign Ministry said in a meeting with his North American counterparts.
What’s new to the documentary revealed by Julian Assange’s internet portal is that U.S.-Pastian cooperation on nuclear proliferation is diminishing and the increased pace at which Pakistan manufactures nuclear weapons.
In May, from an embassy office describing communication with Pakistan as "frustrating," it declares: "Frequently there is no transparency; we don't get any responses to offers of aid or they are rejected."
In another, Peter Lavoy, state intelligence official for South Asia, confirms that "despite the economic catastrophe, Pakistan is producing nuclear weapons more quickly than any other country."
Ambassador Patterson askes Washington during a visit by the top army leader, General Ashfaq Kayani to "clarify" those responsible that "Pakistan's army must cease its tacit support of terrorist groups."
On the hunt for WikiLeak's boss
Obama appoints a high leader in charge of the anti-terrorism struggle to prevent new revelations.
Interpol issues a red warning to prosecute and detect Assange.
Everything related to WikiLeaks and its founder, Jullian Assange, forms an intricate labyrinth in which the facts are difficult to clarify.
Yesterday Interpol issued a "red warning" about "finding and prosecuting" Assange.
However, this is not a traditional arrangement for arrest.
Besides, it's not new, it's from November 20.
It also has nothing to do with the latest revelation of over four million diplomatic documents from the U.S., but with the accusations of sexual abuse that the Australian must face in Sweden.
Furthermore, WikiLeak's founder's lawyer Mark Stephens assures "the police know where and how to contact Mr. Assange."
Already on the 20th, the international police authority based in Lyon (France) issued the order at Sweden's request, but it was not announced until yesterday after the Swedish authorities were granted the authority.
That's how things lie. The 188 Interpol countries are instructed to identify and find Assange "to provisionally arrest and extradite him."
Although Interpol cannot order the arrest of an individual affected by a red warning, according to the organization, many member countries regard the red warning as a clearance of arrest - which must be carried out by the national police - especially if they have a bilateral extradition treatise, as is the case of Sweden and England, where Assange presumably resides.
The crackdown outraged Assange's lawyers, which they published a letter informing that the 39-year-old founder of WikiLeaks is willing to answer the Swedish authorities' questions via videoconference.
They are also reminiscent that Assange requested the departure from Sweden and received the appropriate authorization from the prosecutor, who is compared to the legendary Lavrentiy Pavlovich Beria, Stalin's head of security.
"That's like a hunting, not like a legal prosecution," the letter charged.
As the world seeks him - and his mother from Australia asserts her son's innocencence - Assange stays in an undisclosed place but not in silence.
On Monday, he gave an interview to the head of Time magazine via Skype from an indefinite location. During the conversation, he requested the resignation of U.S. Secretary of State Hillary Clinton.
"If it can be proven that she was responsible for ordering North American diplomats to spy on the U.N. and thereby violating U.S.-signed international agreements, it would have to resign," he said.
His opinion was condemned yesterday by White House press secretary Robert Gibbs, who described the request for resignation as "ridiculous and absurd."
"I'm not sure why we should attach importance to the opinion of a guy with a website," he explained to CNN.
Obama himself has tasked a senior leader in the fight against terrorism, Rusell Travers, with preventing new document revelations.
President Barack Obama's attempt to pay less attention to Assange's attempt is contrary to the opinions conservative politicians and commentators announced these days.
Congressman Peter King, who will preside over the National Security Committee of the House of Representatives from January, has urged the State Department to investigate the possibility of putting WikiLeaks on the list of terrorist organizations "to seize their funds and prosecute anyone who supports them or make a contribution."
Tom Flanagan, an adviser to Canadian President Stephen Harper, had gone even further, and he had to revoke his statement that Assange must be "murdered."
“Obama would have to turn him into a target and use an unmanned plane or something like that.”
Whoever didn't apologise was Mike Huckabee, baptist minister, former Arkansas governor and possible presidential candidate.
"Whoever it was who leaked information in the government, he is guilty of betrayal and all that isn't an execution is too lenient," he told Fox.
Puigcercós seeks peace in the ERC with a winkink to the Carod
The Republican leader allows the party a secret ballot on its continued existence in office.
Huguet proposes a more pluralistic government and the end of the principle of General Assembly.
Joan Puigcercós appears to have found a recipe to avoid directionless ERC for the twent for the twenth of an internal turmoil in the ranks after the loss of 11 deputies and half of the electoral votes.
The party chairman is aware that his resignation threat is not enough for former party leader Josep Lluís Carod-Rovira. But he also knows that the influence of this sector is as weakened today as her mentor himself and won't give him the same heappy-healing as other former currents.
That's why Esquerra's leader is trying to win over the new critics with a wink, which would secure his position until the next local elections.
For the beginning yesterday announced that ERC's more than 200 regional councillors could comment secretly on his remaining in office.
With ballot papers, not with a raised hand.
If the carodists wanted to give the president a ticket, they could do so without having to fear reprisals.
In fact, the boss wants to avoid both the impression of division and that of submission to his person.
In several interviews, he reiterated that he wouldn't feel vindicated with half of the votes plus one, and he added without specifying percentages, a result "in Bulgarian style" wouldn't be good either.
Carod-affiliated people are demanding from the president more than just providing his office to the party. An insufficient gesture as Puigcercós controls the supreme organ of congresses.
But since they don't have a sufficient majority to promote resignations, they will agree with the board a plan of action from now until May.
To this end, they expect to be able to participate in the preparation of the document on strategic reorientiation that the management will present to the Council for a vote on 18.
Their intention is to vouch for Puigcercós. In return, he should promise to move the Congress forward to the fall of 2011, which the party's leadership already considers to be a fact.
The ERC's party leader is of the opinion that "polyphony" is detrimental to stability and he strives once and for all to enable the organization to wash its dirty laundry behind closed doors.
In this sense, the article incumbent Conseller Josep Huguet signed on his blog is symptomatic.
In view of which Puigcercós is an ideologue, the owner of Innovació considered it a fact that the party chairman would be supported "in exchange for various goals."
He counted five, two of which are in the foreground.
Firstly, the "reorganization" of the board so that it represents at least 90 percent of the party members, i.e. the inclusion of new faces from Carod's environment.
The second could be the big news.
Huguet proposes that ERC should finally give up the principle of the General Assembly as a functioning.
In doing so, he advocates a reform of the statutes, which should make it possible to hold the congresses with elected delegates.
Puigcercós's team, which was already fighting at the last two congresses at the last two congresses - an exception in the Spanish party system - is obsessed.
In fact, at the quakingling assembly of 2008, the party leadership was taskeded with reviewing the principle of General Assembly at a congress that was supposed to deal with the reforming of the statutes.
This date is still pending, as infogle the internal resolence of recent years was not advisable to tackle this matter.
Now, without the critical sectors, the time may have come to revisit this matter and conclude it forever.
23 murdered in November, 290 in 2010
According to MILENIO's count, four fewer cases were recorded in México than in 2010.
In November, 23 people were murdered in México, according to MILENIO México's counting, that's four fewer than in the previous month.
Among those murdered are three women and the discovery of two amputated bodies in the valley of Mexico.
In the penultimate month of the year, we come to a total of 390 deaths, which are presumably related to organized crime.
Among the cases stands out in San Diego de los Padres Cuexcontitlán, a place in Toluca.
The subject had at least three bullets caused by firearms, the gnace shot was delivered to his head and feet and hands were tied with a cable.
The next day, a person's body was found in the trunk of a vehicle parked on Mixteca's street in the Santa Cruz Acatlán settlement of Naucalpan.
On Thursday the 4th, agricultural workers founded a body in the early morning with traces of torture and at least three deadly bullets in the rib cage on the Tenango-Ixtapan de la Sal highway on the section that crosses Villa Guerrero.
In addition to the body, a message presumably signed by the crimal organization La Familia Michoacana was found.
A day later, a 50-year-old man with eight shots was murdered after leaving his home to do his work as a rubbish driver.
On the seventh of November, two 30-year-old people died as a result of bullets in the head in the settlement of San Pedro Xaloxtoc in the village of Ecatepec.
In Chalcro, a man's bodise was found with the mercy shot in his forehead on a lonely path in the settlement of El Naranjo and under the body a shell casing shot down with a caliber firearm.
In Ecatepec, parts of a human body have been discovered in the sewer of Playa Golondrina.
The emergency service searched for the remaining parts of the body but did not find them.
Residents of Tepotzotlán notified of the discovery of a corpse they had discovered in the area of Peña Colorada. The hands were tied backwards and the head was connected.
The man had a wound stemming from the shot of a firearm into the neck.
In the middle of the month it was reported of the murder of two men wrapped in blankets and discovered in the trunk of a vehicle parked on the streets of Villas de la Hacienda of the municipality of Tizapán de Zaragoza. In addition to the victims, two death threats were also found.
A day later, a human head was found in a cardboard board in the settlement of Valle de los Reyes in the village of Los Reyes La Paz.
In a nearby settlement, the rest of the body was found in a black plastic sack with a message signed by El Incorregible.
On the 17th, two dead women wrapped in blankets were discovered in the Zoquiapan settlement of the municipality of Ixtapaluca and the next morning in Huixquilucan they were found who, according to the news that his murderers left beside him, was probably called Mario Ríos.
In the San Francisco Acuautla settlement of Ixtapaluca, the lifeless body of a woman was founded with bullets of firearms in his neck.
On the woman's skin, Santa Muerte tattoos as well as necklaces with corresponding illustrations were discovered.
In the last half of the month, the body of a man with at least three bullets and a message signed by a criminal group was found in the municipality of Zinacantepec.
Two days later, the bodies were discovered by two men whose heads were covered with adhesive tape and a notice in the municipality of Puente de Piedra in Santiago Tepatlaxco.
Opposite the body was a message presumably signed by a criminal organization that said: "This happens to those who don't participate."
In Ecatepec, two bodies tied to their hands and feet were found with traces of torture inside a taxis parked behind the judicial center of the San Agustín settlement.
On the last day of the month, the bodies were discovered by two men in Los Reyes La Paz and Nezahualcóyotl communities.
The first had been beheaded in the settlement of Cuchilla Ancón and constricted into black plastic bags, next to it was a message. The second had been tortured and was found in the streets of Juárez settlement in a plastic sack.
Next to him, the police found a cardboard with a message.
China upholds its greenhouse gas reduction targets
China's representative stated at COP16 that his country had made enormous efforts to reduce carbon footprints and it would maintain its supply to mine 40 to 45 percent of its greenhouse gases by 2020.
China considers its supply to reduce the emission of greenhouse gases by 40 to 45 percent by 2020, assures Su Wei, this country's representative at COP16.
In his first public appearance at the 16th United Nations World Climate Summit (COP16), Su Wei assured his country had made enormous efforts to reduce carbon footprints and transform its industry.
He said that over the past four years, his country has invested $245 million to reduce greenhouse gas emissions.
On the other hand, he stated that China would not change its goals, but would work together with the international community on this matter.
He explained that China's climate change strategy is divided into four aspects: dialogue, capacity building, building institutions and supporting pilot programmes.
Progress has been made in all aspects, including the adoption of a new energy law last year, which includes the objectives of reducing the emission of gases.
On the other hand, he emphasized that in China, the authorities of the states had cooperated with those of the central state and the municipalities to achieve energy-saving goals, effective use of energy and the reduction of emissions.
In addition, he stated that a number of multi-yeal plans have already been tackled or completed, both those funded by the Chinese government and those who received funds from other countries.
For example, he referred to the effective use of energy programme launched over the past five years, which had received investment from the United Nations (UNO), the government and from private hands.
There is also a provincial-level project on climate change, another to exchange traditional light bulbs through energy-saving bulbs, and another to mitigate and adaptation, he explained.
Martin Krol, scientific government adviser on climate climate in this country, confirmed that China's efforts were far greater than it would be generally assumed.
He pointed out that the per capita emission of greenhouse gases in China was six tons per person, compared to 100 tonnes in the United States. China is responsible for a nine-percent total contribution to pollution.
It is also making unprecedented advances in the field of energy utilization. Energy effectiveness has been increased by twenty percent in the last five years and another is expected to happen by 2020, he explained.
Legal Exclusion of Espino: César Nava
The national leader of the PAN declared that the former panist party chairman had the right to turn to the National Order Commission and to demand what the right gave him.
The national leader of the PAN, César Nava Vázquez, assured that that the expulsion of Manuel Espino Barrientos was legal and that the former party chairman of the PAN had the right to turn to the National Order Commission and demand that what the right would suit him.
In an interview that took place prior to Gabino Cué Monteagudo's governor as the state's governor, he said Espino's exclusion was a decision that had been taken at the local level.
“This is an independent decision of the Order Commission in Sonora.”
"Mr Espino has the right, in his case, to contact the Comisión de Orden del Consejo Nacional so that a decision is made by a competent body," he added.
On the other hand, he explained, it is not time to think about 2012 and that an alliance with the presidential candidate had never sat at the negotiating table.
Nava Vázquez was convinced that Acción Nacional would emerge strengthened from the internal vote next 4 and 5 December, at which his successor will be elected.
"That (an alliance in 2012) is an issue that has never been on the negotiating table, at least not until now. It was a proposal for the municipal election, it was successful and served its purpose," he elaborated.
The panistic leader explained that the PAN looks back on 71 years of dialogue, that's why "internal competition doesn't terrify us; on the contrary "we enjoy it and it strengthens us," he commented.
He said that the council would undoubtedly select the or the best chairwoman whom Acción Nacional needed for the next three years. That's why he would hand over the squadrons "to whom the council would determine."
"I'm completely unconcerned and open. I'd be proud to transfer the office of party chairman to each of the five."
He predicted that the party's upswing recorded in the last July 4 elections would be reflected in the next election, although "it is not time to think of 2012."
“Now it’s a matter of assisting Governor Gabino Cué in his task.”
In this context, he emphasized that the three governments that emerged from alliances between Oaxaca, Puebla and Sinaloa would carry the flag and observed throughout the country. That's why "a lot of hopes and longings are placed in them and I'm sure Gabino is at the height of demands."
When questioned about the PRI's criticism of President Felipe Calderón over his four-year-old government, César Nava called on supporters of the PRI to reflect on the six-year period of former president Ernesto Zedillo and Carlos Salinas de Gortari.
"If the PRI believes the governments of Zedillo and Salinas were better than those of Fox and Calderón, they should start defending, should they finally come out of the closet," he affirmed.
He is of the opinion that the owner of the federal government expressions such as those he can make in his discourse in the Auditorio Nacional, because in this country there would be no mouth prohibition and even less censorship.
Obama campaigns for law in favor of undocumented students on Twitter
"If you're in favor of passing the Dream law, call your senators and push for the passage of that law," the United States president tweeted on the popular social net.
President Barack Obama today backed nationwide mobilization in support of the Dream Act law legalizing undocumented students with a message on his personal blog on the social web Twitter.
"If you're in favor of the passage of the Dream Act law, call your senators and push for the passage of that law," Obama tweeted.
His briefing includes a cyberlink to a more extensive document that urges the public to contact their senatories, since the bill's vote is imminent.
"With your voice we're waiting, the time has come to listen to you," he insists.
The news was circulated after the 42 Republican senators threatened to blost all the vote for any law, except the expansion of tax reductions from Bush's times and government funding would be expanded.
Such a blockade, announced on a letter signed by the 42 Republican senators, could affect voting the Dream Act law and other projects such as ratifying the new START agreement with Russia against the proliferation of nuclear weapons.
Asked about the blade on Republicans of the Dream Act's law and other projects, presidential spokesman Robert Gibbs said he trusts that once the tax issue was resolved, a timetable could be set up for the rest of the projects.
"I think we still have a lot of time..."
"We all agree that the tax matter must be settled before the end of the year..."
"I believe that we can make an agreement on this at any time and after that we can plan the expiration of the rest," he said.
The chairman of the Democratic Majority in the Senate, Harry Reid, announced on Tuesday "he would request the vote at the end of the debate" so that the initiative to legalize undocumented students could be voted on in the Senate from next Thursday, although the next week would be more likely.
The version of the Dream Act or the Law of the American Dream would legalize approximately 800 000 young people who immigrated to the United States when they were not sixteen years old, have lived in this country for the past five years and are enrolled at a higher educational institution or with the Army.
But advocates of the bill need sixty votes to pass the initiative, which is why they rely on the voices of a handful of Republicans. At present, the organizations of the advocates of this project are organizing an intensive influence on the passage of the law.
Democrats, the majority of whom support the Dream Act, have 58 seats.
However, one of them, Senator Ben Nelson from Nebraska, said he would vote against it and only one Republican, Richard Lugar from Indiana, has announced his support of the law.
That's why defenders of the Dream Act need to get several Republicans on their side if other Democrats decide to turn their backs on the initiative.
The most courted Republicans include those from Maine, Olympia Snowe and Susan Collins, as well as Senator George Lemieux from Florida, who took over the mandate of Senator Mel Martínez.
Other Republicans who supported the Dream Act in the past are Orrin Hatch from Utah and Kay Bailey Hutchinson of Texas, but they said they no longer supported it on another occasion.
Another Republican who had advocated for the law, John McCain, hasn't determined his stance yet.
Although the House of Representatives has a different version, analysts agree that the Dream Act in the Senate is facing the biggest obstacles.
Put your finger in the wound with literature
The second round of 'The Lust for the Language' was a walk in which the participants campaigned for the narrative
"That with sex in the language I understand yes, but... the finger in the wound? What is expected of me?" asked writer Luisa Valenzuela, who was to open the second round of 'Los placeres de la lengua', a cycle that celebrated Spanish-language literature and, on this occasion, used the subtitle of her question (at another event was dedicated to sex in language, she explained).
The finger in the wound... "puts us into trouble," the Argentinian scripture spit sweetly towards the audience, which was composed mainly of young people who came together at the International Book Fair in Guadalajara (FIL) to attend to this round table moderated by Marisol Shultz, in which Valenzuela, Sergio Ramírez, Mayra Montero and Luis Garcí.
Luisa Valenzuela read out a text she dedicated to Gonzalo Celorio and whom Shultz afterwards characterized as a great narrative. Luisa recounts in the first person and is the protagonist who is looking to learn what a "wound" is and goes from doctor to doctor, of which she receives no answer, but hints for the guide of her narrative.
She leaves the infectologist convinced: "Patience is not my strength, maybe that's my wound."
Finally, after a frustrating approach to the traumatologist, who took her breath away in the middle of the aisle, and who explains to her that wound is not a scientific term, the psychoanalyst reveals the secret: "the wound is the really open, the symbolic is the scar."
The main character of the narrative sighers and concludes that she "only learned from the wound that it is really open and does not exist". But she is convinced that the lust for the language and its traps mix.
Valenzuela, the writer, played with both.
The Nicaraguan Sergio Ramírez also presented an adorative narrative, which he called 'Praise of the Invention', in which the finger in the wound is not mentioned but discovered.
With allusions to magical realism, he tells of a famous sorceress who touched the clouds, brought back far-traveled men, or restored the virtue lost by a girl. The narrative made a U-turn and told of America, the great novel that didn't lack its own chronicler from the beginning, and he quoted Bernal Díaz del Castillo, who criticizes the abundance of stories told by the conquerors.
“Cervantes, who invited all of us (the writers), scoffed the fake witnesses who appeared in the stories portrayed as true, but in other ways the story of America would never have been told—with imagination that even lost the sense of shame.”
However, he points out "it's the madmen and liars who best imagine things," and there reality and lies approach each other, where one no longer knows which side the narrative belongs to, where fictional literature reaches the summit.
He actually calls this the 'Tradition Cervantes'.
“We inherited from him to be what is told.”
When it was Mayra Montero’s turn, the writer commented and praised the variety of interpretations to which the FIl’s invitation to the round table ‘The finger in the wound’ had contributed.
The Cuban and Puerto Rican dedicated her contribution to the great wound to the societal violence that infests her country - as a result of the struggles over drug trafficking.
"I believe that good literature, good poetry, good songs contain a tacit, not intentionally planned engagement," she explained after highlighting that literature is communication and commitment that builds up between the author and the readers as with a "chemical reaction."
The evening was decided by the Spanish poet Luis García Montero, whose contribution also emphasized "this finger of the committed writer in the writer's wound".
He noted that in a fast-paced world the intellectual habits and the control of consciousness also amount to dogmatisms, and it is forgotten "that behind every affirmation there is a little no and behind every denial a little yes."
“In order to be in possession our thoughts and reality, we must nuance: and nuancers often mean putting their finger in the wound and harassing those who feel much more comfortable when they think in the form of caricatures, in black and white, in ready-made ideas without nuances, and also harassing the powerful, who prefers to classify the world into black and white.
Romano fights for his first title
Rubén Omar Romano looks back on three defeats in final matches, he has witnesseed ups and downs in his career, but he played decisive games three times.
In the duel between Santos Laguna and Monterrey at the final game, two different styles set by their respective coaches are also confronted, and their encounter, marked by contrasts, is reflected in the results.
Rubén Omar Romano looks back on three defeats in finals, he has experienced ups and drops in his career, but three times he has had decisive games, and so far he has held up well, which is why this opportunity is of very special importance for him.
The Argentine has played his way up with the medal to the second champion, he has fallen one step behind the winners and, amazingly, he has played consecutive finals with the same team.
The santistic strategist reached the first final game as coach at 'Torneo Apertura 2002', when del Morelia had the reins in his hand, with which he against Toluca was defeated by 4-2; later, at the 'Clausura 2003' he reached the decisive duel, which he also lost with 3-1 against Rayados.
Already with Santos Laguna on the 'Torneo Bicentenario 2010', in which they played against Los Diablos, Rubén had to take a setback again, since the game with 2-2 against Los Choriceros went out after 210 minutes of draw, but at the penalty he came forward with 3-1 and was given two opportunities to take his title, but ultimately three of his players missed the eleven steps and the game turned.
Rubén had already lost two final games with the Diablos Rojos and one with Monterrey, his great rival. That is why he does not want to be haunted again by misfortune and free himself from the evil spell to be able to celebrate a title for the first time, although he reaffirms that the most important thing for him is the game itself.
"I don't want to be a champion, I don't want to break statistics, that's for a club, for my affection that I'm surrendering, and that's why the title will come to me, but what's happening before is the most important thing," the helmsman commented when asked about the lost finals.
"The only lesson we can draw from it is that the referee is that the referee doesn't whistle the end, we can't celebrate, we're prepared at the line, the players too, because the last time we were already prepared to retire with two penalty kicks, and I don't think that's a lesson until the game isn't finished."
Knowing that this series could be decided by penalty shoot-outs doesn't concern him, because he trusts his players: "You never want to arrive at the penalty shoot-out, you want to finish in regular game time, but sometimes there's no alternative and you have to be prepared for it, in the previous tour we were prepared, but unfortunately we didn't have a bad game, sometimes because of nervousness or the fierce aspiration to keep the title behind us."
It's in Romanso's power to no longer just be a second champion and, for the first time, taste the glory the titles bring.
U.S. changes rules for obtaining a visa
Persons have to introduce themselves at the consulate before their consultation at the service offices for visa application; costs are thus shortened.
From January 10, 2011, the procedures for applying for a visa at the United States embassy and consulates will change.
In the new procedure, most applicants have to introduce themselves before their interview in the consulate at the service centres for visa application (ASC, the initial letters in English).
The staff of the ASC records the applicant's biometrical data reviewed by the consulate prior to the interview.
The new procedure has certain advantages for the aspirants, including:
The total cost of processing the visa decreases, because it is no longer necessary to pay the appointment, the processing of the visa application and the transport service with which the document is handed out separately.
The applicant makes only a single payment covering the three services.
The current price remains unchanged: $140 for $140 for a tourist visa, £150 for visas with application (including visas for temporary workers) and $390 for hands and investors.
Those who only want to extend their visa do not have to have a conversation in the consulate, they only have to introduce themselves at the nearest ASC, where they present their documents and their fingerprints are taken.
Since the biometrical data is collected in the ASC, the applicants, who need a second appointment for the interview in the consulate, remain there less time than before.
Those who apply for the visa in the consulates of Ciudad Juárez, Monterrey and Nuevo Laredo will no longer have to pay the $26 service surcharge.
Additional information about the new visa application procedure can be found in the following website: http://mexico.usembassy.gov/boletines/sp101201_Visas-FAQs.html
NASA announces the find of extraterrestrial life
The North American space agency convened a press conference to introduce a find tomorrow that will have an impact on the search for sentiences in space.
The National Aerospace Agency (NASA) of the United States announces an astrobiological discovery tomorrow, Thursday, that could confirm the theory of the existence of extraterrestrial life.
The find will be presented tomorrow at 13.00 a.m. at a press conference at NASA's headquarters in Washington by experts in astrobiolity that explores the origins, development, distribution and future of life in the universe.
Convenining the press conference, NASA assured it would present "a prudential find for the search for extraterrestrial life," sparking speculation among experts from other countries.
The discovery could prove the theory that human-like creatures could exist in hostile environments that were previously considered uninhabitable. The hypothesis has been raised that these could turn into intelligent sentient beings - such as humans - if their living conditions improved.
Seemingly, it's a microbe that's found at the bottom of Mono Lake in Yosemite National Park, California. It lives there in an environment that has been mistaken too toxic to all imaginable life forms because the lake is heavily arsenic.
In some way, the microbie uses arsenic as a survival resource. Taking into account this ability, the chances increase that there could be similar lives on other planets that don't have our protective atmosphere.
When these organisms metabolize arsenic, according to astrobiologists it is proven that "there is actually another life form, a second form of life that differs from those known to us."
The space agency announces tomorrow, on Thursday, at the press conference the actual reach of its find.
The conference will be attended by geobiologist Pamela Conrad, who studies the possibility of life on Mars, and biologist Steven Benner, one of the scientists that Titan (Saturn's largest moon) is studying the question of whether its chemical composition resembles Earth.
Gesta PRD, PT, Convergencia and Patrón form an opposition bloc
With the reform of the organ law on Congress, the two-party system will be introduced, they complain.
The 60th legislature had just begun, and an opposition bloc, led by MPs from the PRD, PT, Convergencia and Jesús Manuel Patrón Montalvo, who introduced himself as an interdependent, was already installed.
Before the start of the introductory session into the 60th legislature, Felipe Manzanarez Rodríguez of Convergencia, Ezequiel Reynoso Esparza of the PT, Javier Corvera Quevedo of the PRD and Patrón Montalvo weathered against the reform of organ law, since they would be excluded.
With this reform, they reiterated, a 'two-party system' would be introduced, because now it would be two people, the same number as parties that controlled Congress.
These four deputies, together with Gloria Margarita Santos Aguilar, coordinator of the PRD, were the only ones who voted against the presidium led by panist Salvador López Brito and Luis Antonio Cárdenas Fonseca in his capacity as deputy chairman.
Corvera Quevedo explained that with these reforms the PRI and PAN parties had institutionally introduced their political fringe position.
He stated that over the next few days they would speak with the representatives of PAN and PRI in order to change the scheme. In doing so, they proceeded from their political will and if they did not have it, they would be subjugated again by both parties, which would put an end to the opposition coalition.
“Until here I (the opposition coalition), that’s what I see, because we are excluded from all decisions that do not even take into account the opinion of the rest.”
Felipe Manzanarez Rodríguez of Convergencia explained that there were agreements with PRD, PT and Patrón and that therefore a pluralistic group would be formed.
It cannot be allowed, he continued, that Congress, having been monopolistic, is now supposed to be "dupopolistic."
"It is not the spirit of legislative reform that a perpetual two-party system is supposed to rule in Congress."
"For the beginning, this group starts with five MPs and they are talking to other colleagues so that they become more," the Convergencia member explained.
Ezequiel Reynoso Esparza of the PT alluded to PAN and PRI that the Congress had currently turned into a 'prey' for two.
“Before, it was just one party, now they’re dividing it up in pairs.”
Patrón Montalvo requested that MPs should be respected and assigned their place at the negotiating tables.
"There must not be five because of top decisions, which are likely to be nine MPs, to be left out. The five, which are currently seen as divided into different factions, have decided to unite to take our place at the negotiating table, because due to the reform that has been carried out and agreed by the others, we have stayed outside and we do not agree with this reform."
200 000 peso more for "the supplier"
Mazatlán's city administration pays another cheque to the company's controversial owner for electrical supplies.
Another cheque for a little more than 200 000 peso in favor of Lorena Guadalupe Camacho Palazuelos was issued by the city administration.
As a result, the sum paid in the second half of the company’s owner for electrical material amounts to 1 200 000 pesos.
According to the page for more transparency of the city administration, this cheque issued to an amount of 200.144.56 peso with the number 9258447 was issued last November 16 with the purpose "billing open invoices".
The treasurer Luis Miguel López Moreno had already predicted that more money would go from the municipality to this supplier. She is watched by the Auditoría Superior del Estado because she sells at excessive prices.
What has not been mentioned is the totall that is still owed to her and, as a result, the number of cheques still issued in favor of Camacho Palazuelos.
"Nothing was bought from her, it's just paid the debt, just like with the rest of the suppliers," he said in an interview.
The last cheques "reappeared" in the last quarter of the year: September 24, two from October 15, October 19, October 29 and that last of November 16.
Although the supplier has not yet been published in the Official Journal, as required by law, if a company is challenged by the ASE to avoid other administrations requesting their services, the treasurer assured that their services would not be used again.
After the Noroeste newspaper published on 25 July the examination of the cheques issued by the city administration to its suppliers, it became known that from March 2008 to 9 July 2010 during the three-year tenure of the mayor Jorge Abel López Sánchez had already paid the municipality over 10 million pesos with 42 cheques to Camacho Palazuelos.
There were also deals with Jumapam, where she collected cheques worth over 3 million peso.
Touristic places need to be improved
Tourism companies regret the accident of the foreign tourist as a result of the poor condition of the Glorieta Sánchez Taboada in Mazatlán.
After the fall of a foreign tourist in the burglary of Glorieta Sánchez Taboada, Mazatlán's tourism industry requested the authorities to pay special attention to the areas that are in poor condition and cast a bad picture on the port city.
Salvador Barraza Rubio, chairman of the merchants' association of Zona Dorada, reminded that on various occasions it was demanded to maintain the destination and to turn the poor conditions that tourists find into new employment opportunities or routes.
He said the poor condition of the streets and sidewalks had had a negative effect, as the tourists would come to stroll and see the clutter, whereupon they would turn their backs on the nearby shops.
"Politicians don't know what it means to have to close a company one day and they don't worry about how much money you're losing, because otherwise they wouldn't carry out the construction work slowly and unplanned in this way."
The hotel owner José Ramón Manguart Sánchez said the overthrow of the tourist had drawn attention to the lack of prevention and the lack of maintenance on the part of the city.
"The community has to take on its responsibilities and avoid that something like this happens again. How to do this? Well, through preventive maintenance instead of repairing everything afterwards," he explained.
"We are eagerly waiting to have a clean city. Because not only because it is a tourist area, it has to be maintaineded, we residents also have a right to a clean habitat. The city administration must change its strategy to keep the city in good condition."
"There are other places that also look unkempt and unfinished," he said, "for example el Escudo y el Venadito from Mazatlán."
"These days we need more than just advertising to attract tourists. A good product must be offered, a city that keeps its beaches and roads clean, and the tourist attractions constantly in shape," he added.
“Not enough attention has been paid to these precautions and these kinds of incidents expose what is obvious to everyone: maintenance is urgently needed.”
Raúl Llera Martínez, undersecretary of Promoción Turística, expressed the importance of saving the tourist places of Mazatlán, because they were not used properly and gave a bad picture.
He mentioned a number of places that, together with the city, would have to be brought to the fore. Making the city clean, tidy and attractive for tourism should be a priority.
“Fortunately, Mazatlán offers many historical and cultural sites that would first have to be tidy and cleaned in order to make them attractive for tourism and supply them to the inventory with which we promote this destination.
IP Demands Results From Congress
Eduardo Dávalos, chairman of the CCE, suggested that MEPs should take on the great responsibility to give a role model for a better Sinaloa facing many challenges.
Mazatlán's corporate industry calls for a dignified parliament, free from collusion between the parties and the government, because Sinaloa is agitated and faces major problems.
Yesterday the 60th parliament was inaugurated. It consists of seven political forces, 19 MPs of the PRI, 13 of PAN, three by Partido Nueva Alianza, two by PRD, one from PT, one from Convergencia and still one from PVEM. That makes a total of 40 people's representatives.
The new members of the State Congress are expected to do a clean job, to be committed to society and to the various social groups that are dependent on responsible parliamentary work, and that the same patterns of partiality and paternalism are not repeated.
Eduardo Dávalos, chairman of the CCE's Business Coordination Council, suggested that the new MEPs should take on the great responsibility to set an example for a better Sinaloa facing many challenges.
"We hope that MPs truly exercise their right to act freely from collusion between the parties, because it is customary for them to make arrangements with which they represent their own interests, transforming themselves into carnival comparses of the rulers and the leaders of the parliamentary factions."
He said they should demonstrate their commitment to building the necessary bridges to support the current government.
"We take care that they carry out a trustworthy job and, above all, stand up for society and its groups, because it was the inhabitants of Sinaloa who expressed their confidence in them in the last elections."
José Ramón Manguart Sánchez, chairman of the Tres Islas hotel association, believes that one of the major challenges faced by the 40 representatives of the people is the drive and joint work for the economic growth of the state, because nowadays the economic situation is extremely complicated.
“The most important thing is that they drive economic growth, create investment incentives and infrastructure measures, which in some way will promote job creation.”
With regard to the tourism industry, he demanded from the new Members that they should allocate the necessary importance in the state budget to the Ministry of Tourism.
"I have understood that it is the ministry with the second fewest means. This is incompatible with an important economic activity that brings foreign exchange and investment into the country and is in direct competition with the agro-industry."
Funds must be allocated to tourism projects, state security must be supported and the alcohol law, which has not been re-enacted in previous legislative periods, must be updated.
Holy King is wanted
Dolls, cars, bicycles and other toys the children of the Coronado Valenzuela family are longing.
It will be difficult for the parents to fulfill these wishes on the next Three King’s Day, because the household is composed no more and no less than twelve people and a goat.
Nevertheless, Adelaida, Isabel, Jesús, José Alfredo, María Guadalupe, Isaías, José Juan and Miguel do not give hope that Melchior, Caspar and Balthasar will give them the toys and garments they so long for.
The family lives in a modest house in the settlement Salvador Allende. They live from hand to mouth, because the father has no fixed work.
"Sometimes hard times aren't because my husband doesn't have a job. But we sell mandarins and potatoes... Already in the afternoon I'm going out into the streets with the brats to sell," Cayetana Valenzuela Andrade, the mother of the little ones.
Four-year-old Adelaida would like a doll or bicycle, while her three-year-old sister Isabel wants a Barbie doll.
Ten-year-old José Alfredo wants to have a 'monster wagon' for some time and for his eight-month-old brother Jesús Cayetana proposes clothes in size for one-year-olds.
" (José Alfredo) told me, 'I'd like this monster,' but I replied, 'let's wait, boy,' because it was very expensive," Cayetana recalled sadly.
For the older children who went to school, clothes and shoes would be ideal.
Eleven-year-old María Guadalupe has the size 12; the ten-year-old Isaías the size 10, while the eight-year-old Miguel has size 8.
14-year-old José Juan is missing tennis shoes. A pair in the size 5 1/2 or 6 would be very helpful.
If you want to help realize the dreams of the children of the Coronado Valenzuela family, you can participate in the campaign 'Holy king is wanted' by donating a toy or cash in the reception hall of the Noroeste newspaper.
14 Suspected Drug Dealers Arrested
On Wednesday, around ten house searches were carried out in the administrative region of Estrie as part of the smarking of a production and sales network of cannabis.
Fourteen people have been arrested and will be placed against various charges.
The house searches took place in Sherbrooke, Orford and Stoke, where they are said to have mainly served drug manufacturing.
"About 750 cannabis plants, four kilos of hashish, cash and equipment that served production have been confiscated," Louis-Philippe Ruel, spokesman for Quebec's security police, said.
A total of eleven men and three women were arrested, two of those of the men, who are considered the main wire pullers of the drug ring, from Sherbrooke.
Those arrested are said to have appeared in front of the justice building in Sherbrooke hours after their arrest.
They could, among other things, be charged with the production of cannabis, its possessions for sale purposes, the plot, as well as the service of crimes.
These house raids had taken place as a result of a lengthy investigative activity that took place for over a year.
"The information received from the public sector has enabled us to carry out these arrests," M. Ruel clarified.
Around 70 officers of the Regional Investigative Unit Estria of the Quebec Security Police, the Sherbrooke Police Service and the Memphrémagog Police Department had participated in this police operation.
Clandestine immigration could be tracked easily
The Conservatives' bill, which according to the government is supposed to regulate the problem of ships loaded with secret immigrants, is most likely to be stampeded after the three opposition parties announced on Wednesday that they can't endorse the proposal.
“I strongly oppose this charter.”
"I'm sticking with a party that's shackled to your feet," Michael Ignatieff, head of the Liberals, declared after the weekly caucus meeting in front of the assembled MPs.
The Liberals claimed that the bill, which allows any seagoing ship identified as for transporting illegal immigrants for up to a year, violated the Canadian legal and custodial of freedom as proposed.
Gilles Duceppe, head of the Bloc Québécois, also hinted that he could not support the bill, as he opposes the wrong target group.
"We're against it and it seems that all opposition parties are against it, so we'll wait to see what the government is doing," M. Duceppe explained.
Criticism regarding Olivia Chow's immigration issue, NDP, has dismissed the critics who demand that her party not behave steadfastly enough towards the secret immigrators.
"The neo-Democrats agree to take measures against the blind passengers, and this is exactly what we are trying to implement by mediating another bill, the C-35," Chow said.
We therefore do not support the Conservatives' bill because it seeks blame among the refugees and keeps children captive for at least a year," Chow explained.
Immigration Minister Jason Kenney accused the opposition parties of playing with politics and ignoring the will of Canadians.
“Canadians strongly inform MPs that they do not tolerate this kind of secret immigration, these criminal processes that affect Canada and that treat the country like a footer.”
"They expect Canada to take tough action," according to Minister Kenney's response.
“The government has not announced any vote of confidence regarding its bill, which could have led the country to elections that the government would have rejected.
However, a Conservativist has told QMI agency that the government would convene a vote so that every opposing MP would immediately explain himself to his constituents.
The new unit finds no unanimity
Despite the announcement of the eventual establishment of a new, stand-by unit to fight corruption and collusion by Jean Charest, numerous observers continue to call for the formation of a public committee of inquiry on the subject.
Robert Dutil, minister of public safety, claims that this idea did not come from yesterday: Quebec had already consulted the New York Department of Investigation last January on this issue.
Since 1870, the metropolis has had a standing unit against corruption.
Nevertheless, Wednesday's announcement has surprised more than just one.
It should be said that this measure was not discussed in the slightest at the meeting of the Permanent Committee of the Quebec Police Service on 29 October last year, despite Robert Dutil's presence.
There was also no shortage of Stéphane Bergeron, critic and member of Parti Québécois, qualifying this announcement in terms of public safety as a "rabbit conjured out of the hat" as well as "solution rightly crafted at the end of the table".
Minister Dutil, for his part, had in turn reached out to those who fear that political power would have too much influence over the future unity in question.
"They will see the result when we announce it," he contented to reaffirm.
“One is aware of this problem.”
According to Réjean Parent, the president of the CSQ, nothing can replace conducting a public poll.
"The more M. Charest insists on wanting to prevent the formation of this investigative committee, the more people like you and I will think he has something to hide," he explained.
"The only means of forming a committee of inquiry is the resignation of Jean Charest," said Yves Boisvert, lecturer in ethics and political systems at the ENA (state university for the training of senior administrative officers).
In order to get out of this impasse, he proposes that Quebec be given a special mandate for financial audit control so that it can investigate corruption and collusiviation.
"One should be able to extend the mandate of exam control," he decided.
He also argues that Quebec could grant audit control the same power as commissioners, which would also allow it to oblige people to testify.
The formation of a standing unit isn't the only project in the drawers of the Charest government.
The Quebec will also require Ottawa to amend the criminal law in order to better attuned to these kinds of crimes.
This demand stems from Operation Marteau's Chief Prosecutor, which has informed Justice Minister Jean-Marc Fournier that the case-by-law is inconclusive.
Vic Toews, Minister of State for Public Security confirmed that his colleague from the judiciary, Rob Nicholson, is currently dealing with certain amendments relating to this dossier.
During Operation Printemps in 2001, which concerned the groups of motorcycle police, 131 people had been arrested, although the criminal law had not been amended.
As a result, Ottawa enforced gangsterism law.
Rivers under strict supervision
Several rivers remain under severe guardance due to the high rainfall that has poured in some regions of the province since Tuesday.
A landslide occurred in Shawinigan, in the Mauricie administrative region, on Wednesday afternoon.
Two houses have been evacuated preemptively.
In the evening, another landslide occurred in Saint-Lin-Laurentides, in the Lanaudiere administrative region.
A road has collapsed into itself.
From Tuesday to Wednesday evening, heavy rainfall of 40 to 70 milliliters of rainfall has poured into several regions.
Between 20 and 40 more millimeters of precipitation were expected here according to the regions by Thursday evening.
Areas north of the river as well as the valley of the Outaouais region were most affected.
A total of up to 100 millimeters of rain showers had gone down here.
Since the snow melt, which has already accumulated in certain sub-areas is to be addeded to these precipitations, the crossing of several watercourses is feared.
On Wednesday evening, warnings were issued by the Quebec security service about the flood alarm regarding the Ouareau and Achigan rivers in the Lanaudiere region.
The rivers in the north, in Saint-Jérôme, in the Laurentine mountains and the Chêne in Lotbinière become an object of flood monitoring here and remain under observation.
In the Lanaudière region, the heavy rainfall has resulted in the crossing of some lakes in Sainte-Julienne, Sainte-Marceline-de-Kildare as well as in Rawdon.
In Saint-Calixte, an evasive bridge has been torn due to the heavy rains.
Ten residencies had to be evacuated as a preventive measure.
In Outaouais, the crossing of certain lakes has caused the closure of several roads, particularly in the municipality of Mansfield-et-Pontefract.
The security service remains on alert and closely monitors the situation, according to security councillor Annik Bouchard.
For its part, the Quebec State Flood Centre issued warnings of the sharp rise of several rivers on Wednesday, in particular Maskinongé, Assomption, Ouareau, Achigan, Batiscan and Loup.
Dupont partly rebuked
Quebec broadcaster Stéphane Dupont has suffered a partial setback in front of the Canadian Supervisory Board for Broadcasting Standards (CCNR) because of his controversial statements about social welfare and Haitians, as well as because of his faul-failing speech via radio.
The CCNR argues that M. Dupont and his CHOI 98.1 FM team have the right to their own expressions, but that certain statements contained "inaccurate information about social welfare and unjustifiably negative representations of Haitians, as well as such an unsophisticated mode of speech that is not appropriate to the broadcasts broadcast during the day."
The Supervisory Board had also concluded that a discussion of suicide involving the dissemination of the names of two persons who committed suicide violates the code of radio transmission.
The CCNR had received several complaints relating to the comments made during broadcasts in 2008 and 2010.
The first injunction was lodged as a result of a complaint by the "Front commun des personnes assistées sociales du Québec".
The activist organization had complained that the amounts mentioned in the shipment were inaccurate.
The moderators should rather give solid rather than misleading information.
With regard to the subject of suicide, presenter Stéphane Dupont had argued that suicide was an expression of cowardice.
The majority of the members of the Supervisory Board had drawn the conclusion that the message conveyed was that suicide should not be seen as a solution to life crises.
"The majority of the Supervisory Board takes into account that the consequences in question "Dupont le midi" would have raised a sensitive issue in a family-friendly and suitable manner."
Therefore, he was slapped with no rebuke to his person.
The last point of contention was the statements related to the earthquake in Haiti.
Stéphane Dupont and Jean-René Dufort had delivered a media-effective dispute that had provoked a lot of reactions.
The CHOI presenter had stated that he had not donated in favor of Haiti aid, as one would not know for sure whether the needy would actually receive the monetary donations.
The Supervisory Board considers that these comments were not of an improper character, but that their cumulative effect presents an unjustified negative representation of the Haitians.
"I've already served my three-day suspending sentence on this matter."
"My file has been virginal for a year and a half," the presenter explained.
In all resolutions, the Supervisory Board denounces the dissemination of an unsophisticated mode of expression.
In all cases, the CHOI-FM broadcaster had acteded "beyond its obligations".
The consequences for the broadcasting station, which, among other things, was supposed to communicate its decisions once during the prime period within a period of three days, are minor.
A king of Kung-Fu in Montreal
Nacereddine Zemmal, Montreal of Algeric origin, has taken a very special course.
The athlete, who excels in "Sanda" (full contact Kung-Fu), in kickboxing as well as Thai boxing, is now increasing in the search for new challenges on the Tatami and into the boxing rings of the world.
"I started martial arts like kung-fu and kickboxing 12 years ago in Algeria," explained Zemmal, who has been based in the metropolis since 2008 to develop among professionals.
“I’m personally looking for the fight everywhere, because for those who don’t like ground combat as the mixed martial arts present, competitions in North America are scarce.”
Recently, he who worked as a security officer and employed by UPS, gave his rivals bad news by winning two gold medals from the World Karate and the Kickboxing Association (WKA) at the World Amateur Championship, held between 23 and 30 October in Edinburgh, Scotland.
"I won the victory in the categories of full contact kickboxing and Thai boxing," he explained.
"Currently I'm preparing for the World Cup at San Shou in China."
“There had been those fighters from each category who had placed in the top four at the last World Cup.”
"I won bronze in Toronto in 2009."
“In 2005, I finished eighth in Vietnam.”
"In 2007 I finished the tournament in China in fifth place while I finished third in Toronto in 2009," he added.
“It’s worth noting that I’ve made progress in every competition.”
“With this rhythm I’m going to be first this time or next year!”
"San Shou" or "Sanda" are disciplines originating from China, in which punches and foot kicks as well as ground throws are permissible.
The duels usually take place on a platform, less often in a boxing ring.
The most renowned boxing fighter that has emerged from this kind of martial art is the American of Vietnamese origin Cung Le, arguably well-known in the world of martial arts.
Should Nacer Zemmal retain only one event in his career, he'd certainly call his presence at the super tournament "King of Sanda," the Kung-Fu's Super Bowl.
"This competition is the highest-ranking and most respected in China," the sympathetic pound guy explained.
"It's a bit like the equivalent to the Lumpinee stadium (the largest stadium in Thailand)."
“That was a super experience.”
Zemmal had won his first fight in the semi-finals of his category (80kg) before then losing in the final.
"The one who defeated me was a real professional," he said.
"There are four competitors per category (80 kg, 85 kg, 90 kg and 95 kg)."
“The winners of the semi-finals will each face each other in the final of their category.”
“Then the victors of each category offer their foreheads to crown a single champion, the king of kung fu.”
€450 million in non-tender contracts
Hydro-Québec has mutually agreed project planning firms €450 million in contracts for the Eastmain-1-A-Sarcelle-Rupert hydropower project.
In the middle of this avalanche of more than 100 contracts is a consortium consisting of BPR, Dessau, Groupe SM and Axor, which has received the most important share, namely that of the project management, at a sum of 295 million dollars without tender.
This consortium, nicknamed "Énergie gérance," was formed in 2004 as a side effect of Hydro-Québec's decision to the private sectory to hand over this important $5 billion hydroelectric project.
At the beginning of 2005, the "Énergie gérance" consortium obtained the lucrative treaty without a public tender.
For Parti Québécois, resorting to corporate consortia poses a serious ethical problement evoking the risk of competition as well as an increase in prices.
"This is a new way of a mapped-out game and invalidates the competition."
"This means that instead of carded games instead of in a secret way, you are now in open form," Sylvain Simard, PQ's lawmaker, explained at the press conference yesterday.
In the early morning, Simard had raised the question before the National Assembly, because it seems difficult to understand how the interest of the public can be served in this way.
Resources Minister Nathalie Normandeau had reacted in the afternoon by vehemently denying that this contract was a mapped-out game.
"I'm staying true to my line," she weatherered that the PQ had proceeded "dishonestly" and it "laccense in hardship."
She said it "was the least" the PQ apologised.
The consortium's model is common practice worldwide.
It is usually a temporary alliance that allows two or more companies to pool their expertise with regard to a bid on a tender for a complex project.
The "Eastmain" project has a high level of complexity in terms of its worought.
The construction process is underway and is expected to be set up by 2012.
Nevertheless, the contract were awarded without a competition having taken place; there had already been negotiations.
In addition, the model of the consortium in Quebec is by no means an exception, even with relatively simple projects.
As the Le Devoir newspaper noted last week, the consortium's model applied in the field of civil engineering and consulting is the subject of government investigations.
A short overflow of the Quebec business register allows you to designate more than 200 consortia, some of which appear to be of consistent duration.
In this way, for example, Dessau cooperates in partnership with the majority of its core competitors, which include CIMA+, Génivar, SNC-Lavalin or Roche and Tecsult.
Any connection, and there are several dozen of them, traces back to one and the same legal person, namely on Dessau.
As for the "Énergie gérance" consortium, Minister Normandeau has emphasized that "the Baie James territory is subject to a special labour dynamic and that Hydro-Québec has paid particular attention to this fact since the 1970s".
Réal Laporte, the great employer of the Société d'énergie de la Baie James (SEBJ), has made a statement hugging the nose about the fact that the contract for project management has been approved by agreement.
“If you make a tender and there are only a few applicants, your prizes will skyrocket.”
“So you’ve got two options: either you take the people who are on this mart and tell yourself that you’re going to negotiate a satisfying agreement at a good price.”
"However, the idea is that the price is crucial, but the expertise of the people," Laporte affirmed.
This contract, approved to the "Énergie gérance" consortium, is part of a long list of treaties for more than 50,000 dollars received by Parti Québécois.
The contracts have been approved by either Hydro-Québec or SEBJ for the Eastmain-1-A-Sarcelle-Rupert project.
After review, Le Devoir newspaper counted more than 100 engineering and consultant contracts granted to companies or their branches, including in particular resource laboratories, between 2002 and March 2010.
SNC-Lavalin had collected 22 contracts, Génivar had received 16, Tecsult 14 and Dessau 10, without including their participation in "Énergie gérance".
All of these treaties have been negotiated.
In total, they amounted to $449 783 344, including the contract with "Énergie gérance" over $295 131 564.
Sylvain Simard had announced that the four companies that form the consortium (BPR, Dessau, Groupe SM and Axor) of the "Parti libéral du Québec are well known".
Minister Normandeau had suggested that the PQ was equally connected with the companies that "financed" them, before clarifying that it was rather their leaders.
Barometer responsible consumption - The "green" label is not confidence-inspiring
Greener, but unabashedly sceptical.
The Quebec consumer has little confidence in the main players of sustainable growth, but neither has the brands or eco-labels that are supposed to steer his choice.
Pity, although it bathes in a media-effective universe, where it is teeming with information in favor of a more responsible consumption: the barometer of responsible consumption has a lack of credible information to lament, an inadequacity that identifies itself as the brake of its commitment, as the first reading that was held yesterday showed.
Although they advanced the consumption of the so-called socially acceptable products in 2010, Quebecers still seem to pay little credit to those who are trying to sell them.
Two-thirds of the Quebec population admits, in fact, to do not believe the environmental commitment of the companies as much as the green corporate advertising, so reveals the measuring instrument elaborated by the responsible consumer center of the University of Sherbrooke.
Another finding: half of consumers doubt the trustworthiness of the official certificates attached to the supposedly fair products, but also the credibility of the products and brands, which declare themselves as environmentally conscious, according to the barometer.
According to the same pattern, the discourse of politicians on this subject is taken seriously by only 14.6% of the Quebec population.
Nevertheless, the information regarding responsible consumption seems important for the stimulation of this consumption segment, which is currently revived by approximately only a third of Quebec consumers.
In fact, emptiness and dubious nature of the information dampened the zeal of 46% of consumers.
According to the higher price of the products, this is the second brake identified by the barometer.
In addition, one-third of Quebecers are distrusful with the stated performance of the green products and services they are committed.
According to another communication, more than half of consumers had already changed their habits in 2010 and mainly changed the brand of one or more products to express their conviction.
According to Barometer, they had also favored 61% purchases from merchants in their neighborhood and favored the purchase of food grown in their region.
Greener and more responsible - the index of eco-conscious consumption had reached a 64 of the maximum value of 100 this year - the Homo consumus of Quebec loves to present itself as the king of recycling, trying to reduce its traces left in the environment by taking care of animal welfare.
But the social and ecological consciousness nevertheless seems to stop in front of his car, according to the barometer: only 28% of Quebecers have indicated that they have used public transport in 2010 or preferred the ride-sharing center to demonstrate their ecological interest.
Footing as well as cycling were also favored by 39.1% of participants in this comprehensive behavioural survey, which feeds the clichés.
Government bond to acquire eXcentris
The Quebec government, requested by the Société Angus and Parallèle to participate financially in the takeover of the eXcentris cinema complex, agrees to cooperate, but only in the form of a competitive bond.
The developer company Angus and Parallèle, which had hoped for a donation of seven million dollars from the Ministry of Culture for the takeover of the Daniel Langlois-owned cinema complex eXcentris on Boulevard Saint-Laurent in order to hand it over to its actual cinematic function, apparently do not receive what they have demanded.
The government has accepted to get involved in this matter, but on its own terms.
Instead of the expected donation, the Quebec government offers a bond at less than the initially hoped-for amount, which could launch a viable solution on favorable terms, at least in the opinion of sources withdrawn from the state rumor mill.
A first action plan and a request for 12.8 million had already been rejected last spring by Quebec, the petition has been correcteed downwards since the again.
A proposal together with an action plan has already been sent to Minister St-Pierre by the president of the "Société de développement des entreprises culturelles" (SODEC), François Macerola, who directs the eXcentris file.
Several days agove, he had sent her a final report.
However, the cabinet's counter-proposal was submitted to SODEC on Wednesday.
Parallèle and Angus now have until 31 December to respond to them.
In any case, the premise of state donation seems to have been really pushed aside.
Let us recall that the Quebec-based ownership community of cinemas and cinema complexes had rebelled against a direct state investment in support of the takeover of eXcentris by making the argument that the Minister of Culture had no financial assistance to provide for real estate projects there, which he did not also submit to other cinema halls.
However, several other market participants in the segment sued, in contrast, substantial, economic assistance.
After the self-financed construction of the cinema, Daniel Langlois had presented the author's cinema in the premises of its cinema complex for more than ten years under great difficulty, which could justify the exceptional position on the part of the treasury.
The lack of cinema halls showing independent films is more than obvious in Montreal, and eXcentris knew how to keep his clientele at bay.
The Société Angus and Parallèle with Christian Yaccarini at the helm, in turn, are heading for a privately funded campaign, from which they are hoping for $700 000 to boost the acquisition.
Since the spring of 2009, only a single screen has been able to show films in eXcentris, namely Le Parallèle.
The aim of the future acquirers is to reopen the three cinema halls on February 27 next year with the screening of the very successful film by Xavier Beauvois "Des hommes et des dieux".
Christian Yaccarini, however, did not dismiss the newspaper "Le Devoir" and one ignores what he and his group think of the downward-corrected proposals that have been made.
Mystery of complex-shaken Canada revealed
The new, American diplomats circulated by WikiLeaks, reveal the mystery of what the United Nations considers under the inferiority complex of Canadians towards the Americans; but, at the same stroke, they expose the feeling of superiority of the latter towards Canada.
Consequently, American diplomats are astonished at the fact that Canadian-American relations don’t have Canadian-American relations in mind during the ballot, and they believe that President Obama’s visit would have helped the Conservative government enforce its 2009 budget!
The four new messages between the American embassy in Ottawa and Washington that were publicly circulated yesterday offer even more piquant details than the mere substantial content to nibble on.
Dated September 22, 2008, a connection, i.e. during the period in which the federal electoral campaign was in full swing, reports at which point the United Nations no longer participates in the operations discussed by its political leaders.
To the great astonishment of David Wilkins, the American ambassador deployed in Ottawa.
Mr Wilkins offers various explanations of this situation.
"An inferiority complex almost inherent to the Canadians may cause Canadian, political leaders much more likely to talk about internal issues of everyday life rather than to the United States in this election (as in the 1988 campaign on free trade)," he describes.
The United Nations, he concludes, is thus "the proverbial Goliath of this election: omnipresent, but potentially too threatening to speak of him."
The ambassador understands this "deafening silence" all the less, since the two countries have intense political and economic ties.
"Logically," he assesses, "it should be the ability of a candidate, a party, and much more of a party leader to lead such an essential relationship that the elector has in mind as a key factor at the ballot."
The other expression of this feeling of importance reads in the message sent on January 22, 2009 by Terry Breese, managing director in Ottawa, which President Barack Obama is to prepare for his first visit to Ottawa.
"Their trip will help ensure the government's continued existence in the vote of confidence over the federal budget in early February, in which Canada will announce its first deficit after more than a decade that has arisen due to a $30 to $40 billion revitalization program," Breese describes.
Nowhere had he mentioned that the government had to adjourn the parliamentary session before Christmas to prevent parliament from being overthrown by the coalition and that the opposition parties' conditional continued existence would affect the quality of the proposed revitalisation programme.
Another announcement, dated from January 2008 and authored by Ambassador Wilkins, informs Washington of the increasingly negative imagination that the dramatic series broadcast via CBC are spread by the United States.
The ambassador is disturbed about broadcasts such as Border, Little Mosque on the Prairie, Intelligence and H2O, in which one can observe little subtle allusions to Maher Arar, the "No-Fly-List" or even for the purpose of interrogations carried out by Muscles of the CIA.
Another communication reports in detail how Hillary Clinten asked her French counterpart Bernard Kouchner at her first bilateral meeting whether Europe was ready to take in certain prisoners from Guantanamo.
Mr Kouchner is said to have answered yes to the question, albeit on the basis of an individual "case-by-case" decision.
At the end of the meeting, "he handed the secretary of state a paper on Omar Khadr, a 15-year-old Muslim of Canadian origin."
“The Secretary of State has pledged to look through the file again.”
Finally, let’s note that the tempers are heating up regarding the WikiLeaks website.
Tom Flanagan, an old and Stephen Harper very close-knit adviser, had stated via CBC that those responsible for this massive leak should be "killed."
He'd been refereeing to Mike Huckabee from the United States here.
Global plan to renew lighting
The U.N. in Cancun yesterday proposed a plan to renew the lighting of households, commerce and industry in 100 countries with a view to achieve a reduction in greenhouse gas (CFCs), equivalent to nearly a third of the additional reduction that needs to be implemented to stabilise the Earth's climate by 2020.
This ambitious plan occupies its position in the steps of the concrete large-scale projects that the UN hopes to implement in Cancun in order to compensate for the absence of mandatory reduction commitments of the industrialized countries.
According to the plan put forward yesterday, the main problem would be avoided in the form of 1.8 billion tonnes (Gt) of the CFC released on the ground due to lighting if the white-annealing bulbs, which so far still represent a monopoly of 50 to 70% on the market, were replaced by fluorescent or better yet, by light-emitting diodes (LEDs).
The lighting is responsible for 8% of the world's emissions.
This corresponds to an equivalent of 70% of the exhaust gases of the worldwide fleet.
In Copenhagen last year, the major exhaust emitters, such as the United States, China, Europe and others, including Canada, were committed to voluntary reduction plans that were supposed to reduce global CFC emissions from 53 Gt to 49 Gt.
However, one would still have to deduct 5 Gt from this assessment in order to stabilise the climate increase by 2 degrees Celsius from today until 2020 in order to prevent it from spiraling out of control.
In the white-annealing pears, 95% of the energy escapes in the form of heat, and the remaining 5% produces the light.
With the compact fluorescent bulbs, the energy consumption is reduced by 75%, with the new LED light bulbs, this energy requirement is reduced again by two times.
The compact fluorescent bulbs last between 6000 and 10,000 hours on average.
The new LED light bulbs, such as those launched by the manufacturer Philips this week - by the way, this company participated in the U.N. study - have a life expectancy of 25,000 hours and consume 80% less energy than a white-annealing bulb, which conveys a hint of the savings possibilities of the replacement plan to be applied in 100 countries.
In addition to the fact that these completely novel light bulbs release very little heat, they can also work with a control resistance.
And they don't contain any mercury.
Several specialists have shown that compact fluorescent pears have reserves because they contain mercury and that they therefore have to be disposed of as special waste, which is not always possible in developing countries, unless the producers provide an acceptance point.
According to the report, however, the main source of mercury emissions on the planet is not the compact fluorescent pear, but the coal-fired power plants, which emit significantly more mercury to generate the electricity wasted by the white-annealing pears.
In the United States alone, these coal-fired power plants emit 25,000 tons of mercury annually.
The report lays out in detail the gains that each country would generate thanks to this international programme.
Indonesia, for example, would save one billion dollars a year on the purchase of electricity and eight million tons of CO2, which means an equivalent of 3.5 coal-fired power plants whose construction would cost Indonesia 2.5 billion.
Ivory Coast excitedly await the announcement of election results
U.N. Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon has calleded for the "immediately" publication of the results of Ivory Coast's presidential election right after its deadline.
"The Secretary-General underscores the need by the independent electoral commission to preserve its work and to announce the preliminary results without further postponement," a spokesposser for the U.N. chief stressed.
“He asks all parties and institutions of Ivory Coast to demonstrate their accountability and abstain from any initiative pending promulgation of preliminary results.”
Despite the pressure exerted by certain foreign governments, the Electoral Commission had let the deadline, which was set for Wednesday at midnight, three days after Sunday's election, without publicizing the results.
Approximately forty minutes before the deadline, the president of the CEI had confirmed on television that the commission would "continue with work" to announce the results.
On Wednesday, numerous appeals from all over the world became loud that the CEI should finally proclaim the name of the winner of the second round of voting under Laurent Gbagbo and his opponent Alassane Ouattara.
The Gbagbo camp, however, anticipates the annulment of "fraudulent" votes in the north by the Constitutional Council, which was achieved by the ex-rebel camp of Forces nouvelles (FN) during the coup that failed in September 2002.
Pascal Affi N'Guessan, head of Ivory Coast's presidential campaign, had confirmed that the FN's maneuvers to intimidate voters in the north of the country had deterred supporters from going to the election.
The entire election could be annulled if Gbagbo's lawsuits are taken into account by the Constitutional Council.
Gbagbo opponents, however, are now more of the view that the country's highest court is not neutral as it is led by Paul Yao N'Dré, a politically closely linked to Gbagbo.
Alassane Ouattara, man of the North, who denies any connection to the ex-rebels, has judgeded the delay in proclaiming the election results as "unacceptable" and asked Gbagbo to respect them, even if he has not gone so far as to claim victory.
His alliances reaffirmed Gbagbo was blocking the publication of the results, knowing that he had lost; and filing a complaint before the Constitutional Court, after the electoral commission was prevented from publishing the results was illegal.
Sunday's second round of presidential elections was held for the coronation of the unification and reconciliation process of a country that was divided into two parts for years.
The United Nations had warned the politicians of the Ivory Coast on Wednesday that it could be held responsible in the event of possible riots.
Laurent Gbagbo had trailed the first round of elections on October 31 with 38% of the votes against the 32% for Alassane Ouattara, who enjoyed the public support of former President Henri Konan Bédié (25% in the first round) as a front-runner.
Unemployment rate remained stable in the third quarter
The unemployment rate in Frence within the meaning of the International Labour Office (IAA) in the metropolis is at a stable 9.3% (with the inclusion of the overseas departments at 9.7%), according to the provisional, season-adjusted data published on Thursday by the Federal Statistical Official Office of Insee.
The unemployment figure in the metropolis is established at 2.631 million.
Second-quarter data amounted to 9.3% of the working-age population in the metropolis and 9.7% involving the departments overseas.
Over the year, the unemployment rate saw an increase of 0.1 percentage points.
From an in-depth, 3.4 million employees of the French metropolis were not employed in the third quarter, but wished to find a job, including whether they were operational every two weeks or not or whether they were looking for a job or not.
The situation of young people on the labour market continued to deteriorate with an increase in the unemployment rate of 24.2% in the metropolis (+ 0.9 percentage points within three months, +0.5 percentage points over the year) with the inclusion of the overseas regions.
In the third quarter, 637,000 of young people aged between 15 and 24 were unemployed, according to the IAA.
For their part, the elderly have seen a slight increase in the unemployment rate of 6.4% in the metropolis (+0.2 percentage points over three months and over the year), i.e. 482 000 of those over the age of 50, 6.6% with the involvement of the departments overseas.
Only the 25-49-year-olds had been able to benefit from the stability of unemployment in the third quarter; 8.3% in the metropolis (-0.1 percentage point over three months, +0.1 percentage points over one year) and 8.8% in France overall.
After a dispute over its calculation procedures, the Federal Statistical Office of Insee no longer publishes the monthly unemployment figures since 2006.
Eurostat, the statistical office of the European Union, on the other hand, continues in this method and on Tuesday proclaimed a quota of 9.8% in France for October (including the overseas departments) compared to 9.9% in September.
Alexandre Guérini charged and arrested
An investigative proceedings were initiated against Alexandre Guérini, 53, head of a disposal company and brother of the president of the General Council of the Bouches-du-Rhône (PS) region, and he was sent to prison in Luynes near Aix-en-Provence during an investigative proceeding on public and suspected of fraud.
Alexandre Guérini has been charged with embezzlement of company assets, embezzlement of common goods, concealment, complicity in corruption, unauthorised bribery and gun ownership, according to his lawyer.
“Alexandre Guérini ready to fight and intends to comment on all the questions that the prosecutor’s office will ask him, instead of spreading fantasies.”
"It's easy to accuse him of a number of criminal charges, another thing is to prove them," his lawyer Florence Rault told AFP (Agence France Presse) before his client's hearing.
Mr Guérini, brother of Jean-Noël Guérini, the first man of the PS (Parti socialiste) in the department, had attached great importance to sending a memoradum to his justification to the judge before his hearing by means of his lawyers.
A Guérini's relative Philippe Rapezzi was also charged and detained on Wednesday night.
According to lawyer Lucien Simon, her client had never done anything other than to keep his landumn running.
He would never have had any knowledge of the business area of competence or the destination of the associated funds, according to Simon.
Daniel Pinna, former director-general of the public service of the Aubagne metropolitation area, was also charged with embezzlement and complicity in this affair on Wednesday, however, under the condition to stay away from the protagonists of these acts under judicial supervision.
"My client was able to provide explanations of the facts he was accused of."
"The lead prosecutor has taken into account not depriving him of his freedom," his lawyer Yann Arnoux said.
Four other people had been imprisoned and released from custody on the night from Tuesday to Wednesday, including two of them under legal supervision, according to deputy chief prosecutor Michel Raffin.
Michel Karabadjakian, deputy managing director of Marseille Provence Métropole (MPM), is prosecuted for "unauthorised influence and passive bribery".
Two state officials of the Aubagne metropolitan area, Michèle Duval and Christophe Bringuier, were also arrested, one on the basis of "evasion and embezzlement of common property by a civil service commissioner," the other for "complicity in embezzlemention of public funds."
Alexandre Guérini's partner, Jeannie Peretti, was also accused of "concusting and embezzling society assets."
One of the persons confronted with Guérini is Stéphane Brunengo-Girard, recently imprisoned due to a similar affair of fraudulent machinations from the public hand that affected Corsica.
Investigators suspect his society of having helped to launder money from "high" criminal circles.
The investigation against X for alleged fraud in the award of public contracts, in this case in particular in the disposal sector, will be carried out in April 2009) as a result of anonymous letters in the files of several open investigative proceedings.
It is clear from nearby sources of investigation that the investigation focuses in particular on the conditions of the surcharge and the operation of a landfill in La Ciotat, which also covers waste from the Aubagne area, as well as from certain cities in the Marseille urban catchment area (MPM).
Affaire Clotilde Reiss: the Elysée Palace exaggerates its intermediary function for Syria
Within more than six months, from July 2009 to May 2010, the fate of Clotilde Reiss, a young French female researcher detained in Iran for "espionage," put diplomacy on alert and accentuated the deterioration of relations between Paris and Tehran.
All of this under the observant glances of the diplomats of the United States.
The American documents received by WikiLeaks and reviewed by the newspaper "Le Monde" reveal some backgrounds of this crisis.
They describe how the Elysée had explained its strategy to the Americans, who went so far as to give this advice on how to deal with their own crisis of "hostages" in Iran (a small group of hikers).
They show how the French authorities have tried to convince Washington that the liberation of the young Frenchwoman did not involve the release of Majid Kakavand, an Iranian held captivated in France and wanted by the American judiciariary for trade in material in favor of Iran's nuclear program.
Finally, they put in a glaring light the way in which the French Presidium tried to manipulate the media for the purpose of making them credible the idea that Syrian President Bachar Al-Assad played a central role in liberating Clotilde Reiss.
In truth, he had done nothing in this regard.
A diplomat's telegram edited in Paris on August 12, 2009, is titled "Iran: French commentary on how to deal with hostage-taking."
François Richier, Elysée adviser on strategy issues, chatted with the American diplomat Kathleen Allegrone accompanied by Patrice Paoli, director of the Quai d'Orsay for North Africa and the Middle East.
They explained to her the French method.
They advised the Americans to do everything possible to return their own nationals, who would be forcibly detained in Iran.
"Makes noise," they said, "articulates loudly and distinctly and unceasing" the words "fundamental rights," "innocence," "immediate release" in the media.
This is necessary to embarrass the Iranian regime, which pays very sensitive attention to its image.
“Do this all the more, especially as the Iranians ask you not to do that.”
Because "quiet silence does not solve anything," past the attempts to discuss in an inconspicuous way, the Iranians are "masters in evasive manoeuvres."
Mr Richier adds that the arrest of foreigners was a "commodal, Iranian tactic": "hostage-taking for the purpose of political blackmail."
He announces the release of Clotilde Reiss from prison (which was supposed to come about on August 16, on the day she flees to the French embassy) "required the payment of a large sum of money to the Iranians" (230,000 euros bail).
And he announces that Elysée anticipates further hostage-taking, a phenomenon that "will intensify."
"In the near future, the Iranians could arrest another of the approximately 1,800 French citizens living in Iran," he said.
August 28, 2009.
Clotilde Reiss was released from prison twelve days ago, but continues to be detained in Iran.
An information has been circulated: Iranian Majid Kakavand, whose extradition the United States is demanding due to illegal trade of sensitive electronic materials to Iran, has been released from custody following the decision of a judge in Paris.
The U.S. embassy, like numerous other observers, faces questions about this disturbing coincidence.
For Washington, which pursues the illegal Iranian networks of acquiring the material intended for the nuclear program, the release of Majid Khakavand represents bad news.
Did France accept a cow trade?
The liberation of the Iranian against that of the Frencess ?
On the occasion of another interview with Kathleen Allegrone on September 4, François Richier assures that this is not the case.
The Elysée, he says, was not only "completely surprised by the judge's decision," but had also tried to rebel against it.
"There has been a lively exchange between the French Bureau and the French Minister of Justice" (currently Michèle Alliot-Marie), but "the French government cannot change the judicial decision," he explains.
Mr Richier would also like to point out that Majid Kakavand continues to be under "strict" oversight of the French secret service.
The Elysée adviser dials a telephone number, hangs up again, then informs the American diplomat that the Iranian "is currently in the buildings run by the Iranian embassy in Paris."
During the custody of Clotilde Reiss in Iran, the Elysée does not spare its efforts to make it believe that Syrian President Bachar Al-Assad plays an "important" and "influencing mediator role".
Americans do not observe this activity without irony and state that the manipulation bears fruit, as several press articles reflect the official French version.
A telegram dated August 25, 20009 conjures up "the media flash of the French authorities."
What so much "attention of thanks" to Mr Assad is based on, the Paris embassy wonders.
“Despite their statements full of praise, the French authorities secretly admit that they only have a vague idea of what the Syrians actually did.”
The document then deals with the reasons that led the Elysée to fall into this fiction.
The intense communications efforts "aimed at confirming Sarkozy's policy of opening up to Syria," he analyzes.
A policy launched in 2008, which at the time "was perceived as premature and undeserved by numerous critical voices in France and abroad."
“Exaggerating the Syrian role, Paris is trying to show Syria what commendation can be achieved by playing a constructive role in the region.”
The aim is also to "sow discord between Syria and Iran."
The U.S. embassy knows this from a good source.
François Richier confided in her on 11 August: "Of course, we don't know if the Syrians have done whatever."
“But we thanked them in spite of everything.”
“This should at least create confusion among the Iranians.”
Clotilde Reiss returns to France on May 16, 2010.
Nicolas Sarkozy welcomes them at the Elysée.
In a communiqué, he thanks the presidents of Brazil, Senegal and Syria for their "active role in favor of the release of our compatriot."
No online games for young Koreans after midnight
The South Korean government will pass a law, called "the Cinderella," which will prohibit the youngsters from playing online games after midnight by blocking their access, government officials indicated.
A bill designed to urge online gaming operators to deny young people under the age of 16 after midnight access to these games will be submitted to parliament in December, representatives of cultural and family ministries described.
“Many children play these games all night long and then have difficulties with learning at school and also about living a normal life during the day.”
"We are of the opinion that this law is necessary to ensure their health and their right to sleep," explained Jo Rin, ministerial commissioner entrusted with this bill to the French press agency Agence France Presse.
The online games will be accessible again from 6 a.m.
The manufacturing companies would have one year to prepare for the application of the law, he clarified.
The government is also considering to ask the manufacturers to reduce the time limit granted on a daily basis for access to these games to those young people whose parents are asking, the person responsible added.
He will also provide free programs to those people who wish, which make it possible to limit the time spent on the Internet.
South Korea is one of the most "connected" countries, and the newspapers regularly report on various events associated with intensive internet use.
The country is also undisputed champion in worldwide online game competitions.
In November, South Korea won four gold medals in the World Cyber Games compared to 58 other nations.
