Euro: the ECB is forced to maintain its purchases of government securities
Last year’s Central Bank Council’s meeting was intended to provide an opportunity to identify ways to get out of the crisis, or at least announcing the arrangement of a large number of unconventional measures of monetary policy that are due to conclude at the end of the year.
The crisis obligates the European public debt, the central bank in Frankfurt can undoubtedly not afford to forfeit even today's, more than tense situation in the European bond markets, in order not to cause its aggravation.
The political cost would be far too high.
French Finance Minister Christine Lagarde welcomed the ECB’s active role in favour of resolving Europe’s national budget debt at a press conference yesterday.
She did not dare to say whether she thought the central bank could do even more, for example by boostinging its programme of government bond purchases.
"I reserve my considerations for my European trading partners (...)".
Jean-Claude Trichet, President of the ECB in Brussels on the occasion of his hearing by the Economic and Monetary Affairs Committee of the European Parliament, had not been very eloquent with regard to his plans, but was rather content to point out that the programme of purchases of government securities on the secondary market would be "continued" to "continue" and that we "will "see how the decisions turn out".
Only certainty: the ECB will leave its key interest rate unchanged at 1%, the historically lowest level since May 2009.
In practice, treasury's bond purchases were doubled by the ECB last week to about €1.35 billion.
And according to indiscretions of some traders, it is said to have made new purchases on Irish and Portuguese government securities yesterday.
In total, Eurosystem central banks have made €67 billion since May 2010.
Among traders, the anticipation of a continuation of the purchase rate is by no means disputed (see page 29).
Economists no longer expect a remarkable change of course.
If bond transactions of more than three months have already disappeared from the ECB's arsenal, the latter will still face the unrestricted cash reserves in a generous way, over a period of one week to three months.
Economists are already thinking today that this measure will expand by a few more months.
“I can’t imagine that the ECB will change its programme in an essential way.”
"Otherwise, it would take the risk of provoking new tensions in the short term," suspects Michael Schubert, economist at Commerzbank.
"A progressive phase-out planned for the course of the first quarter of 2011 seems to me more likely," anticipates Norbert Bräms, an economist at Sal.
Oppenheim.
In terms of its interventions in the public debt market, "the ECB should maintain such a volume of bonds that enliven the sluggish market," Norbert Bräms adds.
In view of the relevance of the exposed position of European banks in the markets of sovereign debt in the eurozone, the central bank has no choice but to maintain its presence in the market.
"It has to replace the lack of investors in the market at any price and act in such a way as to prevent the hasty enthusiasm about the yields paid to the weakest countries," a key bondholder testified yesterday.
Because if the interest rates start, the states would be hindered even more from reducing their debt or even stabilizing.
This is the price for protecting the euro.
Berlusconi's government takes cover in Italy
After a worrying beginning of the weest for Italy, the situation eased a little yesterday with the fall in corner interest rates to 10 years.
A return to deceptive silence.
At the end of the meeting with the Minister of Economic Affairs, the region's chairman Giulio Tremonti had qualified the situation as "indeciprablable" and heard him expect an "unpredictable" weekend, which the person in question immediately denied.
On Tuesday, however, scissors with German interest rates would have reached its record of 210 basis points, and the prices of the treaties to cover bankruptcy had risen to 263 points, which had caused a certain headlessness in Rome.
Silvio Berlusconi had pointed out to his Council of Ministers that Spain's discrepancy at Germany's interest rates is 400 basis points.
However, his right-hand, Gianni Letta, had expressed himself as "severely concerned" by the "market turmoil" and a possible risk of contagion from Ireland.
"The good news is that Italy, the eurozone's third economy, isn't like Greece or Ireland."
"The bad news is that Italy is like Portugal," explains Irwin Stelzer, an economist at the Hudson Institute.
In plain English, this means that its problem is the absence of economic growth and the accompanying, dwindling tax inputs, which put the state at risk of no longer being able to meet its financing needs.
The recent Brussels forecasts were a cold shower for the government.
In the future, Italian growth of 1.1% is expecteded in 2011 and 1.4% in 2012... although the Senate's budget is expected to be 1.3% and 2%, respectively.
Under these conditions, the European Commission calculates, the budget deficit will still account for 4.3% of GDP next year (contrary to the 3.9% predicted by governments) and 3.5% in 2012 (instead of 2.7%).
Hence the uproar around a new budget collective, which could be used after the holidays at a sum of 7 billion euros.
"If necessary, new austerity measures must be taken," says auditor Olli Rehn.
At the moment, Italy receives the support of international institutions.
"The foundations of Italy's economy are in order and its accounts are regulated, we have been repeating this for a long time," assures Arrigo Sadun, Italian trustee of the International Monetary Fund, which is entrusted with Italy at the IMF.
"At the moment, there are no problems, neither in the short-term nor in the long-term severe, despite its hefty debt (119.2% of GDP in 2011, Lower stabilization progress is required by Italy than by other countries," observes Pier Carlo Padoan, chief economist at the OECD.
The question remains of political inconsistency.
On December 14, Silvio Berlusconi will face a motion of no confidence by the Chamber of Deputies, as a result of which the early elections could be organised as early as the end of March.
There is, therefore, a less favourable period imminent for a decision on a serious crisis.
