How much would that bring?
It's a mystery.
This amount is not quantified in the text.
Of the $4 billion you're a long way off.
"It is stated that the tax resources are added to the €4 billion of the €4 billion allocated by the state of the state's funding, which will be paid by the Société du Grand Paris.
With the SGP, you don't find this system offensive.
"Since the moment you record revenue (the two taxes, i.e.), you can cover project planning for over three years and then only draw from the €4 billion," he said.
“This is recorded in the budget and gradually withdrawn.”
The SGP estimates that 2013 will start listing the €4 billion for the 2014 budget.
This measure, whose 4 promised billions will still be able to search for a long time, runs the risk of evoking a quite moving, parliamentary discussion.
The first public meeting will take place on December 7.
Vengeful hackers and bustling spies
WikiLeaks has a twofold meaning.
On one side, it has turned out to be an obvious scandal, a scandal that is merely a scandal in the sense hypocrisy that governs the relations between the states, the citizens and the press.
On the other hand, it announces profound changes at an international level, and gives a taste of the future dominated by regression.
But let's proceed in turn.
The first aspect of WikiLeaks is confirmation of the fact that each file (of any nation) created by an intelligence agency is composed exclusively of newspaper clippings.
Berlusconi's "extraordinary," American revelations about Berlusconi's sexual habits throw nothing more than what you got to read for months anyway in any journal (except for those whose owner is Berlusconi), and Gaddafi's frighteningly caricatured profile has been offering cabaret artists fabric for their skits for a long time.
The rule according to which the secret dossiers are only allowed to consist of already known news is essential to the dynamics of the secret services, and not only in this century.
When you go to a library dedicated to esotherical publications, you will realize that each work (above the Grail, the Mystery of Rennes-le-Chateau, the Templars or the Rosicrucians) repeats exactly what has already been written in previous works.
This is not only because the author of occult texts does not want to publish any research (nor because he does not know where news about the non-existing could be found), but because those who devote themselves to occultism believe in nothing other than what they already know and confirms what they have already learned.
This is also Dan Brown's mechanism of success.
Same as the secret files.
The informant is lazy, and lazy (or limited mind) is also the head of the secret service (otherwise he could be whatever, for example editor at Libération), who considers only true what he acknowledges.
The top-secret information about Berlusconi that the American embassy in Rome had sent to the State Department was the same as those Newsweek had already published the week before.
So why was such a fuss made about the revelations of these dossiers?
On one side, these state what each educated person already knows, namely that the embassies - at least since the end of the Second World War, and ever since the heads of government can also call each other or take a plane to meet for dinner - have lost their diplomatic function and, with the exception of a few small representational tasks, turned into spy centers.
Any viewer of investigative films knows this very well, and it does not happen out of hypocrisy that one pretends to ignore this.
Nevertheless, the fact of repeating it publicly violates the task of hypocrisy and serves to put American diplomacy in a bad light.
In second place: The idea that an arbitrary hacker can tap into the most secret secrets of the world's most powerful country carries the significant blow to the prestige of the State Department.
In addition, the scandal brings less the sacrifices, but rather the "executioners" in distress.
But let's dedicate ourselves to the profound nature of what happened.
Formerly, in Orwell's time, you could bundle all power like a Big Brother who controlled every gesture of his subjects.
Orwell's prophecy could have since turned out to be perfectly correct, where you can control every movement thanks to the phone, every transaction made, the hotel visited, the chosen highway and so on; the citizen would become the total victim of the eye of power.
However, if one considers what is going on today, that even the secret crypts of power can no longer escape the control of a hacker, the course of control ceases to be unilaterally directed and becomes circle-shaped.
Power controls every citizen, but every citizen, or at least the hacker - chosen as avengers of the citizen - can learn the secrets of power.
But how can a power that is no longer able to keep its own secrets?
It is true, Georg Simmel already said that a real secret is an empty secret (and an empty secret can never be revealed); it is also true that any knowledge of the character of Berlusconi or Merkel is fundamentally a secret free of secrets, as it exposes the public sphere; but in the style of WikiLeaks to reveal that Hillary Clinton's secrets were empty mysteries, means depriving them of any power.
WikiLeaks did neither Sarkozy nor Merkel's wrong, but Clinton and Obama.
What will be the consequences of this insult inflicted upon such tremendous power?
It is obvious that in the future states will no longer provide any reserved information online - this would amount to a notice glued on the street corner.
But it's equally obvious that with the current technologies it's in vain to be able to hope to keep confidential reports by phone secret.
Nothing is easier than figuring out if and when a head of state moved on a plane and contacted one of his colleagues.
In the future, how could private relationships be maintained and only for certain ears?
I know very well that my prediction at the moment sounds like science-fiction and romantic, but I find myself compellered to introduce myself to the government agents, in a secret wan't move on uncontrollablible marsh routes in a secret wan't waying their communications anywhere other than in mere memory, hiding the little written information they've received in shoe heels.
The information would be stored in locked drawers in single copies: basically Watergate's espionage attempt had less success than WikiLeaks.
I've had the opportunity to write that technology is progressing like a cancer these days, namely backwards.
A century after the wireless telegrapher revolutionized communication, the Internet reconstructs a telegraph with (telephonic) wire.
The (analogous) video cassettes had enabled the film researchers to scout out a film volume after tape by swiping from the back to the front and unraveling all the secrets of assembling, while the (numerical) CDs nowadays allow nothing more than to jump from chapter to chapter, i.e. into macro portions.
The high-speed trains run from Rome to Milan within three hours, while this takes three and a half hours by plane, including changing trains.
Therefore, it is not unusual for politics and communication technology to come back to the car and horse.
One last observation.
In earlier times, the press attempted to understand what secrets were forginged between the messages.
At present, it is the messages that demand confidential information from the press.
Climate: how Frence's financial promises
One year after the Copenhagen Treaty, it is time to investigate compliance with the quickly resolved financing promises (also called "fast start" or premature financing) realized by developing countries.
And already the rough French fine-tunings are uncovered.
With the Copenhagen Treaty, developing countries have pledged to provide $30 billion between 2010 and 2012.
The EU has pledged €7.2 billion over three years, France €1.26 billion (i.e. €420 million a year).
On the occasion of the opening of COP16, the EU has drawn up all the registers to convince its partners of the fact of their commitment to fast-start financing: detailed action report from their Member States, press conferences, major side events, brochure distribution.
Operation Transparency is remarkable and highly valued for its actual value: developing countries and civil society would like to see all developed countries doing the same at the same level of precision.
The EU announces that €2.2 billion would be released in 2010, putting it on the right path to resperving its commitment of €7.2 billion in 2012.
However, if you look into detail, things are a little more complicated.
According to the Copenhagen Treaty, this money should be "new and additional" in order to ensure that an additional deployment has been brought in without being to the detriment of official development assistance.
Unfortunately, there is no consensus on what "new and extra" means.
While the European Commission had proposed harmonisation, certain member states, including France, have blocked the procedure.
Today, everyone chooses the indicative that they deem best.
For example, the EU Commission has chosen itself as a benchmark for that of 2009: international financing will be retained, and will add $150 million over three years as part over three-year financing.
Other countries have chosen similar benchmarks, either with regard to year or in terms of conditions at the level of official development assistance.
In return, France was of the opinion that the financing "of all new projects" of 2010 was "new and additional".
In other words, there are no indicatives for the previous year's expenditure or at the global level of official development assistance.
Specifically, the total of French support for developing countries can remain stable (or even decrease) - France pretends to comply with its commitment regarding "new and additional" financing.
Another, crucial element is the breakdown into donations and loans.
There's no rule about that yet, and everyone chooses their computation mode.
Some, such as France, are €1 borrowed € as equivalent with €1 donated.
Clearly, this is hardly justifiable: the effort is not comparable.
If the use of loans can be justified for certain profitable investments (often projects tied to emission containment based on the most effective technology), it is deceptive to present the euro, which one collects as the equivalent for a donated euro... In addition, only the costs of the loan for the investor country should be counteded as an exposure (i.e. the equivalent of an interest rate).
But France has nothing but the following consideration: it "respects" its commitment of €420 million by borrowing €31.5% (€389.8 million) and donating €8.5% (€36.1 million) - see also the EU document to convince itself.
France is the only country in the EU that uses loans to a large extent.
Germany is demanding 46%, while Denmark, the Netherlands or Slovakia (among other things) carry out their commitments 100% with donations.
In other words, if the vast majority of the European Union spends €1 to fulfill its obligations, France gives only 10 cents and means a comparable stake.
Nice hocuspocus!
All in all, France operates in its fast-start financing with a double: in part with a further development of the action over time and partly with the reality of financial commitment.
France succeeds in believing that it has spent 420 million "new and additional" millions in accordance with its promises, saving 36.1 million euros in the process.
The manipulation of the Ministry of Finance (since it seems that it was Bercy who carried out the arbitrage) could have a doubly negative effect; once within the EU (why do the other member states continue with the donation, if it is enough to borrow?) and then in the negotiations, since the developing countries are not completely deceived...
The chapter of premature financing only a small question for a relatively short time compared to the totality of discussions about the climate.
But it could be the drop too much in the UNFCCC's immense machinery.
Let's hope this isn't going to be the case.
However, it is essential to learn his lesson from this first year of premature financing.
First and foremost, France could announce a change in its mode of accounting for the years 2011 and 2012, in a spirit of respect for its commitments and compensating justice with the other countries of the European Union.
Above all, however, France could bring forward the discussions on the subject of long-term financing (another, crucial and currently discussed topic): one cannot escape the question of "calculatability" or the type of posting of its loans.
France could change its position in order to be just as progressive about the issues of contention as it is with other matters.
So the hope rests on the new Environment Minister Kosciusko-Morizet, who will arrive in Cancun next week.
A surprise announcement about France's fast-launch and its vision of "predictability," as well as the use of loans, would inherently raise the international basis of trust necessary for further discussions.
Alarm - pesticides taken by children
A child over the age of 10 occupies 81 different chemical substances over the day, according to a study published this Wednesday by the Générations Futures (former movement for the protection of the rights and respect of future generations).
The association, which declares that its results "given the small number of samples analyzed do not reflect a statistically significant value, but represent illustration character of the treated problems", had between July and September the food usually consumed within four food consumed by an approximately ten-year-old child for meals, plus a snack in the supermarkets in Oise and Paris.
These typical meals - including the official recommendations of five fresh fruit and fruit parts, three dairy products and 1.5 liters of water per day - have been studied, as well as indulgencies from laboratories in search of chemical substances, pesticides, heavy metals and other pollutants.
The plate balance is devastating: 81 chemical substances, including 42 classified as possibly or probably carcinogenic and five classified as safe-carciogenic, as well as 37 substances with endocrinin-disturbance, according to studies of several institutions such as the International Cancer Research Center.
"Even if, in the approximate totality of cases, the legal limits for each chemical, individually taken substance are not exceeded, it is very good that the reality, in which the consumer may be exposed to carcinogenic substances or substances with endocrinin disturbing factors, is worrying," the association observes.
Already at breakfast, butter and tea with milk alone contain more than ten of the possibly carcinogenic residues and three are safely proven to be carcinogenic, as well as more than twenty residues that can affect the hormonal system.
The apple imported from Brazil, intended as a snack for the study, shows traces of six chemical substances, including a fungicide banned in France.
The same applies to the green beans from Kenya, included in lunch, in which the study found traces of an insecticide strictly banned in the EU.
In addition, the chopped steak, the tuna from the box, even the baguette and chewing gum of pesticides and other chemical substances were peppered.
In tap water, the analyses have brought the presence of nitrates and chloroform to light.
At dinner, salmon steak was the "richest" with 34 determined chemical residues, and even the plastic plate used to warm up the meal in the microwave was not unencumbered.
The final risk of this "pollutant cocktail" to the consumer "is probably undervalued," according to the study.
The "Alliance Europe Ecologie-Les Verts" (EE-LV), for its part, has proposed "giving the urgency of the situation" to introduce a moratorium on the use of pesticides to stop the "increase in diseases of civilization."
In July, a study by the European Food Safety Authority (EFSA) had identified reference to the presence of 365 different pesticides identified in fruit and vegetables that are consumed in Europe, including 76 in cereals.
